\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: DJIA Posts Fifth-Straight Gain After Fed Statement\b0\par\par\uc2 2,098 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 00:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China markets and Taiwan markets are closed Wednesday for the Mid-Autumn Festival, while South Korean markets are closed for the Chusok Holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10761.03   +7.41  +0.07% \par Nasdaq     2349.35   -6.48  -0.28% \par S&P 500    1139.78   -2.93  -0.26% \par FTSE 100   5576.19  -26.35  -0.47% \par Xetra DAX  6275.98  -18.60  -0.30% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 28/32  +2/32 0.432%  -3.2BP \par 5Y Tsy  99 22/32 +14/32 1.317%  -9.6BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 8/32 +30/32 2.596% -10.8BP \par  \par USD/JPY       85.02-03     -0.81% \par Range         85.77-84.99 \par EUR/USD       1.3264-67    +1.55% \par Range         1.3281-1.3059 \par AUD/USD       0.9550-55    +0.86% \par Range         0.9564-0.9443 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.40-10.70% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    11.05-11.45% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    12.80-13.10% \par  \par Comex Gold (electronic trade) $1,287.70 +$6.90 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $73.52   -$1.34 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Blue-chip stocks finished slightly higher for a fifth-straight session Tuesday, led by Caterpillar, \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  and Intel, after the Federal Reserve hinted it could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 7.41 points, or 0.07%, to 10761.03, closing at its highest level since May. The blue-chip index has risen in 13 of the last 15 sessions and is up 7.45% in September.\par \par \uc2 Caterpillar powered the Dow's gains, rising $1.64, or 2.2%, to $76.39. \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  gained 53 cents, or 1.4%, to $39.92 and Intel rose 21 cents, or 1.1%, to 19.14.\par \par \uc2 The Fed concluded its one-day policy meeting without announcing additional steps to boost the recovery. But \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials signaled they are uncomfortable with the recent low levels of inflation and said they're prepared to take additional action to boost the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "It looks like the Fed is keeping its foot on the economic gas pedal but hasn't yet pushed the turbo button," said Burt White, chief investment officer at LPL Financial. "They definitely showed a lot of restraint and frankly confidence that the economy is sort of plowing through a soft spot and not headed toward a double dip."\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite snapped a nine-day winning streak and fell 6.48 points, or 0.3%, to 2349.35. The index is up 11% in September.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-share index fell 2.93 points, or 0.3%, to 1139.78, its first drop in three days and biggest decline in two weeks. But the broad index is still up 8.6% in September.\par \par \uc2 Financials were the S&P 500's biggest-declining sector Tuesday, falling 1.03%. \uc2 Legg Mason\uc2  fell 90 cents, or 2.9%, to $29.98 and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  declined 78 cents, or 2.9%, to $26.06.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks ended marginally lower Tuesday as investors stayed cautious before the upcoming U.S. Federal Reserve policy board meeting, while a slightly stronger yen triggered selling in exporters.\par \par \uc2 "It's not like investors have high expectations that the Fed will ease its monetary policy, but if it does, they can't afford to be on the wrong side of a decision, so they are being careful," said Hikaru Sato, senior technical analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 Sato said that the Nikkei Stock Average will likely trade between 9500 and 9800 this week. The market will be closed Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures settled lower in U.S. trading Tuesday, pulled down by concerns about the sluggish economic recovery and its potential to hurt oil demand.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures set a new record high in electronic activity after the settlement Tuesday on the view that the Federal Reserve would initiate another round of government asset purchases to stimulate the economy, boosting gold's value as an alternative asset. The contract had settled lower Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed lower Tuesday as cautious investors await the outcome of a meeting of the Federal Reserve's rate-setting committee.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar stumbled broadly Tuesday after the Federal Reserve Tuesday said it stood ready to kick-start a slowing U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose sharply, racking up gains of more than 1.5% against the dollar, while the greenback wilted against the yen, briefly dipping below Y85, considered by some analysts to be Japan's threshold for yen strength after last week's massive market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What we see is the door being kept open to further quantitative easing," Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNY Mellon in New York, said of the Fed's possible fresh round of economy-stimulating asset purchases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Quantitative easing is broadly viewed to be corrosive to a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value, and so with the increased probability of easing measures, the knee-jerk reaction in the market is to sell the dollar," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed statement also seemed to confirm--at least for now--that a double-dip recession is "off the table," Woolfolk said, allowing investors to leave behind the worst-case scenario in favor of taking risks on higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , such as the Australian dollar, which soared to a two-year high against the greenback at $0.9566 from $0.9473 late Monday, reaching its peak level since July 29, 2008, according to CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday in New York, the euro was at $1.3247 from $1.3061 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y85.12 from Y85.75, while the euro was at Y112.75 from Y112.02. The U.K. pound was at $1.5627 from $1.5552. The dollar was at CHF0.9976 from CHF1.0052.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 80.442 from 81.324.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the end of their one-day policy meeting, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials said they expect the economy's recovery from a deep recession to be modest in the near term. In a statement, officials said they are "prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed to support the economic recovery and to return inflation, over time, to levels consistent with its mandate."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additional quantitative easing would play into the Fed's Tuesday statement, which Woolfolk said went "from a neutral view of inflation to, it looks like, an increasing risk of deflationary conditions."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though steps to boost growth were deferred amid intense debate about what to do next, officials said they are uncomfortable with the recent low levels of inflation, indicating more bond purchases to stimulate growth could soon take place.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "One could argue that the Fed is now pursuing a weak-dollar policy," to fix the U.S. economy, said Jessica Hoversen, fixed income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the wake of the Fed statement, which stood pat on ultralow interest rates, the dollar fell sharply against its higher-yielding peers, dropping more than 1.7% against the Swedish krona and sliding steeply against emerging-market rivals, with the greenback losing more than 1.2% against the Korean won.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The main focus in the session ahead will be whether the BOJ/MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, but it's likely they won't move unless the USD/JPY cross moves sharply lower and well below Y85.00 region, CBA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Joseph Capurso said. "I suspect they won't unless it starts falling very sharply."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys rallied hard Tuesday as expectations grew that the Federal Reserve will eventually launch another large-scale bond buying program to help the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The two-year yield hit a record low of 0.415% after the Fed's latest statement on the economy, which was downbeat and suggested that the Fed is especially concerned about the threat of deflation. The 10-year yield fell as low as 2.578%, a level it hit in early September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The statement fanned hopes that the Fed will have to lend more support to the economy, and pushed the Treasury market to gain for a third consecutive day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains were the most pronounced in longer-term Treasurys, the most sensitive to inflation. That's because the Fed's emphasis on the decline in inflation suggested that policy makers are especially worried about deflation, which means a lower threshold to do more bond buying or so-called quantitative easing, to support the economy. Moreover, investors have speculated that if the Fed does buy more bonds, it would target long-term Treasurys, because buying in that area would have the biggest impact on mortgage rates, pushing them down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is the first time the Fed spent so much energy in the statement talking about a decline in inflation," said Gary Pollack, who helps oversee $12 billion as head of fixed-income trading in New York at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank AG\f28 \uc2 \f2 's private wealth management unit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the risk of deflation increasing, "it is obviously boosting the value of Treasurys, and people are buying," he said. He believes the 10-year yield could fall to the range of 2.25% to 2.5% in a month or so.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 James Combias, head of U.S. Treasury trading at Mizuho Securities USA Inc., said the 10-year yield could even eventually fall below its record low yield of 2.04%, to 2%, though a drop below that would be unlikely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tuesday afternoon trading in New York, the two-year was up 2/32 in price to yield 0.432%, down from 0.448% before the decision. The 10-year was up 30/32 in price to 2.596% compared with 2.661% before the Fed's announcement, and the 30-year was 1 12/32 higher to 3.794%, down from 3.831% before.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended higher Tuesday, following gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight due to renewed hopes for additional monetary easing measures by the Federal Reserve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Akito Fukunaga, chief rates strategist at RBS Securities, expects JGBs to maintain a solid bias at least during this holiday-shortened week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, some analysts think any topside for JGBs will likely be capped by lingering concerns over the government's worsening fiscal health. [ 21-09-10 2205GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The long-term JGB yield may lack clear direction, as it is expected to nervously move up and down due to mixed cues, such as oversupply worries over the government's supplementary budget for this fiscal year and speculation over additional easing by the BOJ," said Jun Ishii, chief strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia August CPI data, due Wednesday, is likely to show a 2.0% rise on the year compared with a 1.9% rise in July, according to the median forecast in a Dow Jones poll of 11 economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Fed Hints At New Steps To Support Economy \par US Housing Starts Surge 10.5% In August \par Summers To Leave After Elections \par Geithner Says Consumer Agency Focus On Disclosures \par Prudential Financial Near Deal To Buy Two AIG Japan Life Insurers \par AIA Gets HK Listing OK For IPO \par Soaring Prices On Lack Of Inflation \par Diller Says AOL, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  Deal Is A Good Sign \par Hamp Sees Slow Growth - BofA \par =ADB's Kuroda: Yen Was Overvalued Ahead Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  2Q  Balance of Payments \par 2245  New Zealand  2Q  International Investment Position \par 0030  Australia    Jul \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2 -Melbourne Institute Indexes of Economic \par                    Activity \par 0430  Japan        Jul All Industry Index \par 0500  Japan        Aug Supermarket sales \par 0830  UK           Sep Bank of England MPC meeting minutes \par 0830  UK           Sep Agents' Summary of Business Conditions \par 0900  Malaysia     Sep International Reserves, middle of month \par                    Reserves, (US dollars) \par 0900  Malaysia     Aug CPI \par 0900  EU           Jul Industrial new orders \par 1000  EU           European Parliament votes on a new EU financial supervisory framework \par 1100  US           Sep 17 MBA Mortgage Applications Survey \par 1400  EU           Sep FCCI Flash Consumer Confidence Indicator \par 1430  US           Sep 17  EIA Petroleum Status Report \par 1800  US           Tsy Secy Geithner testifies before House Financial Services \par                    Committee on the State of the International Financial \par                