\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on the defensive near 5-wk low as FOMC looms\b0\par\par\uc2 896 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 06:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Market thinks Fed will not begin QE just yet but cautious\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fear keeps yen buying in check\par \par \uc2 * Australian dollar below 2-year peak after RBA minutes\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Tuesday towards a five-week low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with traders cautious ahead of a Federal Reserve policy meeting that may discuss the need for further easing.\par \par \uc2 It struggled at 85.50 yen, supported by fear of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  below that level but unable to rally, while the Australian dollar remained up near a two-year peak.\par \par \uc2 Few traders expect the Fed to apply another dose of quantitative easing (QE) just yet -- and the dollar could advance short-term if that view proves correct.\par \par \uc2 But some said the greenback was unlikely to gain much respite as the policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) would probably signal its readiness to take QE steps if needed, keeping expectations of more dollar-printing intact.\par \par \uc2 "The pressure will still remain (on the dollar). The FOMC is caught between a rock and a hard place. They would like to have an outlook on the economy that is a bit rosier than it actually is," said Robert Reilly, head of trading, flow, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Asia at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "But in reality the way the market's pricing and looking at it, the market is looking for the FOMC to come out and indicate that further measures in terms of quantitative easing will maybe remain and this will be negative for the dollar."\par \par \uc2 The dollar index fell 0.2 percent to 81.14, towards a five-week low of 80.865 hit last week.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not sure the market's going to get too much resolution after the FOMC today. Even if they don't signal any imminent QE, the market's speculation is more focused on the next few months," said Sue Trinh, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBC in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 0.2 percent against the yen to 85.55 yen, keeping a tight range after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market last week for the first time in six years.\par \par \uc2 It has failed to get above its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  high of 85.94 yen set last Friday, capped by Japanese exporter selling ahead of half-year book-closing on Sept. 30, and more sales are expected towards the 86 yen level before then.\par \par \uc2 Its 55-day moving average, now at 85.87 yen, has become a resistance level since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Wednesday, and further resistance lies at 86.26, the bottom of its daily Ichimoku cloud.\par \par \uc2 Rangebound yen trading since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped bring down dollar/yen options' implied volatilities. Its one-week volatility dropped to around 10.65/12.05 percent after shooting up close to 15 percent right after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In short-dated options for up to two weeks, demand for dollar calls is stronger than dollar puts, meaning dollar calls are trading at a premium to yen calls. Dollar puts are normally more expensive due to Japanese exporter hedging demand.\par \par \uc2 Option traders said dollar calls with strike prices above 90 have been traded as some market players sought protection against a possible jump in the dollar in the event of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some market players don't rule out another push by Japanese authorities to shunt the greenback over 86 yen. Many doubt they would let the dollar fall below 85.00.\par \par \uc2 Still while short-term short dollar positions may have been cleared out by last week's action, longer term players are said to remain short.\par \par \uc2 "History shows \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been successful over the medium term. This year strength in the yen has been more of a dollar weakness story and we think that's going to continue, against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as the yen," SocGen's Reilly said.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of longer term players have profited very well from being long the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and will continue to be positioned that way and short the dollar."\par \par \uc2 The commodity-linked Aussie held near a two-year high of $0.9495 hit on Monday after the head of its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggested Australian interest rates would rise further.\par \par \uc2 It rallied again briefly after minutes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September meeting showed policy makers think interest rates are likely to rise.\par \par \uc2 But talk of a large option barrier around $0.9500 with expiry at the end of the month helped cap its progress higher.\par \par \uc2 The euro edged up 0.2 percent to $1.3090, less than a cent below a five-week high at $1.3160 hit on Friday, despite renewed worries over some euro zone countries debt.\par \par \uc2 A triangle is forming on the hourly euro/dollar chart with parameters of $1.3100 and $1.3030.\par \par \uc2 While the euro holds above the $1.3030 area, some chartists see its Aug. 6 high of $1.3334 as an upside target, but there are hurdles before it gets there, including the triangle top and its 200-day moving average which comes in at about $1.3220. (Additional reporting by Charlotte Cooper and Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts Krishna Kumar in Sydney and Rick Lloyd in Singapore)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|GNN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100921e69l0005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
