\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japan On Guard For Dollar Slide After Fed Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2010\par 21:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--After \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to hinder its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation, Japan isn't likely to stand idly by if nervous investors once again rush to the perceived safety of the yen.\par \par \uc2 Even the hint of a fresh injection of stimulus into the moribund U.S. economy in the Federal Reserve statement that follows Tuesday's meeting would spell trouble for the greenback, but Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to barricade the doors against the dollar's decline, keeping the greenback propped above Y85.\par \par \uc2 "Eighty-five [yen] is kind of the very crucial level for Japanese authorities, exporters and market participants," said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "If the [Bank of Japan] fails to defend 85, it will cause a disastrous result," he said.\par \par \uc2 A breach of that key level could lead to a flood of investors piling on, forcing a steep and rapid decline in the dollar. That's exactly the opposite desire of Japanese authorities, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week brought the dollar back from 15-year lows just under Y83.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, most Fed watchers, including Yanagihara, expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take a steady-on approach, not making any changes to key rates or the accompanying policy statement, as recent economic data have not worsened dramatically since the last meeting of the country's rate-setting committee.\par \par \uc2 "The economic indicators are relatively stable," Yanagihara said of recent U.S. data. "It's too early to make an additional decision in terms of how they're going to stimulate the economy."\par \par \uc2 In the case of a stand-pat, no-change Fed, the dollar is likely to remain trapped in its recent range against the yen, with cautious investors unwilling to test the determination of Japanese authorities to wrest yen strength.\par \par \uc2 "If the Fed isn't going to significantly change its language, or introduce any further [quantitative easing], the dollar will firm," said Amelia Bourdeau, senior G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Stamford, Conn., noting \uc2 UBS\uc2  called for a stand-pat decision and statement.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the dollar gaining on the Fed's announcement, Japan's MOF is not likely to dip into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to speed the greenback's rise, she said.\par \par \uc2 "The market will be doing the work for them," Bourdeau said.\par \par \uc2 Monday afternoon, the dollar traded in an extremely tight range against the yen, as it has since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. The dollar was at Y85.78 from Y85.82 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 "We haven't seen a lot of client interest either way in the cross," Bourdeau said. "There's a good chance if the Fed comes in as expected, it will just stay around the level it is."\par \par \uc2 As long as the Y85 floor is not breached, Japan is not likely to try to push the dollar much higher, say to Y88 or Y90, Mizuho's Yanagihara said.\par \par \uc2 Bourdeau noted \uc2 UBS\uc2  had not seen any signs of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  directing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since Wednesday's action.\par \par \uc2 (Bradley Davis covers \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires from New York. He can be reached at 212-416-2654 or bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 09-20-10 1547ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100920e69k000bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
