\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Base Metals Market Nervy Ahead Of FOMC\b0\par\par\uc2 2,136 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 01:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals market nervous ahead of U.S. Federal Reserves policy meeting later today, says major market participant. Concern is that the Fed will highlight need to stimulate a struggling U.S. economy, and confirmation U.S. economy still struggling may spark a wave of profit taking, weighing on base metals prices once again, it says. Triland Metals, though says general copper market sentiment remains positive, even if metal now is seated in front of a wall at $8,000/metric ton. As for aluminum, coming week quite uncertain with holidays in Asia risking to take some buying support away while a rich range of economics events in U.S. could if trend keeps strong give further boost to prices with some key levels now in sight, Triland says. Aluminum faces some key resistance levels, but these can be overcome, it says, seeing the "usual" supportive factors, such as a weak USD, stronger equities and upbeat outlooks; says today could prove interesting as it is the last day in China before the mid-Autumn festival that marks an almost week long holiday, but adds that a late selloff overnight taking prices near intraday lows could be seen as a warning. \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  said cuts to some Chinese aluminum production to conserve power could prompt further cuts to aluminium production in other provinces "and will likely support aluminium prices over the next month or two." LME 3-month copper slipped $5 on day yesterday to $7,715/metric ton, tin lost $150 to $23,450, nickel lost $90 at $22,635, lead eased $7.50 at $2,195, but aluminum added $15 at $2,195, zinc jumped $29 at $2,180. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT [Dow Jones] Next resistance level for AUD/USD at 0.9498, followed by 0.9541 and 0.9650, says John Kyriakopoulos, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at NAB. Says should RBA hike rates over coming months and Chinese GDP growth re-accelerate, then a rise to 0.9650 is on cards. Add in U.S. quantitative easing and a fresh high above 0.9850 is likely. Says FOMC statement will be scrutinised for any hint Fed is closer to restarting QE. NAB doesn't believe U.S. economic outlook has weakened appreciably which would be the trigger for more QE. While Fed unlikely to announce more QE, statement might provide more details on contingencies which could cap any rise in USD. Adds RBA Board minutes at 0130 GMT might indicate RBA's intent to hike in October. AUD/USD now 0.9463. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise after 3-day holiday-extended weekend, helped by overnight gains in U.S. stocks; some Japan tech shares may outperform on higher ADRs such as \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO), Hitachi (6501.TO), say market analysts. Also say Nikkei may trade between 9600-9800 after ending up 1.2% at 9626.09 Friday. "Most buying will likely be done in morning trade," says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities, adding that investors are still cautious about USD/JPY level (now 85.72). Says rising gold prices may also help some commodity-linked stocks such as trading houses. Nikkei 225 December futures ended yesterday's Chicago trading at 9675 (vs Friday Osaka close of 9570).(ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ August migration gain should continue to aid economic recovery as remains fairly buoyant, steady after weakness earlier in year. Statistics NZ says on seasonably adjusted basis country gained 840 people in August, down from 890 in July, but considerably lower than 1,610 in August 2009. Seasonally-adjusted visitor arrivals +0.6% on month, +4.3% on year. Susan Hollows, NZ Stats acting population statistics manager says visitor numbers from China more than doubled in August on year; "Visitor arrivals from China during the last two years were heavily affected by a number of events including the global economic downturn, Sichuan earthquake, Beijing Olympics and H1N1 pandemic." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong inflation likely accelerated to nearly 2-year high in August due to higher rentals, food costs on back of buoyant consumer demand, economists say. August CPI tipped +3.2% on year vs July's +1.3%, according to median forecast from Dow Jones poll of 10 economists. "Food prices are expected to have stayed high due to bad weather during the summer months in mainland China, which is the main source of fresh food supply to Hong Kong," Hang Sang Bank chief economist Joanne Yim says. Standard Chartered bank economist Kelvin Lau says rising private housing rentals would have contributed to higher consumer prices, likely more apparent from 2H10 until early next year. Data due 0830 GMT Tuesday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.9% at 4693 after Wall Street broke through key resistance levels on encouraging earnings reports. DJIA up 1.4% at 10753.62, S&P 500 up 1.5% at 1142.71, Nasdaq up 1.7% at 2355.83. U.S. 10-year bond yield rose 7 bps to 2.71%. S&P 500 Home Building index up 5.6%, with Lennar up 8.2% after 3Q earnings beat expectations. S&P 500 Financials up 2.0%, with KBW Bank index up 2.3% and \uc2 Discover Financial Services\uc2  rose 3.8% after its earnings hinted at improving credit trends. S&P 500 Materials up 0.6%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 1.9%, Vale up 0.8%, Freeport up 2.0%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 1.6%, Barrick up 1.0%. BHP (BHP.AU) ADRs at A$39.07 vs local close at A$38.78. London-listed BHP up 2.1%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.3%. S&P 500 Industrials up 1.5%. S&P S&P 500 Energy up 1.7%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 1.9%. Spot gold at US$1,278.40 vs US$1,276.50. Nymex crude oil up US$1.20 at US$74.86. LME copper near flat, zinc up 1.3%, aluminum up 0.7%, nickel down 0.6%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3062 vs 1.3038. AUD/USD at 0.9459 vs 0.9372. S&P/ASX 200 last 4631.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2246 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian government bonds to keep a watchful eye of RBA policy minutes at 0130 GMT. Adam Carr, senior economist at ICAP, says it's pretty clear RBA is going to raise rates. Economy is close to capacity, with growth expected to move above trend in 2011 while inflation is at the top end of the 2%-3% target band. Says only timing is an issue. Adds FOMC is also on the radar but Carr expects substance of minutes will be little changed. Says private demand growth has been strong in U.S. with downward revisions in growth outlook fueled by stronger import growth. December 3-year bonds now 95.04, down 6 bps. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2246 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold $1,277.20/oz, down 10 cents early in Asia, after yesterday posting a fresh record at $1,283.70/oz, up 80 cents on the previous record, supported by concerns of a faltering economic recovery, USD movements, then helped late by stronger U.S. equities. In daily technical commentary, ScotiaMocatta said gold posted its 6th up day yesterday in the past seven trading sessions, now has a short term target of $1,290, a longer term measured target at $1,356, with support now at a previous high around $1,265. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold edged 80 cents higher overnight to $1,283.70/oz, before easing to be last $1,278.20, down 90 cents early in Asia. Gold futures on Comex in NY settled at 3rd consecutive record high on speculation about further stimulative U.S. government action ahead of today's meeting of FOMC. Gold "just defying gravity," said Ralph Preston, senior market analyst with Heritage West Financial in San Diego, with its momentum fueled by lingering uncertainty and risk aversion as U.S. and European economic recoveries stumble. "This market looks like it's poised to make its assault on $1,300," said Scott Meyers, senior trading analyst with Pioneer Futures. "This trend is so fiercely bullish and so strongly in place that nothing short of fundamental news that would directly affect this market" is likely to slow gold's advance. LME base metals mostly fell a tad as investors squared books ahead of the Fed meeting, with Calyon Credit Agricole analyst Robin Bhar saying this meeting may give clues about possible further quantitative-easing measures. LME 3-month copper slipped $5 vs Friday PM kerb to $7,715/metric ton, tin reversed direction, falling $150 to $23,450, while nickel also lost $150 at $23,050, lead eased $7 at $2,195, but aluminum added $14.50 at $2,194.50, zinc jumped $27 at $2,178. Of other precious metals, silver early in Asia adds 2 cents at $20.75/oz, platinum eases $2 at $1,621, palladium slips $1 at $536. Nymex October crude futures adds $1.02 overnight at $74.68/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely higher, taking cue from solid gains on Wall Street; financial stocks could outperform, in line with their U.S. peers. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets likely to take lead from stocks; a gain in equities could lift demand for riskier assets, help EUR, AUD, NZD; caution ahead of results of Fed policy meet later could mean tight ranges; USD could be pressured lower by worries of Fed quantitative easing; market will also be on lookout for any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Tokyo authorities to weaken JPY as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  returns from Monday's holiday. EUR/USD at 1.3060 vs 1.3061 in late NY trade, EUR/JPY at 111.96 vs 112.02, USD/JPY at 85.72 vs 85.75. South Korean markets shut for holiday. On data slate there's RBA board meeting minutes at 0130 GMT, NZ credit card statistics at 0300 GMT, Hong Kong CPI at 0830 GMT. Later there's UK public sector finances, Canada CPI, U.S. ICSC-\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chain store sales, housing starts, Johnson Redbook retail sales, FOMC rate decision, API oil report, ABC News consumer confidence. (colin.ng@dowjones.com) [ 09-20-10 1917ET ]\par \par \uc2 2240 GMT [Dow Jones] Both major influences on Australian wheat point to prices falling today with AUD still strong, last AUD/USD last 0.9459, holding most of yesterday's gain. In U.S. overnight, wheat prices retreated on profit taking after reaching their highest level since Russia announced a ban on grain exports early last month. Benchmark soft red winter wheat for December delivery on CBOT fell 7.5 cents, or 1%, at US$7.3175/bushel. Despite the slide, market participants remain nervous about global grain production because Russia needs more rain to plant its next wheat crop, a weekend freeze likely reduced the quality of Canada's crop, and Western Australia is too dry. A disappointing U.S. corn crop is seen as another supportive factor for wheat because both grains are used for animal feed. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales jumped A$9.50 to settle at A$287.50/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia surged A$13 to A$317.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2238 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA is more likely than not to raise cash rate target at Oct. 5 policy meeting, says Terry McCrann, columnist for the Herald Sun newspaper and a widely watched commentator on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There could also be a back-to-back hike in November, McCrann adds. Says RBA has signaled it has to start raising rates before its sees a rise in inflation, the premptive move driven by the strength of the mining boom. Elsewhere, Alan Mitchell, RBA watcher with the Australian Financial Review says there's more than a slight possibility the RBA will hike in October. Tightening labor markets and an investment booms means the RBA may have to step in to take some steam out of the economy. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 09-20-10 1917ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100920e69k000dc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
