\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei Likely To Rise; 9600-9800 Range Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 01:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise after 3-day holiday-extended weekend, helped by overnight gains in U.S. stocks; some Japan tech shares may outperform on higher ADRs such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  (6758.TO), Hitachi (6501.TO), say market analysts. Also say Nikkei may trade between 9600-9800 after ending up 1.2% at 9626.09 Friday. "Most buying will likely be done in morning trade," says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities, adding that investors are still cautious about USD/JPY level (now 85.72). Says rising gold prices may also help some commodity-linked stocks such as trading houses. Nikkei 225 December futures ended yesterday's Chicago trading at 9675 (vs Friday Osaka close of 9570).(ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ August migration gain should continue to aid economic recovery as remains fairly buoyant, steady after weakness earlier in year. Statistics NZ says on seasonably adjusted basis country gained 840 people in August, down from 890 in July, but considerably lower than 1,610 in August 2009. Seasonally-adjusted visitor arrivals +0.6% on month, +4.3% on year. Susan Hollows, NZ Stats acting population statistics manager says visitor numbers from China more than doubled in August on year; "Visitor arrivals from China during the last two years were heavily affected by a number of events including the global economic downturn, Sichuan earthquake, Beijing Olympics and H1N1 pandemic." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong inflation likely accelerated to nearly 2-year high in August due to higher rentals, food costs on back of buoyant consumer demand, economists say. August CPI tipped +3.2% on year vs July's +1.3%, according to median forecast from Dow Jones poll of 10 economists. "Food prices are expected to have stayed high due to bad weather during the summer months in mainland China, which is the main source of fresh food supply to Hong Kong," Hang Sang Bank chief economist Joanne Yim says. Standard Chartered bank economist Kelvin Lau says rising private housing rentals would have contributed to higher consumer prices, likely more apparent from 2H10 until early next year. Data due 0830 GMT Tuesday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.9% at 4693 after Wall Street broke through key resistance levels on encouraging earnings reports. DJIA up 1.4% at 10753.62, S&P 500 up 1.5% at 1142.71, Nasdaq up 1.7% at 2355.83. U.S. 10-year bond yield rose 7 bps to 2.71%. S&P 500 Home Building index up 5.6%, with Lennar up 8.2% after 3Q earnings beat expectations. S&P 500 Financials up 2.0%, with KBW Bank index up 2.3% and \uc2 Discover Financial Services\uc2  rose 3.8% after its earnings hinted at improving credit trends. S&P 500 Materials up 0.6%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 1.9%, Vale up 0.8%, Freeport up 2.0%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 1.6%, Barrick up 1.0%. BHP (BHP.AU) ADRs at A$39.07 vs local close at A$38.78. London-listed BHP up 2.1%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.3%. S&P 500 Industrials up 1.5%. S&P S&P 500 Energy up 1.7%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 1.9%. Spot gold at US$1,278.40 vs US$1,276.50. Nymex crude oil up US$1.20 at US$74.86. LME copper near flat, zinc up 1.3%, aluminum up 0.7%, nickel down 0.6%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3062 vs 1.3038. AUD/USD at 0.9459 vs 0.9372. S&P/ASX 200 last 4631.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2246 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian government bonds to keep a watchful eye of RBA policy minutes at 0130 GMT. Adam Carr, senior economist at ICAP, says it's pretty clear RBA is going to raise rates. Economy is close to capacity, with growth expected to move above trend in 2011 while inflation is at the top end of the 2%-3% target band. Says only timing is an issue. Adds FOMC is also on the radar but Carr expects substance of minutes will be little changed. Says private demand growth has been strong in U.S. with downward revisions in growth outlook fueled by stronger import growth. December 3-year bonds now 95.04, down 6 bps. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2246 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold $1,277.20/oz, down 10 cents early in Asia, after yesterday posting a fresh record at $1,283.70/oz, up 80 cents on the previous record, supported by concerns of a faltering economic recovery, USD movements, then helped late by stronger U.S. equities. In daily technical commentary, ScotiaMocatta said gold posted its 6th up day yesterday in the past seven trading sessions, now has a short term target of $1,290, a longer term measured target at $1,356, with support now at a previous high around $1,265. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold edged 80 cents higher overnight to $1,283.70/oz, before easing to be last $1,278.20, down 90 cents early in Asia. Gold futures on Comex in NY settled at 3rd consecutive record high on speculation about further stimulative U.S. government action ahead of today's meeting of FOMC. Gold "just defying gravity," said Ralph Preston, senior market analyst with Heritage West Financial in San Diego, with its momentum fueled by lingering uncertainty and risk aversion as U.S. and European economic recoveries stumble. "This market looks like it's poised to make its assault on $1,300," said Scott Meyers, senior trading analyst with Pioneer Futures. "This trend is so fiercely bullish and so strongly in place that nothing shor