\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Treasurys Steady Ahead Of FOMC Decision Tuesday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2010\par 10:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0828 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are steady in London with Asian trading also thin due to a holiday in Japan. The market is expected to trade in a narrow range ahead of the FOMC rate decision on Tuesday, with participants continuing to speculate on the possibility of additional asset purchases by the Fed despite somewhat mixed economic data. The NAHB housing market index at 1400 GMT is the day's only data print of note Monday. The 2-year note is lower at 99-26/32 to yield 0.468%, the 10-year note is up at 99-02/32 to yield 2.734%. The 30-year bond is up at 99-16/32, yielding 3.903%. December bunds are down 0.20 at 129.57. December gilts are 0.03 lower at 122.46. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD ends higher at 31.7520 vs 31.7390 Friday, off 31.5700 intraday low as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected to of buying late, may have bought around US$16 million-US$20 million to curb TWD strength, traders say. "TWD and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still on the upward trend, tracking the strong economy in the region," says one of the traders. Another trader tips pair in 31.6400-31.7700 Tuesday. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY is finding the 86.00 area a tough nut to crack, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , with its 60-day moving average at 86.10 just above. Says a break above the latter would likely trigger a short squeeze as trend-following systems recalibrate and push the rate toward 87.00. However, with U.S. yields within striking distance of their August/September lows and the dollar generally weak, USD/JPY is more likely to be rangebound between 84.45 and 86.00 for a little while longer. Stands now at 85.64. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR is mostly higher, regaining some of the losses it made Friday as the cost of insuring Irish debt rose to a new record high. A hawkish RBA and rallies in both Asian and European stocks have lifted risk sentiment, but moves are expected to be limited ahead of Tuesday's FOMC. Also, liquidity levels are low with Japan on holiday. The EUR is up at $1.3115, while the USD is down at Y85.58 despite fears of more BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The GBP is up at $1.5652. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary will do its homework to draft the 2011 budget in line with its newly-found deficit goal of less than 3% of GDP, says Raiffeisen Bank says. The planned 16% flat tax likely won't come yet in 2011, while other sectors besides the financial sector may need to pay an extra levy. The government won't be able to hide for long, at least not from financial markets, the fiscal consolidation it needs to carry out, Raiffeisen adds. Fiscal consolidation would bring a decline in Hungary's risk premia and lead to an appreciation of Hungarian assets. However, don't expect falling T-bond yields and HUF strengthening just yet as the government may continue its election talk before the Oct. 3 local vote. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY is looking constrained. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  notes that JPY bulls will refrain from taking aggressive positions ahead of Tuesday's FOMC, as there is a risk of more QE being announced. This is leaving the pair under 86.0 amid continued fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will return to the market with more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pair is now at 85.61. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian government bonds mixed, with short-to-medium term securities higher, while long-dated securities dragged by profit-taking, dealers say. "For local investors, bonds are already expensive. But for foreigners, the yields are still good for carry-trade purposes," says Bank Danamon's bond analyst Helmi Arman; 5-year yield down at 7.41% vs 7.42% late Friday; 10-year yield down at 7.87% vs 7.89%; 15-year yield up at 8.42% vs 8.39%; 20-year yield up at 8.88% vs 8.87%; and 30-year yield up at 9.18% vs 9.17%. Still, analysts expect investors to continue accumulating bonds, especially over 10-year tenors, as new supply of such securities to dry out toward year-end. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0813 GMT [Dow Jones] Poland's zloty is up in tandem of the euro's strengthening against the dollar, a dealer says. The zloty is still seen in a 3.93-3.96 range against the euro, where it has remained in recent weeks. EUR/PLN is at 3.9510 from 3.9563 late Friday, USD/PLN is at 3.0138 from 3.0332. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.64% from 4.65% late Friday, 5-year at 5.09% from 5.11%, 10-year at flat at 5.48%. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0811 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA chief Stevens has articulated a case for raising rates again soon, with tighter monetary policy screws likely to come at the expense of still-fragile consumer demand. Such is the overwhelming impact of resource sector surge on overall economy that RBA may now think it doesn't have luxury of waiting for consumption to bounce fully before hiking; indeed, it may want to prevent the full resurrection of consumer spending in order to contain inflation, which is now at 2.7%, toward top of RBA's 2%-3% target band. An oft-repeated mantra of the RBA is a mining investment boom can't exist easily next to strong household consumption growth, given combined demand surge; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may feel it needs to move to restrictive policy settings before too long. See MONEY TALKS: RBA May Hike Ahead Of Consumer Demand Recovery. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swedish Government is now supported by a minority meaning "the coming four years will entail a higher threat of recurrent government crisis or the possible need for an extra election," says Swedbank. "We expect EUR/SEK to test 9.30 since we expect investors to decrease long SEK positions," says the bank. In the longer term Swedbank still sees potential for SEK and has not changed its 3-month forecast of 9.10. EUR/SEK at 9.2232. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA Governor Glenn Stevens articulated a case for raising interest rates again soon, with tighter monetary policy screws likely to come at the expense of still-fragile consumer demand. The overwhelming impact of the resource sector surge may have the RBA thinking it doesn't have the luxury of waiting for consumption to bounce fully before hiking rates. Inflation level is likely a source of discomfort for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  proud of its record on containing prices over two decades. 80% bets that rate will rise before year end look solid. That may move a notch higher if consumer data starts to improve quickly. See =MONEY TALKS: RBA May Hike Ahead Of Consumer Demand Recovery (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds mixed late as profit-taking alternated with bargain-hunting amid lack of fresh leads, says dealer with local bank. "But volume traded picked up a bit as more fund managers and investors returned from their extended holidays," says dealer; notes focus still in short-term paper. Tips yields to hold steady at current levels Tuesday ahead of August inflation data (expected on Wednesday) and auction details of the new 3-year Islamic bond (expected Thursday latest). Yields on August 2015 MGS flat at 3.31%, September 2017 MGS +2 bps at 3.56% and November 2019 MGS unchanged at 3.64%.(elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 09-20-10 0428ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100920e69k00059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
