\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Have Found Floor At 82-84 - Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2010\par 08:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may have found a floor at 82.00-84.00 area, following last week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Patrick Bennett, analyst with Standard Bank. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's authorities "have made their intention clear, and they have been successful" in containing the previous JPY rally, leaving market looking for outside direction. Still, reckons some more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be forthcoming in a matter of days or weeks as global economy remains in poor shape and another round of JPY strength is possible. Notes Fed statement tomorrow likely to be a bit of non-event, reiterating previous wariness over pace of economic recovery. USD/JPY last at 85.69 vs 85.82 late Friday in NY trade. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may continue its monetary tightening stance, may lift its two key policy rates by 25 bps each by December, in-line with its commitment to "continued policy response to contain inflation and inflationary expectation," says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . House says RBI will keep up efforts to narrow the gap between real growth and negative real rates, which is currently "still very high" and risks creating imbalances in economy. "Another 50 basis points in rate hikes would take place next (calendar) year, in our view. We expect policy actions to be symmetric going forward, i.e. the 100 basis points spread between repo and reverse repo would persist," house says. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0544 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW trims gains as Kospi turns higher (last +0.3%); pair last at 1,161.1 vs 1,163.7 earlier, 1,160.5 late Friday. But traders peg key support at 1,160, due to chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  should pair fall below that level. Local bank trader expects pair to trade mostly within tight 1,160-1,162 range for rest of session. "Dollar-offers are pretty strong due to foreign equity fund inflows, while offshore players are also (slightly) biased towards selling dollars. Still, KRW1,160 support will likely hold," with corporate demand providing support, he says. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 0.2% at 921.36 at midday break in uninspiring trade. Market awaits fresh catalysts while uncertainty over 3G licensing still in focus. Court decision due Thursday on whether it will overturn injunction against 3G mobile license auction. Benchmark likely to remain in negative territory with 915-927 range tipped, says Finansia Syrus Securities' analyst Somchai Anektaweepon. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) down 3.9% at THB4.90, PTT (PTT.TH) down 0.4% at THB284, Banpu flat at THB648, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) +0.6% at THB90 on rebound after recent falls, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) +1.6% at THB2.56. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] Thailand may lose some appeal as destination for petrochemicals investment after government includes part of sector in list of 11 harmful industrial activities, potentially damaging role as regional exporter. Upstream, intermediate projects at risk, though existing, new downstream petrochemical activity not affected for now, as adequate supplies from domestic plants will still flow. Publication of list seen as another blow to Thailand's investment status, with proposed new projects covered by list having to face environment, health impact assessments, public hearing, approval of independent body. "These higher requirements certainly reduce the attractiveness of these businesses, as investment will take a longer time and costs will be increased inevitably," says Bualuang Securities analyst Thanatthep Chantarakarn. See: =FOCUS: \uc2 Thai Petrochemical Industry\uc2  May Lose Some Investment Shine. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [Dow Jones] Korean shares turn higher, +0.2% at 1830.09 in thin trade, helped by good foreign appetite for local shares. "Trade volume seems very small ahead of the Chuseok holidays (Tuesday-Thursday), but the continued foreign buying is supporting the index," says \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2 ' Lee Kyoung-min. Foreigners, retail investors are net buyers in Korea Stock Exchange today, snatching up around KRW147 billion, KRW88 billion, respectively so far. Techs mostly down after recent strong rally, analysts say. \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) down 1.5% at KRW101,000 while Hynix (000660.SE) down 1.1% at KRW22,250. But auto shares up on positive business outlook, as automakers considered main beneficiaries if free trade between EU, Korea takes effect from July next year, analysts say. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +2.3% at KRW155,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +1.9% at KRW34,900. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation rate up, past RBI's key lending rate of 6% as banks scramble to meet cash short-fall amid liquidity pressures, says trader with state-run bank. Overnight call rate at 6.25% vs 6.00% last close, CBLO at 6.00% vs 5.75% at open. "RBI's rate hikes are, obviously, the main reason for higher borrowing rates but heavy cash outgo in the previous week for advance tax payments (estimated at INR450 billion) has resulted in banks' borrowing heavily from not just the interbank market but also mutual funds (via CBLO route) and RBI's discount window," trader says. Tips call rate 6%-6.25% band in session. Indian banks borrowed INR422.90 billion from RBI's discount window Friday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares by midday down 0.6% at 3363.160 in moderate volume, off earlier low of 3345.220, as investors take profits in banks, consumer-related blue chips after recent sharp gains, traders say; tip support at 3340. "Bargain-buying by some local institutional funds has lifted the main index from its low," says trader at Valbury Securities. Among decliners: Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) down 1.9% at IDR10,200, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 2.9% at IDR6,700, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 0.6% at IDR54,350. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD last at 7.7645 vs 7.7655 this morning, 7.7642 Friday; pair off morning high as some local banks sell, says senior trader at Singapore bank; tips pair in 7.7630-7.7670 band near term. "The sales (of USD vs HKD) by local banks are not sizable with less than US$50 million per deal. Still, the Hong Kong dollar is likely to continue to see demand due to upcoming IPOs," trader adds. IPOs in pipeline include coking-coal producer Mongolian Mining which seeks to raise up to US$677 million ahead of Hong Kong listing Oct. 13. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA Stevens' upbeat speech hints at possible rate hike by year end, though policy makers will be reluctant to make the jump given an uncertain global outlook, says RBC strategist Michael Turner. "We continue to look for the next hike to come in 1Q 2010, although acknowledge risks it may come before this." Markets now pricing 80% chance of 25bp December hike. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures up in Asian trading, as typhoon, geopolitical friction offset worries over outlook for U.S. economy as Fed's FOMC prepares to meet. Nymex crude for October delivery +22 cents at $73.88/bbl on Globex; more actively traded November contract +34 cents at $75.26/bbl. Market likely reacting to Typhoon Fanapi, strongest to hit South China Sea this year, approaching oil installations offshore China. Ongoing political spat between China, Japan also stoking market fears as both sides refuse to back down over arrest of Chinese trawler captain in waters around disputed island chain. However, upward movement in oil prices restrained as investors otherwise have little incentive to take on more risk ahead of Fed's policy meet Tuesday, U.S. housing starts data for August. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0459 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered still expects another 25 bps hike to 2.0% at \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's October 20 meeting but "won't be surprised" if BOT pauses, as MOF and BOT increasingly feel the pressure of strong baht. "Sooner or later, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stand pat on its policy rate and keep it unchanged for quarters," says StanChart senior economist Usara Wilaipich. Says easing global demand, high base effect likely to result single digit export growth in 4Q. Thailand runs trade surplus of $643 million in August; exports +23.88% on year at $16.45 billion, tad stronger than +20.6% in July, though still far below robust +46.3% in June. Imports last month jumped 41.1% to $15.81 billion. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com) [ 09-20-10 0202ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100920e69k00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
