\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Steady; 8,970-8,990 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2010\par 03:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,975 as players cautious, keeping positions small ahead of FOMC meeting tomorrow, dealers say. "I think the dollar-rupiah will remain trapped in a narrow range today with holiday in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  taking out risk of (another yen)\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," dealer says; 8,970-8,990 range tipped for day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures down 0.3% at 21,930, as cash market tipped to pull back, but such retreat likely modest, eyed as normal consolidation, as HSI up in 6 out of last 7 trading sessions. Contract ends Friday at 28-point premium to cash, continuing to signal positive near-term bias. September open interest rises 2,417 lots to 107,450. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] European data not expected to give EUR much assistance this week, Credit Agricole says, anticipating eurozone September flash PMIs and German IFO survey both set to weaken as business, manufacturing confidence comes off the boil. "If we are correct that the Fed (Tuesday) maintains its policy stance whilst risk aversion increases over coming days, the USD may find itself in a firmer position to recoup some of its losses both against the EUR and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," bank adds, noting this should leave EUR/USD vulnerable to drop back toward support in 1.2955 in very short-term. "Sovereign worries in Europe are clearly not fading quickly and sovereign bond auctions in Greece, Spain and Portugal will garner plenty of attention this week, with spreads set to remain under pressure," especially after news that bank stress tests for Greece have been postponed. EUR/USD last at 1.3065 vs 1.3039 late in NY trade Friday. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed's policy meeting tomorrow is the event for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in days ahead, though it's likely it won't opt for more quantitative easing which would be USD positive, says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Joe Capurso. If Fed more bearish than expected, then this should be offset by likely better data in form of jobless claims, durable goods orders. "In our view, the Fed is unlikely to decide to increase its bond-purchase program. But the risk is clearly for more Fed action." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] CBA joins ranks of those calling AUD/USD lower by year end, expecting pair to trade at 0.8800 by December as U.S. economy shows signs of improvement, says strategist Joseph Capurso. Pair last 0.9394, session high in quiet trade. But adds, AUD may get boost near term with focus for remainder of day on RBA Governor Stevens speech due 0330 GMT. "With financial market volatility at the low end of recent ranges, we see AUD/USD tracking higher because the Australian economy continues to out-perform global peers." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI last +0.4% at 1472.11, led by gains in construction stocks on hopes government's long-term economic plan to be unveiled tomorrow may include more details on proposed infrastructure projects. "While plenty of information...will be made available, the focus will fall on sectors, like oil & gas, energy, infrastructure/construction and financial services, with immediate probable implications on the stock market," HwangDBS Vickers Research says. Dealers peg immediate resistance for KLCI at 1480, with support at 1465. Among key builders, Gamuda (5398.KU) +1.1% at MYR3.78, IJM (3336.KU) +2.8% at MYR5.14; HELP (7236.KU) +1.3% at MYR4.60 on plans for secondary listing in Singapore. Among decliners, Tan Chong (4405.KU) down 1.7% at MYR5.90 on profit-taking following 26% rise so far in September. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped to consolidate with downward bias on absence of strong market leads; benchmark PSEi may slide towards 3950 support after closing Friday 0.7% higher to 3979.35, after closing at all-time high day before; next support 3920, resistance 4020. "We may see interest in mining stocks after gold hit record prices last week. But that wouldn't mean much for the index (PSEi) because they contribute little to the index," says Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities; Lepanto Mining (LC.PH) has sought and was granted voluntary suspension because of an impending "material disclosure" which it didn't specify.(cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD gaining again early, and could get further lift should it break back above 0.9400, says Greg Gibbs, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for RBS Australia. "There's not much going on this morning for a real catalyst. But there's been a fair bit of model-generated buying coming in at 0.94 a couple times already," says Gibbs. AUD/USD recently at 0.9394, up from morning low of 0.9363. Looking at charts, cross reached new 2-year high around 0.9470 last week, which should serve as resistance in short term. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY last at 85.73 vs 85.82 late Friday in NY trade, with Japan markets closed for holiday, remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  players focused on Tuesday's FOMC meeting. "As far as exchange rate markets are concerned, this week's FOMC meeting... is more about the Japanese yen, than about the U.S. economy," DBS says. "Last wee