t S$0.22, \uc2 Memstar Technology\uc2  (5MS.SG) flat at S$0.085, \uc2 DMX Technologies\uc2  (5CH.SG) +3.5% at S$0.44, Beyonics (B19.SG) +2.0% at S$0.26. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may take light breather at open after 1.3% rise to 21,970.86 Friday, which marked 6th day of gain out of 7 for cumulative gains of 4.2%. Still, any pullback likely shallow in magnitude, short in duration given strong market momentum. While blue-chip index was only +1.3% Friday, "it masked the fact many stronger stocks, including second-tier companies, were gaining much more," says Alex Wong of Ample Financial Group. Among blue chips, Hutchison (0013.HK) stood out Friday, ending +4.5% at HK$64.15; while near-term consolidation expected, stock's medium-term re-rating since 1H results showing 3G burden finally lifting likely hasn't done yet. \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) may pause ahead of FY results later today (expected after market close). (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold posts fresh record high of $1,282.90/oz on Friday, and has more to go, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in weekly commodities review, citing exchange rate and interest rate trends, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases, heightened macro economic volatility and de-hedging as major demand influences. But how high, and is price over-extended or has a bubble formed?, bank asks, reiterating that prices would need to surpass $1,455 to be considered extreme in real terms, hit $2,000 to represent a bubble. So, bank says its target of $1,600 in 2012 isn't excessive given favourable interest rate, exchange rate trends and appearance of new sources of demand from both private, public sectors. "For the time being we believe the drivers of this rally are fundamental rather than speculative," with physically-backed ETFs playing an important role, it says. Last $1,276.75. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex October crude likely to consolidate for now after rebounding $1 from 2-week low $72.75/bbl Friday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart still negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought, while positive MACD histogram bars contracting. Resistance at $74.11 (previous base set Thursday); breach would expose upside to $75.25 (Friday's high), then $76.21 (previous base set Tuesday, near 55-day moving average), $77.41 (200-day moving average) and $78.04 (September 13 reaction high). But fall below $72.75 support would target $72.63 (September 7 low), then $71.53 (August 31 reaction low), $70.76 (August 25 reaction low), psychological $70.00 and $69.51 (June 7 reaction low). October crude last up 11 cents at $73.77/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP likely in tight range but biased up as losses in regional stock markets expected to hurt risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including PHP; pair may rise to 44.30 vs 44.21 late Friday; tipped in 44.00-44.50 range. Players likely cautious as FOMC will meet this week, says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB steady at 30.74-30.76 vs 30.73-30.75 late Friday, in quiet trade. Pair biased downwards on still bearish overall trend USD vs Asians while firmer gold prices may prompt some USD-selling by gold traders, says local dealer. Eyes on Thai court's ruling whether to accept appeal from National Telecommunications Commission in move to overturn injunction against its planned 3G auction, also on upcoming FOMC meeting. Pair tipped in 30.65-30.80 range. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to rise, challenge key 8200 level vs 8158.33 close on Friday (up 0.7%), boosted by local investors buying ahead of Mid-Autumn Festival holiday Wednesday, but gains likely limited on profit taking, says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Adds investors also likely to cheer smaller-than-expected losses caused by impact of Typhoon Fanapi yesterday, most powerful typhoon to hit Taiwan so far this year. Tips gains in steel makers, builders on reconstruction demand, but petrochemical stocks likely lower after Commercial Times today reports typhoon caused damage to petroleum plants in southern Taiwan; report unconfirmed so far, companies currently being contacted for confirmation. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may rise, tracking slight gains in U.S. stocks on Friday, as concerns over possible interest rate hike likely to ease. Shanghai Composite Index tipped in 2550-2650 range, after last ending down 0.2% at 2598.69. But gains likely small; "ahead of the long holidays starting this week, a downward trend in trading volume is likely as investors appear to be more interested in observing the market than participating in it," says Wei Daoke at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Banks may rise after China's banking regulator Friday says it has punished some banks that offered higher deposit rates than allowed by government. Some economists have called for China to remove the cap on deposit rates to offset rising inflation. Also likely to help sentiment is China Securities Journal report today, citing unnamed sources, that all of China's 5 largest banks have loan-reserve ratios of at least 2.5%. Note, China Business News reported last week CBRC plans to require banks to put aside equivalent of 2.5% of outstanding loans as reserves next year; CBRC has no such requirement currently. Shenzhen index last ends +0.1% at 1157.12. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may consolidate, taking breather after falling for 7 consecutive sessions as fixing expected to be set higher following gains in USD index Friday. Central parity tipped near 6.7250 vs 6.7172 Friday. "If the fixing is set higher than CNY6.7300, traders may push the pair higher on expectations that the PBOC may set the central parity above CNY6.7500 in the near future," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Pair tipped in 6.7200-6.7400 range vs 6.7235 last close. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.6% at 4611.7, hits 2-day low of 4606.6 after Friday's outperformance on the back of Australian dollar strength proved premature. AUD/USD retreated overnight and remains on the back foot this morning, leaving traders with little incentive to expect fresh risk taking ahead of Tuesday's U.S. housing starts data and FOMC meeting. Heavyweight materials and banking stocks restraining the market, BHP (BHP.AU) down 0.6%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 0.9%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) down 1.6% and major banks down 0.6%-1.4%. Defensives taking up some of the slack, with \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) up 0.7%, CSL (CSL.AU) up 0.1%. "Friday's rally in the Australian share market was a little premature, so there's been a little bit of profit taking," says Macquarie Private Wealth Investment Adviser Shannon Briggs. "But Wall Street is holding up so there's not much downside potential and we might just stay a touch quiet for the next day or two." Says Tuesday's U.S. housing starts data could be a reality check for the bulls, although FOMC meeting is likely to take an optimistic view on the economic outlook. "It seems the markets have been trying to find positives rather than negatives in the U.S. economic data, so hopefully that will continue." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] Korean shares under steady selling pressure as investors take profit ahead of Chuseok holidays, says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  analyst Oh Hyun-seok; Kospi last down 0.4% at 1819.94. "The market is a bit weaker right now; (the index) hit a new high for the year (last Friday), and there's some uncertainties with the Chuseok holidays. Those who want to buy will wait until after the holidays, and the sellers will want to cash out now." Tips 1812 support, 1830 resistance for day. Large caps mostly lower; Posco (005490.SE) off 1.2% at KRW506,000, KB Financial (105560.SE) down 1.0% at KRW49,450. But autos outperforming broader market on technical grounds after worries that impact of Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY had dented their share price; \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +1.6% at KRW154,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +1.2% at KRW34,650. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com) [ 09-19-10 2127ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100920e69k0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
