\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-GLOBAL ECONOMY WEEKAHEAD-Slowing recovery a policy headache\b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2010\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeat of item transmitted on Sunday, Sept. 19)\par \par \uc2 * Countries grapple with global recovery losing momentum\par \par \uc2 * Fed unlikely to unleash large-scale bond buys next week\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries other exporters\par \par \uc2 By Kristina Cooke\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 20 (Reuters) - The sputtering global economy is complicating life for policymakers from Washington to Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 When the financial crisis erupted in 2007, authorities around the world began throwing nearly everything they had at the problem to avoid a re-run of the Great Depression.\par \par \uc2 Three years later, with the global recovery losing momentum, they find themselves in a tough spot yet again.\par \par \uc2 This time, if things get worse, they won't have as many tricks left in the bag to jump-start their faltering economies. What's more, whatever medicine they prescribe may well come with unpleasant side effects.\par \par \uc2 For the Federal Reserve, the medicine in question could be more large-scale purchases of U.S. Treasury bonds, though officials are unlikely to unleash that option at their policy-setting meeting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disagree on many fundamental questions: How dark is the outlook? What should the threshold for further support be? Would pumping more money into the financial system be effective or could it do more harm than good?\par \par \uc2 "The economy is not falling apart, so easing now could send a signal that folks at the Fed are a little bit panicked," said Stephen Stanley, chief economist at Pierpont Securities in Stamford, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 "They need to make sure if they do more that the case for it is strong and the case is well-explained."\par \par \uc2 Further Fed easing could make things even more difficult for other countries, which are resorting to their own tactics to defend their economies. <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 For a graphic showing a snapshot of major players: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/kuh44p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/kuh44p}}}\uc2  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in six years to weaken its yen in a last-ditch bid to improve the competitiveness of its exports in a world of weak demand.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen curbs Japanese exports and weighs on production, making it harder for Tokyo to end the long spell of falling prices that dampens consumer demand and discourages corporate investment.\par \par \uc2 Although Japanese markets will be closed for holidays on two days next week, another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still be forthcoming and will keep markets on edge.\par \par \uc2 Japan's unilateral move to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked fears other countries might follow suit and deal a crippling blow to the global recovery. It also elicited stinging criticism from other countries.\par \par \uc2 "We don't like the behavior of the Japanese authorities," the head of the euro zone group of finance ministers, Jean-Claude Juncker, told reporters on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 European growth has been driven by Germany's export-led economy. Policymakers worry a strengthening euro, which rose nearly 5 percent in three days on Japan's move, could further slow Europe's recovery.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone seems to want a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this environment to drive exports, but clearly not everyone can," said Mark McCormick, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 A range of European data next week on manufacturing, consumer confidence and Germany's business climate is expected to add to evidence of a slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Coupled with revived worries about European debt burdens and the health of euro zone banks, the slowdown spells the potential for more anxiety for financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept rates on hold at a record low 1 percent this month and extended unlimited liquidity to banks until at least early next year, further delaying its exit from emergency lending measures.\par \par \uc2 Markets will watch any comments from ECB policymakers about the outlook for the economy and policy there. ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet will attend a euro-zone conference in Estonia on Sunday and Monday, while ECB Executive Board member Juergen Stark will speak in Italy on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The global backdrop will no doubt be on Federal Reserve officials' minds as they mull the costs and benefits of pumping more money into the financial system.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke said in late August that he would need to see a significant deterioration in U.S. economic conditions before easing monetary policy further. The Fed has already cut interest rates to near zero and bought $1.7 trillion in longer-term bonds.\par \par \uc2 It is unlikely the threshold for further large-scale purchases has yet been met, given a slightly better tone to recent data.\par \par \uc2 The data "lifts some of the pressure, and when you have a divided committee, if you are not pressured, you move sideways," said Vince Reinhart, a former senior Fed staffer.\par \par \uc2 Some Fed officials worry that if any further monetary easing is not effective in spurring the recovery, it would tarnish the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's much-needed credibility. Others worry about the potential for market disruption or sowing the seeds for inflation down the road.\par \par \uc2 "They will acknowledge the problem in the outlook and look for a compromise that kicks the can down the road," Reinhart said.\par \par \uc2 Some officials, including Dallas Fed President Richard Fisher, argue that the ball is now in the government's court to do more to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. House of Representatives will vote on a package of loan incentives and tax breaks for small businesses next week.\par \par \uc2 With the unemployment rate stuck at 9.6 percent, Democrats are eager to show voters they are taking steps to help the economy as the Nov. 2 congressional elections approach. Many of their job-creation efforts have been blocked by Republicans this year.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-WEEKAHEAD/OUTLOOK (REPEAT)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|Z|ABX|BNX|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100923e69k000tk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
