\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Week Higher, Boosted By Technology Sector\b0\par\par\uc2 2,554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2010\par 00:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's markets are closed Monday for the Respect-for-the-Aged Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10607.85  +13.02  +0.12% \par Nasdaq     2315.61  +12.36  +0.54% \par S&P 500    1125.59   +0.93  +0.08% \par FTSE 100   5508.45  -31.69  -0.57% \par Xetra DAX  6209.76  -39.89  -0.64% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 25/32 flat  0.472% flat \par 5Y Tsy   99 2/32 +3/32 1.444% -2.1BP \par 10Y Tsy 98 31/32 +5/32 2.743% -2.0BP \par  \par USD/JPY       85.73-77     -0.02% \par Range         85.85-85.68 \par EUR/USD       1.3065-69    +0.18% \par Range         1.3066-1.3044 \par AUD/USD       0.9377-80    +0.16% \par Range         0.9382-0.9359 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.60-11.00% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.50-10.90% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    12.00-12.60% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,277.50 +$3.70 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,273.00-$1,284.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $73.66   -$0.91 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks climbed narrowly into positive territory Friday, as technology companies added to the week's modest gains.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 13.02 points, or 0.12%, to 10607.85 Friday. The measure climbed for a third straight week, adding 1.39% since Monday's open. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-share index gained 0.93, or 0.08%, to 1125.59. The S&P 500 closed up for a third consecutive week, rising 1.45% this week.\par \par \uc2 But the technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite was the best performer, rising for eight straight days and outperforming both blue chips and the broader market. The Nasdaq gained 12.36, or 0.54%, to 2315.61 Friday, and climbed 3.26% over the week, as ebbing fears of a double-dip recession and encouraging earnings lifted technology stocks.\par \par \uc2 BlackBerry maker Research In Motion rose 23 cents, or 0.5%, to 46.72, after posting a surge in its fiscal second-quarter profit and revenue, though the company added fewer new subscribers than it had expected.\par \par \uc2 Business-software company \uc2 Oracle\uc2  jumped 2.12, or 8.4%, to 27.48, after its fiscal first-quarter profit grew 20% as the company benefited from new revenue from its \uc2 Sun Microsystems\uc2  acquisition, as well as strong demand for software licenses.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, chip maker \uc2 Texas Instruments\uc2  climbed 74 cents, or 3%, to 25.72, after the company said it plans to buy back $7.5 billion worth of its shares and would raise its quarterly dividend by 8%.\par \par \uc2 While the large-cap indexes have surged in September, much of that gain came early in the month. The latest week's gains were slim, as investors continued to buy safe-haven assets including Treasurys and gold.\par \par \uc2 "We're still struggling on a daily basis on: are we up or down for the year?" said Julius Ridgway, investment adviser at Medley & Brown. The Dow is currently up 1.72% for the year; the S&P 500 is up 0.94% since Dec. 31. "Before we break out to new highs, you're going to have to see a compelling reason and in the absence of a compelling reason, you'll see choppy markets," he said.\par \par \uc2 China's shares fell slightly Friday to end lower for a third consecutive session, though trading was light amid investor caution about the possibility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising interest rates as part of its efforts to control inflation.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely consolidate around 2600 this week as investors lock in profits ahead of the three-day Mid-Autumn Festival holiday starting Sept. 22, and the seven-day Golden Week holiday starting Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 "We saw very light trading in banks and overall volume is too low for the (Shanghai) index to stage a strong rebound," said Han Hao, an analyst at China Minzu Securities.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices sank for a fourth straight day in U.S. trading Friday on continued concerns over near-term oversupply as a major crude oil pipeline from Canada resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gold futures extended their record highs Friday amid fresh worries about the European banking system, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and talk of further easing of monetary policy to give a jump-start to the sluggish U.S. economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed mostly higher on the London Metal Exchange Friday, but pared gains made earlier in the day as the euro eased against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro fell from a five-week high against the dollar Friday after long-smoldering worries once again flared over the euro-zone's sovereign-debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While Irish officials denied reports that the fiscally stressed country would need a financial lifeline, investors still moved out of the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost to insure Ireland's government debt against default soared Friday to its highest-ever level, with other fiscally shaky euro-zone countries, such as Portugal and Greece, also seeing an increase in the cost to insure their debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What is most disconcerting about recent pressures on Ireland is that Ireland has been the poster child for successful austerity measures," said Win Thin, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, noting the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had so far avoided a steep decline. "If Ireland can't pull through by doing all the right things, how can markets get bullish about Greece, Portugal and Spain?"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro has risen 9% since June, when it hit a four-year low of $1.1876.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was at $1.3039 from $1.3085. The dollar was at Y85.82 from Y85.81, while the euro was at Y111.89 from Y112.29. The U.K. pound was at $1.5626 from $1.5635. The dollar was at CHF1.0098 from CHF1.0146.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 81.437 from 81.224.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Irish Finance Minister Brian Lenihan denied the reports of Ireland having to eventually seek outside help if losses mount in its banking sector. "It is based on a local misinterpretation of a research report," he said, referring to a note from Barclay's bank analysts that had suggested Ireland could eventually have to tap the IMF or EU for help. An IMF spokeswoman Friday also said the IMF doesn't see Ireland needing any financial assistance from the institution.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The denials didn't soothe investor nerves, said Brian Dolan, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com in Bedminster, N.J.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is going to be a source of anxiety for many, many months to come," he said of the euro-zone debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the euro didn't weaken markedly, suggesting "it is possible that investors are beginning to reconcile themselves to the possibility that financial stress in a small euro-zone country may not translate automatically in broader European stress," said Steven Englander, head of G10 strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors slashed their bets against the euro by 60%, according to data released Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net speculative bets against the euro were at 9,644 contracts with a value of $1.56 billion in the week ended Tuesday, compared with the previous week's data that showed 23,700 net short contracts with a value of $3.9 billion, according to a Scotia Capital analysis of the weekly Commitments of Traders report released Friday afternoon by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar remained trapped in an extremely tight range against the yen, holding onto its sharp gains from Wednesday's massive Y2 trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Prime Minister said Friday the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conducted earlier this week has been successful, to some extent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is having a certain impact" in taming the yen's rise, Naoto Kan said at a press conference after reshuffling his Cabinet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bets in favor of the Japanese yen decreased slightly as of the week ended Tuesday, one day before Japan's action to suppress yen strength, the Commodity Futures Trading Commission data showed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the market is bracing for Tuesday's meeting of the Federal Reserve, while staying on high alert for signs of more Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Without a doubt, the Fed rate decision is the main event," said Vassili Serebriakov, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any comments from the Fed that rekindle worries about the health of the U.S. recovery could lure investors into the perceived safety of the yen. A fresh wave of yen appreciation could then draw Japanese authorities back into the market for another round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders expect the dollar to travel between Y84.50 and Y87 in the week ahead. They see the euro traveling between $1.2800 and $1.3250.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys ended the week on a high note Friday as the latest U.S. reports on inflation and consumer sentiment raised worries about deflation and the health of the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An extra boost for the bond market came from renewed concerns about the euro zone's sovereign-debt problems, fueled by some media reports that Ireland may need to seek external funding to support its banking system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Irish officials and the International Monetary Fund denied the claims in the reports, which tempered the rally in Treasurys in the afternoon session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the moderate gains Friday helped long-dated Treasurys snap a two-day losing streak. The benchmark 10-year note made its first weekly gains in four weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1954 GMT Friday in New York, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was up 5/32 in price and its yield fell to 2.743%, lower than 2.794% at the end of last week. The 30-year bond was 12/32 higher to yield 3.905%. Bond yields move inversely to prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Buying was the most heavy in the morning as the consumer price index excluding energy and food was unchanged last month, compared with a 0.1% gain forecast by economists. A private survey by Reuters and the University of Michigan showed consumer sentiment fell to 66.6 in mid-September, the lowest level since August 2009. [ 19-09-10 2205GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The data today reinforces the view that inflation should not be a concern if Federal Reserve policymakers do enact further measures to support the economy, nor should it constrain them from making the decision," said John Briggs, U.S. interest-rate strategist at RBS Securities Inc. in Stamford, Conn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent sessions, fear of deflation had eased, which had cut speculation of further monetary stimulus from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , known as quantitative easing. But Friday's consumer price index report again flagged the risks of deflation, which hurts the economy by discouraging consumer spending and eating into corporate earnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A benign inflation report certainly gives the Fed more flexibility" regarding the option of more Treasury buying, said Richard Bryant, head of Treasury trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York, adding that the selling Thursday that sent the 30-year bond yield to a one-month high of 3.959% enticed buyers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed policy makers will meet Tuesday to set interest-rate policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Friday after the state-run Shanghai Securities News reported there was a very low chance of an interest rate hike by China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the near term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, a trader at a Shanghai-based local bank said bonds would face selling-pressure due to a possible liquidity squeeze ahead of the Mid-Autumn Festival and National Day holidays.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over the weekend, a People's Bank of China survey showed much higher expectation among residents for property price rises, illustrating the challenge that policy makers face to rein in a potential real estate bubble.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the survey conducted in the third quarter and posted on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's website Sunday, 36.6% of residents expected property prices to rise, 7.2 percentage points higher than that in the previous quarter, though still 4.9 percentage points lower than that in the third quarter last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PBOC adviser Li Daokui warned that high property prices are becoming a "social problem" as they have already exceeded a level that most ordinary people are able to pay.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should consider letting bank deposit rates float freely to offset inflation instead of introducing a small interest rate hike that wouldn't be effective in curbing rising consumer prices, an adviser to the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Saturday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In theory it's not right to see negative interest rates for a long time," said Xia Bin, an adviser to the People's Bank of China and an influential economist, in a lecture at a local university.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "However, the markets are quite fragile right now. If the government hikes interest rates, it wouldn't be easy for it to receive the understanding [from people]," said Xia. "Even if it wants to hike rates, the scale wouldn't be too big. So it wouldn't be effective for [managing] the real economy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Consumer Prices Rise 0.3%, Core CPI Flat \par US Household Debt, Net Worth Fall \par US Declares Deepwater Horizon Well Effectively Dead \par IMF Does Not Envision Ireland Will Need Financing \par China SAIC: Closely Watching Progress Of GM's Planned IPO \par Japan Govt Planning Supplementary Budget To Boost Economy-Minister \par PBOC Adviser: Floating Deposit Rates Better Than Small Rate Hike \par BOK Governor: Japan's Unilateral Move To Curb Yen Gains Not Effective \par China Vice Premier: To Boost Supply Of Low-Cost Housing--Report \par PBOC Survey: See Much Higher Expectation For Property Price Rises \par =WSJ: China Halts Talks With Japan in Growing Dispute \par Japan PM's Reshuffled Cabinet Receives High Support Rating -Kyodo \par Everbright Securities To Sell 12% JV Stake To Pramerica For CNY330M \par IRC Aims To Raise US$300M-US$400M From HK IPO - Source \par China Paper:\f28 \uc2 \f2 Sinochem\f28 \uc2 \f2  Has Asked Govt To Back \f28 \uc2 \f2 Potash Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bid-Reuters \par PBOC Adviser: China Won't Allow Yuan Appreciation Solely Due To Foreign Pressure \par Ex-PBOC Adviser: China Needs To Prevent Asset Bubbles -Report \par =WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sahara India Pariwar\f28 \uc2 \f2  In Talks To Buy Debt Of MGM -Source \par ANZ Completes Due Diligence For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korea Exchange Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Stake Bid-Report \par Afghan Elections Marred By Fraud Allegations, Low Turnout \par Investors Slash Anti-euro Bets By 60% \par US Commercial Real-estate Delinquencies Up \par US To Boost Ally Stake Before IPO \par Japan Pension Fund Mulls Investing In Emerging Markets \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0130  Australia    Reserve Bank of Australia Governor Glenn Stevens speech \par 0330  Thailand     Aug Trade data published this week \par 0800  Taiwan       Aug Export Orders \par 0830  UK           Aug BoE capital issuance \par 0830  UK           Aug BoE broad money provisional estimates \par 0830  UK           Sept BoE Trends in Lending report \par 0830  UK           Aug CML mortgage lending figures \par 1130  UK           Sept CML Market Commentary \par 1400  US           Sept NAHB Housing Market Index \par N/A   India        Jul CPI UNME (Urban non-manual employees) \par N/A   India        Aug CPI AL/RL (Agricultural/Rural Laborers) \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 19-09-10 2205GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100919e69j0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
