\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD - the view from Reuters Asia news editors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,273 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2010\par 00:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Following is the view from Reuters Asia editors on the news that is likely to matter most this week:\par \par \uc2 YEN IN THE SPOTLIGHT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to do something. Aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the yen <JPY=> was the quickest and arguably most effective way of throwing sand in the gears of the deflationary spiral gripping the local markets. Especially in light of the disappointment provoked by each new BOJ "easing". What's most interesting is that the deflation trade appears to have been driven primarily by local investors. The weekly and monthly capital flows data have not showed big foreign selling of Japanese shares, nor big buying of Japanese bonds. Even the positions built up in the yen do not seem all that big: yes on the IMM data speculative accounts were holding near record long positions, but in absolute terms those are still less than a third of the short positions built up during the height of the yen carry trade boom in 2007. So it's hard to say that Japanese authorities have broken the back of this vicious circle -- the strengthening yen undermining the Nikkei <.N225> and driving down JGB yields <JP10YTN=JBTC> -- just yet. Reports are emerging of a special budget for this fiscal year, and our sources have said the BOJ is considering boosting its rinban \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it next meets in early October. Will it be enough? If not, watch for more disappointment. Ichiro Ozawa's challenge to PM Naoto Kan seems to have energized the ruling DPJ leadership into understanding the need for bigger action. Yet it is too soon to think that Japan has moved beyond the failed half-measures that will do almost nothing to shake off the deflationary choke-hold.\par \par \uc2 It's worth noting that last Wednesday's estimated $20 billion of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounts to a mere 3.5 percent of daily average trade in dollar/yen. From that perspective, you have to deem the 3 percent squeeze up to near 86 yen fairly successful. But that was just the opening salvo in what could be a drawn out fight.\par \par \uc2 This week is a holiday one in Japan, with markets closed both Monday and Thursday. That could invite hedge funds to see if the MOF/BOJ is on holiday duty.\par \par \uc2 For an updated PDF package on the yen after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , click: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/fac44p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/fac44p}}}\par \par \uc2 Reuters Insider Asia weekahead: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/pav44p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/pav44p}}}\uc2  (eric.burroughs@thomsonreuters.com) > Five world markets themes this week [MKT/THEMES] > MARKETS WEEKAHEAD - Japan's gamechanger and Europe's test [ID:nLDE68G1LX]\par \par \uc2 CONFRONTATION BREWING AGAIN OVER YUAN\par \par \uc2 China and the United States are at loggerheads again over the level of the yuan, and with the U.S. Congress pressing for legislative action against Beijing if it fails to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise faster, there is the risk the row could escalate with damaging economic consequences for both sides. While it is in the interest of neither side to allow a trade war to erupt, U.S. legislators have an eye on November congressional elections and for domestic political reasons many want to be seen to be taking a tough line with China. U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, during a grilling by legislators demanding action, said he would rally the support of other world powers to pressure China and raise the issue at November's G20 summit in Seoul. Beijing's reaction has been predictably chilly -- the foreign ministry said pressure over the exchange rate "not only would fail to solve the problems; on the contrary, it could have the opposite effect". The renewed tension over the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes, interestingly, just as Japan launches heavy dollar buying to try to weaken the yen. The issue may well come up at this week's meeting between Premier Wen and President Obama at the United Nations General Assembly in New York but no one is expecting any major developments. The coming week is mostly holidays in China -- which means there won't be yuan midpoint settings, the daily window into China's strategy -- so the issue could go on hold for a bit.\par \par \uc2 The other potentially big story in China over the next week would be an interest rate increase. This rumour has swirled about for the past few days and taken a chunk out of the Chinese stock market in the process. Many analysts have dismissed it, because the economy is still on uncertain ground and the government has other, more targeted tools if it wants to cool the property market. Nevertheless, the rumours could have gotten their start from officials trying to lessen the surprise.\par \par \uc2 For a PDF package on China's yuan, click: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/cad44p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/cad44p}}}\uc2  (john.chalmers@thomsonreuters.com) > Five key Asia political risk themes to watch [ID:nSGE65501O] > Reuters polls and surveys [ID:nL10903477] > Top economic events [M/DIARY]\par \par \uc2 BHP'S $39 BLN BID FOR POTASH\par \par \uc2 In Asia, China's interest in thwarting BHP's bid remains a top story. In North America, U.S. and Canadian competition regulators are due to rule on BHP's $39 billion bid for \uc2 Potash Corp\uc2  next week. The deadlines are Sept. 20 for Canada's Competition Bureau and Sept. 24 for the U.S. Federal Trade Commission. BHP is confident neither agency is likely to oppose the bid, given that BHP currently has no potash production of its own. But both agencies may ask BHP for more information, which could take a couple of months. (michael.flaherty@thomsonreuters.com) > Company news diary [GLO/EQUITY] > Asia company earnings [ASIA/EQTY]\par \par \uc2 CHINA, JAPAN ROW\par \par \uc2 China suspended high-level exchanges with Japan on Sunday and promised tough countermeasures after a Japanese court extended the detention of a Chinese trawler captain who collided with two Japanese coastguard ships. The spat between Asia's two largest economies has flared since Japan arrested the captain, accusing him of deliberately striking a patrol ship and obstructing public officers near uninhabited islets in the East China Sea claimed by both sides. (john.chalmers@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 NEW ZEALAND ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GDP and current account data this Wednesday and Thursday. This is an economy whose \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has just sent out a very dovish message in that it expects only a 150 bps rise in rates in 2.5 years, which is grim even for an economy that has just had an earthquake coming on the heels of a drought, and where business sentiment, labour markets and consumer demand are weak. Quite reasonably, some analysts suspect the RBNZ is deliberately trying to force its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, because the kiwi was in any case not moving in sync with a deteriorating domestic picture. The RBNZ expects the earthquake to have a marginal negative impact on GDP this quarter, but it also expects the reconstruction efforts to offset that in coming months, which in sum still has the RBNZ pursuing a rather cautious and dovish policy path. We might get a record trade surplus despite a rise in imports, and increased profitability at foreign firms eating into that surplus and leading to a current account deficit, wider than the 21-month low in Q1. (vidya.ranganathan@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 ASIA POLITICAL RISKS\par \par \uc2 For a roundup of the key political risk themes to watch in Asia this week, plus links to related Reuters stories and country risk analysis, please click:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/jum59n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/jum59n}}}\uc2  (Editing by Dean Yates)\par \par \uc2 WEEKAHEAD/ASIA|LANGEN|Table|G|RBN|Z|J|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100919e69j0007k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
