\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ FOCUS: BOJ May Face Feeble Fund Opposition In Yen Sting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Martin  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 15:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan may be more successful in its efforts to weaken the yen than many market watchers assume, because the industry muscle to drag the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back up is weaker than it once was.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made a splash in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week, dumping yen on a massive scale to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 On previous occasions, other big players in the market would call the BOJ's bluff by buying yen, thereby driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back up.\par \par \uc2 But times have changed.\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds have suffered greatly in the market turmoil that has persisted since 2007, and many have shut down or shrunk. Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -dealing banks have slashed their own trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The result is that even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a whole has grown hugely over the past three years--to the point where it now sees $4 trillion in flows each day--the types of accounts that would typically seek to beat down a big \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aren't likely to be in fighting form.\par \par \uc2 "I just can't see the market taking the Bank of Japan on," said Thanos Papasavvas, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -fund manager at \uc2 Investec Asset Management\uc2  in London. "Who has the firepower?"\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds--often viewed as the market's most aggressive investors--are certainly smaller beasts than they used to be. Estimates from Chicago-based \uc2 Hedge Fund Research Inc\uc2 . show that from the peak in 2006, the scale of assets managed by hedge funds as a whole has shrunk by 12%, taking the combined total to $1.65 trillion. The number of individual hedge funds has declined by around 10% over that period, too.\par \par \uc2 That is still a large set of investors, and the proportion that focus on big macroeconomic trends remains large. Still, those that survive have also had a tough year.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, many suffered heavy losses when the fear of a second round of global recession started to bite, and many were caught off guard by unpredictable shifts in individual \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the surprising bounce in the euro since June.\par \par \uc2 "Hedge funds can't now afford to risk a large chunk of their relatively low returns for the year in taking on the BOJ," said Papasavvas.\par \par \uc2 Banks are the other most likely candidates for a market tussle. But while bank-to-bank trading has grown in recent years, the pace of its growth has clearly slowed in comparison with that of other market participants.\par \par \uc2 Overall daily trading flows grew by 20% from 2007 to hit $4 trillion a day, according to the Bank for International Settlements' most recent triennial report on the market, released late last month. The portion coming from banks, however, didn't keep pace with other types of accounts, making banks the second-biggest players in the market for the first time, behind non-dealing financial institutions such as investment firms and funds.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, bank flows may well slow from current levels, because new rules in the U.S. curtail banks' ability to engage in trading in the markets with their own funds, known as proprietary trading.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the most famous bank trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of all--at \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2 . (GS)--shut down. \uc2 JP Morgan Chase & Co\uc2 . (JPM) has also sent many of its traders home. Further closures of big-name proprietary desks are widely expected.\par \par \uc2 Banks rarely disclose any information about their in-house trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but market-watchers agree that bank prop desks have been shut or trimmed around the world in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Among the non-bank financial institutions that now dominate the market, many are the thousands of small retail traders or computer-driven accounts that seek to make returns from tiny short-term market shifts. These aren't the heavy-hitting thematic accounts that are likely or able to take on the BOJ.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it appears that no one is seriously trying. After the BOJ, acting on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets Wednesday, the yen has conspicuously failed to bounce back up. From its low point just under Y83 against the yen immediately before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has kept on climbing, hovering just under Y86 in European trading hours Friday.\par \par \uc2 That reflects, in part, the awesome scale of the BOJ's actions this week. Market insiders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold Y2 trillion ($24 billion) to push its sky-high \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, making this its biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on record, topping any single-day sting from its previous 15-month campaign that ended in early 2004.\par \par \uc2 Japan is acting without the help of other major economic bodies, like the U.S. Federal Reserve or the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which leads many observers to doubt that it can be successful. Still, an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of that size is tough to fight in the short term, even for traders who believe the yen should climb beyond its recent 15-year highs against the dollar. Moreover, the authorities have made it clear that they are prepared to act again if they judge it necessary.\par \par \uc2 "I don't understand the analysts who believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not work," said Lutz Karpowitz, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in Frankfurt.\par \par \uc2 "It has unlimited funds to do this, and it does not need to worry about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  creating inflation. It has a deflation problem, so the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are helping both on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  side and with the monetary base," he said.\par \par \uc2 One major source of yen buying remains, however: other central banks. It is tough to judge exactly how big a role they are playing, but official reserve managers are among the group of so-called non-bank financial institutions that the BIS says now dwarf dealing banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  business.\par \par \uc2 Their total assets under management have swelled from around $2 trillion in 2002 to more than $8 trillion now, according to market estimates based largely on data from the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 China is the biggest of them all, with around $2.4 trillion under management at the middle of this year, and earlier this month, it emerged that Beijing has started building up the share of its holdings denominated in yen.\par \par \uc2 That has already boosted the yen, and the Japanese authorities have said they are watching the move closely. Many market watchers believe the trend will continue.\par \par \uc2 "The [Japanese] authorities are not fighting speculative forces here," said Simon Derrick, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon in London. "We note the accelerated buying of Japanese government bonds by China through all of this year, particularly through May, June and July... Fighting this in the medium term is rather harder than simply taking on speculative players in the market."\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Clare Connaghan in London contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 17, 2010 09:39 ET (13:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100917e69h002v0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
