\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , No Questions Asked\b0\par\par\uc2 1,216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 14:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1204 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's aggressive move to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the yen this week grabbed the attention of investors, but on Capitol Hill during election season it might as well have not happened. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner spent more than four hours taking questions from a pair of congressional panels on Thursday, but somehow the the estimated $20 billion wave in yen sales never came up despite a pair of hearing focused on international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues.(michael.crittenden@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1203 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD and USD/JPY have come off their overnight highs at start of NY trading day, with Aug CPI data for US due at 8:30am ET. Investors will be looking for further clues on state of mixed US economy. Dollar broadly lower across most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with growth \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gaining from slight global risk-on sentiment following successful bill and bond auctions this week out of Euro zone. EUR/USD at 1.3075 from 1.3085 late Thu aftn, according to EBS via CQG. USD/JPY holding fast at 85.67 from 85.81, and continuing to move within extremely tight range after this week's massive Y2 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  gov't. GBP/USD at 1.5643 from 1.5635 late Thu.(andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1156 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds rise while the yield spread on 10-year Irish government bonds vs bunds widens to new high above 390 bps. Traders cite concerns that Ireland may have to seek external assistance from the IMF as it grapples with a huge budget deficit. Irish sovereign credit default swaps also underperform the better market Friday, widening 5 basis points on the day to 385/395 bps, according to data provider Markit. December bunds are up 0.42 at 129.80. 10-year Irish/bund yield spread widens 19 bps to 391.6 bps, a new high. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1155 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is well off its day's highs Friday, and a trader says fresh worries over Ireland are not helping the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Irish/German 10-yr spread is at a record wide 390.6 bps amid worries Ireland would have to seek outside help if economic conditions further deteriorate. EUR/USD now at 1.3070 from the day's one month highs of 1.3160. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1134 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades slightly higher at 24.667-24.697 from 24.65 late Thursday and up from 23-month low of 24.58 earlier this week. "The koruna is very calm today and trades back where it was before hitting its firmest level in months earlier in this week," Jan Vejmelek, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Komercni Banka\uc2  says. Vejmelek expects EUR/CZK to remain in its current range just under 24.700 later Friday and early next week when investors will wait for the September 23 rate-setting meeting of the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1125 GMT [Dow Jones] European corporate credit spreads tightened Friday ahead of U.S. inflation data, due 1230 GMT. The Markit iTraxx Crossover index, which tracks the cost of insuring a basket of 50 mostly sub-investment grade credits, is four basis points tighter, at 465/469 basis points, according to Markit. The iTraxx Europe index, which tracks the cost of insuring a basket of 125 investment-grade names against default, was one basis point tighter at 104/105 basis points. CPI figures are expected to show a 0.3% rise. At 1355 GMT the University of Michigan sentiment survey is released. The tightening is probably driven by technicals rather than data, a strategist said. "It is a very uncertain market, no-one knows which way to go." (irene.chapple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1114 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK trades sideways ahead of Sunday's elections in Sweden notes \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . PM Reinfeldt and his coalition are likely to win but there is the question of the far right. If they get more than 4% of the vote and enter parliament then Reinfeldt could lose his comfortable majority. This scenario could trigger a temporary blip in EUR/SEK which would offer a better selling opportunity. On the other hand if Reinfeldt wins comfortably, then it would be immediately positive for SEK. EUR/SEK now at 9.24. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds have crept higher as some buying comes in after yields rose to their highest in over a month. EUR has also pared gains, while in the peripherals space, yield spreads on Irish and Portuguese government bonds over bunds widen ahead of next week's supply. US CPI figures due at 1230 GMT, followed by University of Michigan survey at 1355 GMT. December bunds are up 0.13 at 129.51. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1100 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR hits 12-week low of 45.80 as selling pressure continues after RBI aggressively lifted key policy rates Thursday, which favors local unit given interest rate differential vs U.S., says state-run bank trader. Adds, rally in EUR on improving risk appetite also aiding INR. Pair at 46.85 vs 46.15 late Thursday; pair hit 45.57 on June 21. "The yield differentials are supporting the rupee after yesterday's rate hike." trader says. Tips pair in 45.80-46.00 band rest of session, 46.50-46.20 band next week. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1050 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro runs into a spat of profit taking in late European trading, and slips to session lows pretty much across the board. EUR/USD trades at 1.3093 from its one month high of 1.3160, EUR/CHF is down at 1.3313 from its one month high of 1.3392, and EUR/JPY is trading at 112.37 from the session's one month high of 112.98. (gary.stride@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1037 GMT [Dow Jones] India interest rate swaps tad higher as market holds view that post-RBI rate hike rally almost done, says trader with foreign bank; 1-year OIS at 6.37%/6.40% vs 6.36%/6.39% last close, 5-year OIS at 7.12%/7.16% vs 7.08%/7.11% last close. "The view seems to be that we're close to the end of tightening," says trader. But tight liquidity likely to support swaps.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1031 GMT [Dow Jones] Yield spreads on Irish and Portuguese government bonds are widening again ahead of supply next week, but Nomura reckons cheapening presents an entry opportunity in the 10-year Portuguese sector. Nomura says Portugal is arguably a better prospect than Ireland with better deficit figures and a lower trajectory of debt to GDP. "The country has completed about 84% of its 2010 issuance, this follows the government yesterday reiterating that it will hit the 7.3% budget deficit target." 10-year Portugal/bund yield spread 2.9bps wider at 349.5bps Ireland 8bps wider at 380.7bps. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com) [ 09-17-10 0804ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h0007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
