\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/TWD Lower Late; 31.70-31.90 Tipped Monday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 11:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.7290 late vs 31.7390 yesterday but off intraday low of 31.6750 as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected of buying, may have bought around US$16 million to curb TWD strength, traders say. "The New Taiwan dollar will continue to go up against the greenback, tracking the strength in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and strong economy in the region," says one trader. Another trader tips pair in 31.70-31.90 for Monday. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] As the euro keeps on climbing against the dollar, some funds are clearly growing nervous that a big drop could be around the corner. \uc2 GE Capital\uc2  has shut the buy side of its otherwise symmetrical EUR/USD algorithm Friday. GE cites the increased risk of an immediate downside risk following the strong upmove over the last few days for the move. It is looking for opportunities to sell EUR/USD in sizes of up to $500M. EUR/USD stands at $1.3153, at a one month high. (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities higher as sharp decline in spot pair over last few sessions fuels gamma bids, says trader with private bank; 1-month ATMF at 6.70%.7.10% vs 6.50%/7.20% late Thursday. "The market isn't really clear on direction, but given that its broken past what was a very sticky range, there's definitely going to be sharp movement," says trader. Notes, 1-week ATMF's, which had sink to bid of just 5% in last week now near trade at bid-ask midpoint of 6.5%. Adds, customer supply of risk reversals, particularly on back-end is helping hold back-end volatilities in check. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end higher after the state-run Shanghai Securities News reports there is very low chance of interest rate hike by China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in near term. Concerns about higher interest rates have risen recently after China's consumer price index rose 3.5% from a year earlier in August, biggest rise since October 2008. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index ends at 126.9 vs 126.76 last close. Still, trader at Shanghai-based local bank says bonds will face selling-pressure due to possible liquidity squeeze ahead of long holidays. Mid-Autumn Festival will take place Sep. 22-24, National Day holiday Oct. 1-7. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] Anlo Irish bondholders will be sweating it over the Irish opposition party's (Fine Gael) recent letter to the European Union asking for a limit on Irish taxpayers' exposure to Anglo Irish's escalating debts. "We see this letter as quite worrying for bondholders as it's a clear statement of intentions of the opposition party if it ends up forming the next government," say \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  banking analysts Ivan Zubo and Olivia Frieser. They reiterate their cautious stance on senior and lower tier 2 bonds, saying the bank's CDS curve could also steepen further given the increase in risk of unfavourable resolution for the bondholders should the opposition government prevail in the next parliament. (michael.wilson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0848 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP turns lower late, weighed by broad USD weakness vs regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with equity fund inflows supporting local unit. Pair at 44.21 in heavy volume vs 44.40 late Thursday. "Market fundamentals are working, there's no reason for the pair to rise," says local bank trader. Tips pair to test 44.00 support Monday; next resistance 44.50. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD index slide through its 200 DMA this week put it on track for a test of 81.00 notes \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2 . The relative ease with which that level was taken out overnight, combined with the total absence of any flight to quality bid suggests the index could carry on falling toward the August low of 80.08 says the bank. USD index now trades at 80.932 from the day's fresh one month low of 80.865. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR falls to 85.72 late vs 85.78 Thursday on export settlements; "Trade wasn't too active earlier in the day but sales by exporters led to a continued dip in the dollar towards the end of the session," says local dealer. Some inflows from remittances also supporting local unit. Dealer expects USD/PKR to trade in narrow band in near term; tips 85.70-85.75 range for Monday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japanese Series 13 indicated at 92.0/94.0 bps vs Thursday's close at 96.5/97.5 bps; players think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again as MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in week suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very serious about preventing sharp rises in JPY, supporting its key export sector, says trader at major Japanese bank. Says as index tightened only modestly after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday due to lingering concern weaker JPY could prove short-lived, effect of Tokyo's move seems to be lasting, so Friday's tightening "reflects a kind of delayed reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Index wasn't actually traded ahead of benchmark contract's rollover next week. Single-name CDS deals done Friday include \uc2 Aeon Co. Ltd\uc2 . (8267) at 102 bps vs 140 bps on Aug. 11, \uc2 Kobe Steel Ltd\uc2 . (5406) at 115 bps vs 97 bps on Sept. 6, \uc2 Itochu Corp\uc2 . (8001) at 80 bps vs 90 bps on Sept. 10, \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K. Lines Ltd\uc2 . (9104)at 63 bps vs 73 bps on Aug. 10. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.9% at 19,583.56 as institutions buy, while gains in regional markets lift sentiment, market participants say. Index hit 19,639.18 earlier, highest level since Jan. 2008; market breadth positive with 27 of the 30 index issues up. Index heavyweights \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY), +1.7% at INR1,017.30, Bharti (532454.BY), +1.8% at INR354.30 leading gains. "Liquidity, positive domestic corporate news flow and stable global markets are acting as strong triggers for the markets," says local dealer. \uc2 Kishor Ostwal\uc2  - head of domestic listed brokerage firm \uc2 CNI Research\uc2  says. Still, thinks markets look overbought; "a correction should set in next week." Tips Sensex in 18,600-19,800 band for rest of this month. ACC (500410.BY) +3% at INR995.25, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +2% at INR607. TCS (532540.BY) +1.9% at INR912.50, Sterlite (500900.BY) +1.7% at INR172.55. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones]--Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the ruble, but the wave of stop loss selling seems to have passed, says Anton Nikitin of Rennaisance Capital. "Still, the market is cautious and wary of strengthening," he says. Ruble is trading at 35.48 against the dollar-euro basket. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY turns lower as strong EUR, gains in most regional stocks support risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including CNY. On OTC, USD/CNY hits low at 6.7240 vs 6.7322 intraday high, 6.7248 last close; pair last at 6.7248. "The renewed yuan strength is all driven by the euro's move," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. "I think it's likely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to set the dollar-yuan central parity at fresh record low next week, though the magnitude of the decline will be small." Next support tipped at 6.7200; "exporters' demand to exchange dollars into yuan in the deliverable forwards market has been decreasing in the past 1-2 months, indicating their orders may not be that strong." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 09-17-10 0514ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h0006b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
