\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/PHP Turns Lower; May Test 44.00 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 10:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0848 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP turns lower late, weighed by broad USD weakness vs regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with equity fund inflows supporting local unit. Pair at 44.21 in heavy volume vs 44.40 late Thursday. "Market fundamentals are working, there's no reason for the pair to rise," says local bank trader. Tips pair to test 44.00 support Monday; next resistance 44.50. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD index slide through its 200 DMA this week put it on track for a test of 81.00 notes \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2 . The relative ease with which that level was taken out overnight, combined with the total absence of any flight to quality bid suggests the index could carry on falling toward the August low of 80.08 says the bank. USD index now trades at 80.932 from the day's fresh one month low of 80.865. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR falls to 85.72 late vs 85.78 Thursday on export settlements; "Trade wasn't too active earlier in the day but sales by exporters led to a continued dip in the dollar towards the end of the session," says local dealer. Some inflows from remittances also supporting local unit. Dealer expects USD/PKR to trade in narrow band in near term; tips 85.70-85.75 range for Monday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japanese Series 13 indicated at 92.0/94.0 bps vs Thursday's close at 96.5/97.5 bps; players think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again as MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in week suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very serious about preventing sharp rises in JPY, supporting its key export sector, says trader at major Japanese bank. Says as index tightened only modestly after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday due to lingering concern weaker JPY could prove short-lived, effect of Tokyo's move seems to be lasting, so Friday's tightening "reflects a kind of delayed reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Index wasn't actually traded ahead of benchmark contract's rollover next week. Single-name CDS deals done Friday include \uc2 Aeon Co. Ltd\uc2 . (8267) at 102 bps vs 140 bps on Aug. 11, \uc2 Kobe Steel Ltd\uc2 . (5406) at 115 bps vs 97 bps on Sept. 6, \uc2 Itochu Corp\uc2 . (8001) at 80 bps vs 90 bps on Sept. 10, \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K. Lines Ltd\uc2 . (9104)at 63 bps vs 73 bps on Aug. 10. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.9% at 19,583.56 as institutions buy, while gains in regional markets lift sentiment, market participants say. Index hit 19,639.18 earlier, highest level since Jan. 2008; market breadth positive with 27 of the 30 index issues up. Index heavyweights \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY), +1.7% at INR1,017.30, Bharti (532454.BY), +1.8% at INR354.30 leading gains. "Liquidity, positive domestic corporate news flow and stable global markets are acting as strong triggers for the markets," says local dealer. \uc2 Kishor Ostwal\uc2  - head of domestic listed brokerage firm \uc2 CNI Research\uc2  says. Still, thinks markets look overbought; "a correction should set in next week." Tips Sensex in 18,600-19,800 band for rest of this month. ACC (500410.BY) +3% at INR995.25, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +2% at INR607. TCS (532540.BY) +1.9% at INR912.50, Sterlite (500900.BY) +1.7% at INR172.55. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones]--Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the ruble, but the wave of stop loss selling seems to have passed, says Anton Nikitin of Rennaisance Capital. "Still, the market is cautious and wary of strengthening," he says. Ruble is trading at 35.48 against the dollar-euro basket. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY turns lower as strong EUR, gains in most regional stocks support risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including CNY. On OTC, USD/CNY hits low at 6.7240 vs 6.7322 intraday high, 6.7248 last close; pair last at 6.7248. "The renewed yuan strength is all driven by the euro's move," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. "I think it's likely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to set the dollar-yuan central parity at fresh record low next week, though the magnitude of the decline will be small." Next support tipped at 6.7200; "exporters' demand to exchange dollars into yuan in the deliverable forwards market has been decreasing in the past 1-2 months, indicating their orders may not be that strong." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe haven Treasurys are down in London as risk sentiment improves, with equities firm in Europe and the EUR higher across the board. Recent economic data in the US have also tempered worries over a slide back in to recession, easing talk of additional asset purchases by the Fed. Friday's data releases include CPI data at 1230 GMT and the University of Michigan consumer survey at 1355 GMT. The 2-year note is lower at 99-23/32 to yield 0.517%, the 10-year note is down at 98-17/32 to yield 2.795%. The 30-year bond is down at 98-26+/32, yielding 3.941%. December bunds are down 0.19 at 129.20. December gilts are