\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: AUD/JPY, AUD/USD Get Lift On PBOC Comments-Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,530 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 06:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] Accelerating AUD vs both USD, JPY partly due to PBOC comments in its annual financial stability report, says senior Sydney-based trader at major bank; "China came out with some comments saying they will keep policy loose and that's helping but it's not just the Aussie. The rally is also (in the) euro and just hard on the dollar." AUD/JPY reaches 4-month high, recently at 80.935. AUD/USD above 0.9406 for only 2nd time in two years, trading at 0.9440; move above 0.9458 would mark new 2-year high. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares +0.4% at 3209.15, largely boosted by 1.4% gain in Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) to NZ$8.45 as investors continue to expect company to benefit from earthquake reconstruction, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  broker Peter Sigley. NZ Refining Company (NZR.NZ) +7.4% at NZ$3.65 as market upbeat after company says throughput for July-August was 6.8 million barrels with refinery operating at full capacity; average gross refining margin was US$7.17 per barrel, processing fee was NZ$48 million. Company also says "refining margins have remained healthy over the period" but uncertainty remains. Volume, however, still tepid as lack of news, drivers has kept most investors on sidelines. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0453 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends losses, testing key 1,160 support vs 1,163.3 midday, 1,164.5 late yesterday in Seoul; stocks strong (Kospi +0.5%) while EUR/USD sharply higher in afternoon trade, lifting risk-sensitive KRW, traders say. "More export settlements and stop-loss sell orders by banks could lead to a breach of KRW1,160 (support). But the KRW1,158-KRW1,168 trading range still seems appropriate," as pair has not dropped out of that range this week, says local bank trader. Expects smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should pair fall sharply late. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 2Q GDP due next week looks like it could surprise and may come in around +0.6% on-quarter, well below RBNZ's forecast +0.9% says \uc2 Westpac\uc2 ; expects to see sharp impact from drought, though construction work should offset some of this. "With so many moving parts, there is plenty of scope for surprise in this quarter's GDP number. GDP forecasts normally come with a standard error of 0.2 percentage points, but we'd suggest the final number is unusually uncertain on this occasion." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures up 1.5% at 9570 vs morning close of 9510, aided by gains in Asian markets, say traders. "There is a sense of relief in the market that (U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy) Geithner didn't say things that imply criticism against \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (earlier this week)," says Japanese brokerage manager. Adds index's resistance is seen at 9600 today, but volume may stay thin as some investors stay sidelined before upcoming 3-day weekend. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Sentiment on Singapore bourse supported by Asia-wide advance, with small caps again driving volume. STI +0.3% at 3077.78 midday, expected to hold below 3100 for rest of session. "The STI's trend is still positive, but shorter-term price action shows a shrinking average range. A minor pullback would still be normal at this point given that broader markets are also consolidating," says Phillip Securities. Market breadth only slightly positive vs almost 2 gainers for every decliner in early trade. Volume above-average at 1.03 billion shares worth S$811.1 million, with 9 out of top 10 most active stocks under S$1.00, including \uc2 Equation Corp\uc2 . (532.SG), +8.3% at S$0.065, Genting Hong Kong (S21.SG), +1.0% at US$0.52, Biosensors (B20.SG), +14.3% at S$0.96, China Energy (A0G.SG), +16.1% at S$0.18, SunVic Chemical (A7S.SG), +2.4% at S$0.425. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.2% at 9620.47, extending morning session gains after Chinese shares entered positive territory. Analysts say investors seem relieved for now by lack of strong criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from top U.S. officials. "The consensus will likely be the continuation of low-interest rate policy" at U.S. FOMC's Tuesday meeting, says Hideo Arimura, senior fund manager at Mizuho Asset Management. Adds, "It's difficult to anticipate a continuous weakening of the yen from here." Puts next week's Nikkei index range at 9400-9800. 30/33 Topix subindexes higher; consumer lending sector top performer (+2.6%), with Credit Saison (8253.TO) up 7.3% at Y1,187 and Promise (8574.TO) up 4.0% at Y646. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) up 2.1% at Y12,190, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) up 2.2% at Y3,025. Canon (7751.TO) down 0.1% Y3,850, Sharp (6753.TO) off 1.8% at Y877 on profit-taking.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.1% at 21,925.67 midday, breaching early August high of 21,805, bolstered in part by expectations of more CNY gains, after PBOC sets USD/CNY central parity at new record low of 6.7172. Volume solid at HK$42.26 billion, 22,000 eyed next. "It's a confluence of factors including renminbi appreciation, funds switching from bonds to equities, expectations U.S. government may launch further measures to stimulate the economy, also quarter-ending window-dressing," says \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2  chief operating officer Ben Kwong. Blue chips' gains broad-based with 41 of 45 index members higher; those rising at least 3.0% include New World Development (0017.HK), Citic Pacific (0267.HK), \uc2 China Resources Enterprise\uc2  (0291.HK), Belle (1880.HK). (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI may hike rates by another 50 bps this fiscal year as it has maintained its hawkish rhetoric which hints that inflationary concerns continue to be on radar, says ICICI Bank. Adds, while RBI noted that process of rate normalisation is on, it is still incomplete. Says 50 bp rate hike forecast motivated by "the need to end the prevalence of negative real rates" and move closer to the neutral policy rate. Notes, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in its annual report in August, had indicated that the Taylor rule estimates the country's neutral rate to be around 6.5% for the repo rate, which also puts across a case for at least another 50 bp hike. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.7% at 4636.9, hits intraday high of 4643.3 in reaction to Aussie dollar strength, says institutional trader at major broker. AUD/USD last at 0.9433, while AUD/JPY up, last at JPY80.91. AUD/JPY has broken the June peak at JPY80.86, which corresponds to the 4550.3 peak on the S&P/ASX 200 Financials index. Given the strong correlation between AUD/JPY and Australian banks, this could trigger major short covering and buying of banks as traders anticipate fresh carry trades. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, reversing early-session gains, as investors take profit, sell recent outperformers like heavyweight \uc2 Manila Electric\uc2  (MER.PH). PSEi ends down 0.7% at 3979.35 in heavy volume. "The market is still bullish but we should go into a corrective phase," says Joseph Roxas, Eagle Equities president. Thinks some investors may continue to take profit next week; next support tipped at 3900, resistance at 4000. Among most actives, PLDT (TEL.PH), +0.2% at PHP2,524; First Gen (FGEN.PH), off 0.9% at PHP13.16; \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH), +7.7% at PHP14.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ$450 million government bond tender strongly bid as market looks for yield with continued pricing strength expected in secondary, says local bond trader. NZ Debt Management Office sold NZ$200 million of April 2015 bond at 4.60% vs pre-tender market yield of 4.635%, trading initially as low as 4.53% post tender; sold NZ$75 million of December 2017 at 5.07% vs pre-tender at 5.075%; NZ$150 million May 2021 bond at 5.32% vs pre-tender at 5.35%. Second trader says NZ government bonds garnering interest as govt bond yield trades above interest rate swap yields, allowing investors to buy government bonds, then paying for a floating interest rate swap, and pocket around 30 bp difference. Reckons it's offshore hedge funds buying to make gain, increasing interest in NZ bonds. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 09-17-10 0055ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
