\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Rise; Some Speculators Going Long\b0\par\par\uc2 1,419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 05:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 86.00 as short-term speculators begin piling into USD/JPY long positions in expectation that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken JPY further, says senior dealer at major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bank. "Speculative moves are coming back," this time, includes players going long dollar, dealer says. Additional gains unlikely, however, as Japan exporters will likely intensify USD sales as end-of-quarter approaches. Says pair may trade in 85.20-86.00 range if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities don't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in global day. Pair last 85.76. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura Securities says RBI likely to pause rest of FY, given expectation growth and inflation will moderate; "inflation has already started tapering off, and we expect a further decline in the coming months as input cost pressures ease." Unless commodity prices spike and put upward pressure on inflation, further rate hikes are unlikely this FY. Says RBI has signaled a shift in policy stance from "normalising mode to a data-dependent/neutral mode." (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0345 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Westpac\uc2  sells unit of NZD/USD at 0.7280, targeting 0.7000 stop loss around 0.7400 after RBNZ's bearish outlook, says strategist Robert Rennie. Says NZD has stayed high despite weakening fundamentals, but that should change. "We believe the RBNZ yesterday has forced a resolution of this divergence, by significantly downgrading its interest rate track for the current tightening cycle. NZ-U.S. interest rate differentials have narrowed further, and this should eventually take its toll on NZD/USD." NZD/USD last at 0.7280 (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones]Indonesia shares up 0.7% at 3,365.096 in moderate volume around IDR1.5 trillion as investors bargain-hunt for select commodity, banks, consumer-related stocks, traders say; but warn profit-taking might happen later in day. \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) +1.5% at IDR56,150; plantation firm Astra Agro (AALI.JK) +0.7% at IDR20,850; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +2.3% at IDR6,800; Bank Central Asia (BBCA.JK) 1.6% at IDR6,450; \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UNVR.JK) +0.9% at IDR16,700; miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK) +0.5% at IDR19,000; cement firm \uc2 Semen Gresik\uc2  (SMGR.JK) +0.6% at IDR9,200. Index now tipped in 3,290-3,390 band. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI's pace of hiking will slow going forward, but use of wording like "unacceptably high" referred to current and expected inflation, while pointing out target to "contain inflation" and "anchor expectations" and the "need to move" real rates higher all seem clear indication that hiking is not over yet, says Cristian Maggio at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . Expects RBI to hike repo rate by 25 bps to 6.25% at November meeting, with another 50bp hike in 1H2011; for reverse repo, change of liquidity pattern and short history of the series pose a risk to the forecast. "Although the RBI has pursued a compression of the spread to the repo rate during the 2010 hiking cycle, we remain sceptical this compression will advance significantly going forward," says house. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude up in electronic trading in response to stronger EUR/USD, after 1.9% loss overnight in New York. Price likely to fluctuate around current levels, continue to take cues from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , equity movement on lack of news, analysts say. Tatsufumi Okoshi, economist with Nomura Securities says prices also likely to remain steady in longer term on likely large spare supply capacity and lukewarm economic recovery in developed world; adds "there's no threat of supply disruptions." October Nymex crude last +21 cents at $74.78/bbl on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD pushing back up near 2-year highs today as dovish RBNZ yesterday has traders rotating out of NZD and into AUD, says Gareth Berry, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategy for \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "International investors that had been dividing resources between Aussie and Kiwi are returning to Aussie because it's a better bet right now," says Berry; though adds, some caution on move. "I'm nervous at these levels but I think we'll go a little higher because the RBA will be hiking at some point before Christmas." AUD/USD recently at 0.9413 vs 0.9352 at morning high and back above 0.9406 for second time this week, just second time in 2 years. Berry tips 0.9500 as resistance, saying any move above that could just "get tired." (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI just breaches 21,800, if index able to stay above this level in short term, would likely to be taken as confirmation of breach of HK market's downtrend since November 2009, as such breach would reverse pattern that each rebound's peak is lower than previous peak, would likely boost sentiment. In Nov. 18, 2009, HSI peaked at 23,099, highest post-global financial crisis, then rebounded to 22,671 in January this year, followed by next rebound high of 22,388 in April, then 21,805 in early August, with each rebound's high lower than previous rebound's high. HSI last +0.8% at 21,870.02. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +1.4% at 455.78, on continued buying on 3Q earnings hopes after some blue chips reported solid results for August. "Sentiment is also rising as players are expecting the government to announce new financial measures, including lower interest rates, from October, to support companies in the fourth quarter, because most companies have faced high borrowing costs this year," says trader. Volume average with 39 million shares valued at VND980 billion traded so far. Trader tips key index resistance at 458. Among advancers, \uc2 Petrosetco\uc2  (PET.VH) +4.9% at VND19,300, REE (REE.VH) last +3.6% at VND17,200, Sudico (SJS.VH) +3.9% at VND66,500. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0328 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered says RBI's combined assessment of inflation, growth and real interest rates does not indicate bias towards immediate pause in monetary policy tightening. Expects RBI to hike both repo, reverse repo rates by 25bps again at its November policy meeting. However adds, subsequent policy actions are likely to become more data-driven. "We are looking for another 25 basis point rate hike in the fourth-quarter of FY11, but there is a possibility that the RBI will pause by then if inflation slows more sharply than anticipated." Expects OIS curve to bearish flatten, albeit gradually. Also, continued supply to exert upward pressure on the long end of government bond curve (INR1.7 trillion 2H FY borrowing slated), keeping yields at elevated levels. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bond higher on increasing view BOT may halt rate hike at upcoming policy meeting. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says strong THB a factor at October rate meeting. "We see some interbank players cashing out of long-term notes and buying bonds in the front end, which should cause yield curve to steepen" says trader. Eyes on annual market dialogue between MOF, bond market participants this evening for details of next FY supply. MOF official says overall supply initially set at THB500 billion, including some 50-year bonds, vs THB446 billion this FY, which market generally view as not surprising. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.77%/2.74% vs 2.77%/2.72% late yesterday, November 2016 quoted at 2.87%/2.82% vs 2.92%/2.87%, June 2019 quoted at 2.97%/2.94% vs 3.00%/2.95%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP lower, tracking broad USD weakness vs EUR, most regional units; pair last at 44.24 vs 44.40 late Thursday; initial support tipped at 44.20 then 44.00, resistance at 44.50. Volume heavy with some banks unwinding long positions. "News flow favors risk appetite, and that's helping the peso. Just look at the euro," says Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 2355ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h0002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
