\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Singapore Market Outlook Dims In 2H10 -Deutsche\b0\par\par\uc2 1,721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 02:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] Sharp deceleration in Singapore's GDP growth, corporate earnings in 2H10, plus less favorable government policy likely to weigh on Singapore stock market near term, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . Notes while 1H10 GDP growth beat expectations, 3Q10 performance likely to decline; "after a surge in industrial production, inventory accumulation accounted for 25% of GDP growth last quarter. We expect production to undershoot exports in 3Q as inventories unwind, leading to negative GDP growth." Expects earnings growth momentum to slow, with estimated average 13% on-year increase in 2H10 vs +40% in 1H10. Says recent government housing-market curbs, plan to slow immigration growth will also take toll on shares. Prefers defensive approach to investing as STI nearing its 3200 target for this year. Favors \uc2 Fraser & Neave\uc2  (F99.SG), \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG), SGX (S68.SG), \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG), \uc2 Singapore Airport Terminal Services\uc2  (S58.SG). STI closed down 0.1% at 3067.11. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, helped by modest gains in U.S. stocks in last 2 sessions while local market was closed for national holiday yesterday. KLCI ended 0.1% lower at 1474.44 Wednesday. "The pullback on Wednesday was quite shallow and this shows that sentiment remains bullish and market momentum is still positive," dealer says; tips KLCI to move in 1470-1480 range today with positive bias, though trading activity likely subdued amid dearth of catalysts. Major exporters such as rubber glove maker Top Glove (7113.KU) may rebound from recent declines on relief over USD/MYR, which off 13-year low. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may rise after state-run Shanghai Securities News reports, citing unnamed sources, there's very low chance of PBOC interest rate hike near term. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to trade in 2600-2700 range, after ending down 1.9% at 2602.47 Thursday, more than 3-week low. "The prevailing mood is likely to be one of caution, as investors lock in profits ahead of the long holidays starting next week," says Li Xiaoxuan at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. China Mid-Autumn Festival national holidays September 22-24, Golden Week holidays October 1-7. Banks likely to continue paring losses after CBRC Thursday afternoon denied media reports it was planning sharply higher CAR requirement for major banks. Shenzhen index down 2.2% at 1156.51. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 last +0.1% at 3199.18 as stocks continue to gain, index trading near four-and-a-half month high, on improved sentiment, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke; "There seems to be a little bit less selling here even though (index) is at the top of the trading range." Adds, there seems to be a change in sentiment, for the better as traders feel prices are set to go higher, despite the earthquake damage, with consumers feeling more secure over current economic conditions. Says weaker NZD providing some support for export stocks; Cavalier (CAV.NZ) +0.3% at NZ$2.92, Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) +0.5% at NZ$1.85. Adds, Fisher & Paykel Appliance (FPA.NZ) +1.6% at NZ$0.62 on ongoing strength due to announcement Wednesday had developed new compressor technology for refrigeration. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0023 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR tad lower at 3.1145 vs 3.1160 late Wednesday (Malaysia markets closed yesterday for national holiday); dealers say pair likely to remain in narrow range near-term as market participants hesitate amid fears over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  action by regional central banks. "Players are adopting a wait-and-see approach for now as it appears that any attempt to push up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the region will be countered by the central banks," dealer says; tips pair in 3.1000-3.1250 range near-term. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0020 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW slips to 1,163.4 vs 1,164.5 late yesterday amid Kospi's mild 0.3% gain; traders put firm support at 1,160, expect quiet session before Chuseok holiday Sept. 21-Sept. 23. In longer term, KRW likely to rise on back of country's economic recovery but "just for day trading, players could have a go at making dollar-long bets" on any USD dips, says local bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0015 GMT [Dow Jones] After surging earlier this week on Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's major auto and electronics shares higher today with USD/JPY remaining solid (now 85.80); still stocks' rally may be limited today, says Tachibana Securities operating officer Kenichi Hirano. Sentiment would be more bullish had USD/JPY risen further (above 86), but with yen-weakening momentum slowing, traders likely to sit tight for now, he says. "There are no domestic trading cues, and it's kind of bad timing with a major holiday week coming up." Japanese market closed next Monday and Thursday for national holidays. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 0.6% at Y2,658. Canon (7751.TO) up 0.3% at Y3,865. Nintendo (7974.OK) up 0.5% at Y23,870. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 0.5% at Y3,075, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) up 1.4% at Y3,000. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures tad down as Tokyo shares higher (Nikkei now up 0.8%), may extend declines to 142.07, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Adds U.S. Treasury yields higher overnight, which could prompt some players to sell JGBs. Tokyo traders paying attention to whether government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again, BOJ indicates it's leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized via \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . JGB futures down 0.01 at 142.25; 10-year JGB yield +1.5 bps at 1.055%. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0009 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may be choppy given Wall Street's flat performance overnight, with investors possibly even holding out because of Friday's 'triple witching' in U.S., European markets, where stock index futures, stock index options, stock options all expire on same day. "The simultaneous expirations generally increase the trading volume of options, futures and the underlying stocks, and occasionally increase volatility of prices of related securities," says CIMB. Resistance for STI, which closed down 0.1% at 3067.11 yesterday, expected at 3100, with support at August 3043 peak. With corporate newsflow thin, attention may again fall on small caps, penny stocks, which have been active lately given increasing reservation toward blue chips with STI now at more than 2-year highs. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals currently consolidating in relatively subdued trade, with mixed data from U.S. overnight failing to push complex strongly in either direction, though weaker USD provided some price support, says major market participant who requests anonymity; added in daily note that with holidays in Beijing and Tokyo, next week should be just as quiet as this week. Triland Metals, meanwhile, noting that workers at Codelco's Radomiro Tomic mine rejected an early wage proposal, says this left "a potential cloud over supply stability at the world's top copper producer;" adds, aluminum appears prone to a downside move, particularly if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support is taken away. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  didn't mince words overnight; despite economic noise, one trend "startlingly clear" is the sustained strong growth in base metals demand; says this evidenced by counter-seasonal decline in reported inventories for a number of base metals, is especially impressive given that it occurred during a period that is usually seasonally weak. Copper stocks, for instance, fell during northern hemisphere summer for first time in six years, and destocking has left consumers with lower inventory levels as they enter a seasonally stronger demand period, house says. LME 3-month copper rose $85 yesterday to $7,700/metric ton, aluminum edged $12 higher at $2,165, zinc firmed $3 at $2,148.00, nickel climbed $50 to $23,250, and tin extended its rally, jumping $345 to $23,495, a 52% gain from June 7. Lead bucked yesterday's trend losing $33 to $2,202. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 looks vulnerable to further profit taking before the weekend, with a fall toward former "inverse head & shoulders neckline" resistance at 4590 (weekly charts) possible after an early bounce toward the 200-day moving average at 4628, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. More likely, the index will stay within those levels today but it's likely to fade in the afternoon, particularly if China's equity market remains on the backfoot. IG Markets tips 0.4% opening rise to 4625, with an afterhours surge in \uc2 Oracle\uc2  and RIM lifting U.S. index futures 0.2% in early trading. However, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is more of a leading indicator for the economy and its disappointing earnings guidance last night should encourage further profit taking on upticks today, as should the disappointing Philadelphia Fed manufacturing survey. Although traders have been turning bullish after the recent break above range resistance at 4622, the break is not set in concrete. Also, while the inverse head & shoulders bottom targets 5025 while the index remains above the neckline, it's interesting to note that Shanghai has been unable to stay above equivalent neckline support. Index last 4605.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely rangebound amid dearth of fresh leads; market participants awaiting auction of 3-year Islamic bonds, likely for amount of MYR3 billion, for clearer direction, dealers say; auction announcement from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected next week. Near-term, MGSs likely to take cue from MYR direction, dealers say. Markets closed yesterday for national holiday. Wednesday, yields on benchmark MGSs ended mixed, with May 2013 +2 bps at 3.19%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.31%, September 2017 down 2 bps at 3.56%, November 2019 down 1 bp at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 2039ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
