eriod that is usually seasonally weak. Copper stocks, for instance, fell during northern hemisphere summer for first time in six years, and destocking has left consumers with lower inventory levels as they enter a seasonally stronger demand period, house says. LME 3-month copper rose $85 yesterday to $7,700/metric ton, aluminum edged $12 higher at $2,165, zinc firmed $3 at $2,148.00, nickel climbed $50 to $23,250, and tin extended its rally, jumping $345 to $23,495, a 52% gain from June 7. Lead bucked yesterday's trend losing $33 to $2,202. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 looks vulnerable to further profit taking before the weekend, with a fall toward former "inverse head & shoulders neckline" resistance at 4590 (weekly charts) possible after an early bounce toward the 200-day moving average at 4628, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. More likely, the index will stay within those levels today but it's likely to fade in the afternoon, particularly if China's equity market remains on the backfoot. IG Markets tips 0.4% opening rise to 4625, with an afterhours surge in \uc2 Oracle\uc2  and RIM lifting U.S. index futures 0.2% in early trading. However, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is more of a leading indicator for the economy and its disappointing earnings guidance last night should encourage further profit taking on upticks today, as should the disappointing Philadelphia Fed manufacturing survey. Although traders have been turning bullish after the recent break above range resistance at 4622, the break is not set in concrete. Also, while the inverse head & shoulders bottom targets 5025 while the index remains above the neckline, it's interesting to note that Shanghai has been unable to stay above equivalent neckline support. Index last 4605.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely rangebound amid dearth of fresh leads; market participants awaiting auction of 3-year Islamic bonds, likely for amount of MYR3 billion, for clearer direction, dealers say; auction announcement from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected next week. Near-term, MGSs likely to take cue from MYR direction, dealers say. Markets closed yesterday for national holiday. Wednesday, yields on benchmark MGSs ended mixed, with May 2013 +2 bps at 3.19%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.31%, September 2017 down 2 bps at 3.56%, November 2019 down 1 bp at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,164.5 late yesterday in Seoul on exporters' USD selling ahead of national holiday next week, and on anticipated foreign equity fund inflows. But traders expect firm support near 1,160 on corporate USD demand, possible USD buying related to recent M&A deals, chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb KRW gains. "I expect a rather quiet session today, with trade largely focused on the market's actual dollar supply and demand conditions," says local bank trader. Another trader tips pair in tight 1,159-1,165 range for today. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +26.3% at HK$4.538 billion Thursday, 6.5% of market total, vs 5.5% Wednesday, suggests HSI may consolidate further (down 0.2% yesterday) near-term after its recent rally (+5.6% month-to-yesterday). A50 (2823.HK) leads with HK$402.74 million, then \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (3968.HK) with HK$217.39 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$197.09 million, China National Building Material (3323.HK) with HK$160.65 million and China Citic Bank (0998.HK) with HK$144.10 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] Autumn may represent "prime buying opportunity" for Japanese stocks, says Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Says if yen appreciates against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on back of concerns over prospects for the US economy, additional monetary easing by the Fed, and fears regarding euro/European financial system, expects selling pressure on Japanese equities to increase. "At the same time though, Japanese companies have strengthened their earnings mixes by becoming more international and tightening cost controls, likely making them more resistant to headwinds." Notes if Japanese stocks pull back further, average P/B (currently around 1X) could drop to historically low 0.9X. Assuming a further sell-off, adds investor interest in Japanese equities will likely shift to high-yield plays as safe havens. "On a long-term horizon though, we see a buying opportunity for companies with high overseas sales weightings active in Asia," says house. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 2026ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
