e slips, but consumers surprisingly resilient despite massive earthquake, collapse of South Canterbury Finance, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  chief economist Brendan O'Donovan. Index falls to 114.1 points from 119.3 points in 2Q. Says fall in confidence, sharp drop in short-term economic outlook "implies that growth in consumer spending will remain modest in the near term." Spending hampered by weak income growth but says once situation improves "it is hard to believe consumers will not act." Adds survey challenges RBNZ's view of subdued consumer spending as index still above long run average of 111.6, broadly consistent with annual average growth in real consumer spending of around 3.0%. Notes RBNZ revised down forecasts for real consumer spending at yesterday's monetary policy review, with growth tipped to slow from 2.3% in the year to March 2011 to just over 0.5% in March 2012. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 2023ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
