\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Exporter Stocks Up But Rise Likely Limited\b0\par\par\uc2 1,782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 02:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0015 GMT [Dow Jones] After surging earlier this week on Tokyo's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's major auto and electronics shares higher today with USD/JPY remaining solid (now 85.80); still stocks' rally may be limited today, says Tachibana Securities operating officer Kenichi Hirano. Sentiment would be more bullish had USD/JPY risen further (above 86), but with yen-weakening momentum slowing, traders likely to sit tight for now, he says. "There are no domestic trading cues, and it's kind of bad timing with a major holiday week coming up." Japanese market closed next Monday and Thursday for national holidays. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  (6758.TO) up 0.6% at Y2,658. Canon (7751.TO) up 0.3% at Y3,865. Nintendo (7974.OK) up 0.5% at Y23,870. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7203.TO) up 0.5% at Y3,075, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7267.TO) up 1.4% at Y3,000. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures tad down as Tokyo shares higher (Nikkei now up 0.8%), may extend declines to 142.07, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Adds U.S. Treasury yields higher overnight, which could prompt some players to sell JGBs. Tokyo traders paying attention to whether government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again, BOJ indicates it's leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized via \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . JGB futures down 0.01 at 142.25; 10-year JGB yield +1.5 bps at 1.055%. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0009 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may be choppy given Wall Street's flat performance overnight, with investors possibly even holding out because of Friday's 'triple witching' in U.S., European markets, where stock index futures, stock index options, stock options all expire on same day. "The simultaneous expirations generally increase the trading volume of options, futures and the underlying stocks, and occasionally increase volatility of prices of related securities," says CIMB. Resistance for STI, which closed down 0.1% at 3067.11 yesterday, expected at 3100, with support at August 3043 peak. With corporate newsflow thin, attention may again fall on small caps, penny stocks, which have been active lately given increasing reservation toward blue chips with STI now at more than 2-year highs. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals currently consolidating in relatively subdued trade, with mixed data from U.S. overnight failing to push complex strongly in either direction, though weaker USD provided some price support, says major market participant who requests anonymity; added in daily note that with holidays in Beijing and Tokyo, next week should be just as quiet as this week. Triland Metals, meanwhile, noting that workers at Codelco's Radomiro Tomic mine rejected an early wage proposal, says this left "a potential cloud over supply stability at the world's top copper producer;" adds, aluminum appears prone to a downside move, particularly if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support is taken away. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  didn't mince words overnight; despite economic noise, one trend "startlingly clear" is the sustained strong growth in base metals demand; says this evidenced by counter-seasonal decline in reported inventories for a number of base metals, is especially impressive given that it occurred during a period that is usually seasonally weak. Copper stocks, for instance, fell during northern hemisphere summer for first time in six years, and destocking has left consumers with lower inventory levels as they enter a seasonally stronger demand period, house says. LME 3-month copper rose $85 yesterday to $7,700/metric ton, aluminum edged $12 higher at $2,165, zinc firmed $3 at $2,148.00, nickel climbed $50 to $23,250, and tin extended its rally, jumping $345 to $23,495, a 52% gain from June 7. Lead bucked yesterday's trend losing $33 to $2,202. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 looks vulnerable to further profit taking before the weekend, with a fall toward former "inverse head & shoulders neckline" resistance at 4590 (weekly charts) possible after an early bounce toward the 200-day moving average at 4628, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. More likely, the index will stay within those levels today but it's likely to fade in the afternoon, particularly if China's equity market remains on the backfoot. IG Markets tips 0.4% opening rise to 4625, with an afterhours surge in \uc2 Oracle\uc2  and RIM lifting U.S. index futures 0.2% in early trading. However, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is more of a leading indicator for the economy and its disappointing earnings guidance last night should encourage further profit taking on upticks today, as should the disappointing Philadelphia Fed manufacturing survey. Although traders have been turning bullish after the recent break above range resistance at 4622, the break is not set in concrete. Also, while the inverse head & shoulders bottom targets 5025 while the index remains above the neckline, it's interesting to note that Shanghai has been unable to stay above equivalent neckline support. Index last 4605.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely rangebound amid dearth of fresh leads; market participants awaiting auction of 3-year Islamic bonds, likely for amount of MYR3 billion, for clearer direction, dealers say; auction announcement from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected next week. Near-term, MGSs likely to take cue from MYR direction, dealers say. Markets closed yesterday for national holiday. Wednesday, yields on benchmark MGSs ended mixed, with May 2013 +2 bps at 3.19%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.31%, September 2017 down 2 bps at 3.56%, November 2019 down 1 bp at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,164.5 late yesterday in Seoul on exporters' USD selling ahead of national holiday next week, and on anticipated foreign equity fund inflows. But traders expect firm support near 1,160 on corporate USD demand, possible USD buying related to recent M&A deals, chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb KRW gains. "I expect a rather quiet session today, with trade largely focused on the market's actual dollar supply and demand conditions," says local bank trader. Another trader tips pair in tight 1,159-1,165 range for today. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +26.3% at HK$4.538 billion Thursday, 6.5% of market total, vs 5.5% Wednesday, suggests HSI may consolidate further (down 0.2% yesterday) near-term after its recent rally (+5.6% month-to-yesterday). A50 (2823.HK) leads with HK$402.74 million, then \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (3968.HK) with HK$217.39 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$197.09 million, China National Building Material (3323.HK) with HK$160.65 million and China Citic Bank (0998.HK) with HK$144.10 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] Autumn may represent "prime buying opportunity" for Japanese sto