\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Lead JGB Futures May Fall To 142.07 - Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 02:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures tad down as Tokyo shares higher (Nikkei now up 0.8%), may extend declines to 142.07, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Adds U.S. Treasury yields higher overnight, which could prompt some players to sell JGBs. Tokyo traders paying attention to whether government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again, BOJ indicates it's leaving \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unsterilized via \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . JGB futures down 0.01 at 142.25; 10-year JGB yield +1.5 bps at 1.055%. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0009 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may be choppy given Wall Street's flat performance overnight, with investors possibly even holding out because of Friday's 'triple witching' in U.S., European markets, where stock index futures, stock index options, stock options all expire on same day. "The simultaneous expirations generally increase the trading volume of options, futures and the underlying stocks, and occasionally increase volatility of prices of related securities," says CIMB. Resistance for STI, which closed down 0.1% at 3067.11 yesterday, expected at 3100, with support at August 3043 peak. With corporate newsflow thin, attention may again fall on small caps, penny stocks, which have been active lately given increasing reservation toward blue chips with STI now at more than 2-year highs. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals currently consolidating in relatively subdued trade, with mixed data from U.S. overnight failing to push complex strongly in either direction, though weaker USD provided some price support, says major market participant who requests anonymity; added in daily note that with holidays in Beijing and Tokyo, next week should be just as quiet as this week. Triland Metals, meanwhile, noting that workers at Codelco's Radomiro Tomic mine rejected an early wage proposal, says this left "a potential cloud over supply stability at the world's top copper producer;" adds, aluminum appears prone to a downside move, particularly if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support is taken away. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  didn't mince words overnight; despite economic noise, one trend "startlingly clear" is the sustained strong growth in base metals demand; says this evidenced by counter-seasonal decline in reported inventories for a number of base metals, is especially impressive given that it occurred during a period that is usually seasonally weak. Copper stocks, for instance, fell during northern hemisphere summer for first time in six years, and destocking has left consumers with lower inventory levels as they enter a seasonally stronger demand period, house says. LME 3-month copper rose $85 yesterday to $7,700/metric ton, aluminum edged $12 higher at $2,165, zinc firmed $3 at $2,148.00, nickel climbed $50 to $23,250, and tin extended its rally, jumping $345 to $23,495, a 52% gain from June 7. Lead bucked yesterday's trend losing $33 to $2,202. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 looks vulnerable to further profit taking before the weekend, with a fall toward former "inverse head & shoulders neckline" resistance at 4590 (weekly charts) possible after an early bounce toward the 200-day moving average at 4628, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. More likely, the index will stay within those levels today but it's likely to fade in the afternoon, particularly if China's equity market remains on the backfoot. IG Markets tips 0.4% opening rise to 4625, with an afterhours surge in \uc2 Oracle\uc2  and RIM lifting U.S. index futures 0.2% in early trading. However, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is more of a leading indicator for the economy and its disappointing earnings guidance last night should encourage further profit taking on upticks today, as should the disappointing Philadelphia Fed manufacturing survey. Although traders have been turning bullish after the recent break above range resistance at 4622, the break is not set in concrete. Also, while the inverse head & shoulders bottom targets 5025 while the index remains above the neckline, it's interesting to note that Shanghai has been unable to stay above equivalent neckline support. Index last 4605.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely rangebound amid dearth of fresh leads; market participants awaiting auction of 3-year Islamic bonds, likely for amount of MYR3 billion, for clearer direction, dealers say; auction announcement from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected next week. Near-term, MGSs likely to take cue from MYR direction, dealers say. Markets closed yesterday for national holiday. Wednesday, yields on benchmark MGSs ended mixed, with May 2013 +2 bps at 3.19%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.31%, September 2017 down 2 bps at 3.56%, November 2019 down 1 bp at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,164.5 late yesterday in Seoul on exporters' USD selling ahead of national holiday next week, and on anticipated foreign equity fund inflows. But traders expect firm support near 1,160 on corporate USD demand, possible USD buying related to recent M&A deals, chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb KRW gains. "I expect a rather quiet session today, with trade largely focused on the market's actual dollar supply and demand conditions," says local bank trader. Another trader tips pair in tight 1,159-1,165 range for today. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +26.3% at HK$4.538 billion Thursday, 6.5% of market total, vs 5.5% Wednesday, suggests HSI may consolidate further (down 0.2% yesterday) near-term after its recent rally (+5.6% month-to-yesterday). A50 (2823.HK) leads with HK$402.74 million, then \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (3968.HK) with HK$217.39 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$197.09 million, China National Building Material (3323.HK) with HK$160.65 million and China Citic Bank (0998.HK) with HK$144.10 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] Autumn may represent "prime buying opportunity" for Japanese stocks, says Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Says if yen appreciates against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on back of concerns over prospects for the US economy, additional monetary easing by the Fed, and fears regarding euro/European financial system, expects selling pressure on Japanese equities to increase. "At the same time though, Japanese companies have strengthened their earnings mixes by becoming more international and tightening cost controls, likely making them more resistant to headwinds." Notes if Japanese stocks pull back further, average P/B (currently around 1X) could drop to historically low 0.9X. Assuming a further sell-off, adds investor interest in Japanese equities will likely shift to high-yield plays as safe havens. "On a long-term horizon though, we see a buying opportunity for companies with high overseas sales weightings active in Asia," says house. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2340 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 3Q consumer confidence slips, but consumers surprisingly resilient despite massive earthquake, collapse of South Canterbury Finance, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  chief economist Brendan O'Donovan. Index falls to 114.1 points from 119.3 points in 2Q. Says fall in confidence, sharp drop in short-term economic outlook "implies that growth in consumer spending will remain modest in the near term." Spending hampered by weak income growth but says once situation improves "it is hard to believe consumers will not act." Adds survey challenges RBNZ's view of subdued consumer spending as index still above long run average of 111.6, broadly consistent with annual average growth in real consumer spending of around 3.0%. Notes RBNZ revised down forecasts for real consumer spending at yesterday's monetary policy review, with growth tipped to slow from 2.3% in the year to March 2011 to just over 0.5% in March 2012. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may get a mild lift from Wall Street's rise but index likely choppy in tight range before long Korean thanksgiving holiday next week. "Since no event is expected and Korea is entering into a long holiday (from Tuesday), the market is likely to remain sluggish for a while," says \uc2 Hana Daetoo Securities\uc2 ' Suh Dong-pill; "expectations for third-quarter corporate earning results will start arising some time at the end of this month at the earliest." Kospi tipped in 1810-1825 range after ending down 0.7% at 1811.85; techs may get a small boost from gains in U.S. peers, analysts say. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude set to consolidate with downside bias after settling $1.45 lower at $74.57/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; positive MACD histogram bars contracting, pointing to downside risk. Support at $74.11 (yesterday's low); breach would target $73.88 (Sept. 9 low), then $73.37 (Sept. 8 low), $72.63 (Sept. 7 low) and $71.53 (Aug. 31 reaction low). Resistance at $75.54 (hourly chart); break would temper near-term negative outlook, targeting $76.21 (previous base set Tuesday), then $78.04 (1-month high when hit Monday), a break of which would open way to $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 Aug. 4-Aug. 25 decline), then $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). October crude now off 2 cents at $74.55 on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 2014ET ]\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may raise to 86.50 as players still unwilling to pile into short positions so soon after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says senior dealer at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  trust bank. "Fear (of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) remains in the markets," dealer says. Says upside limited, however, due to Japan exporters' USD sales orders lined up in 86.00-86.50 range. Traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows big exporters to sell USD at reasonably attractive levels. Pair may trade in 85.20-86.50 range vs 85.86 last. EUR/USD may rise to 1.3150 as Europe sovereign risk fades while U.S. Treasury yields still low, making EUR relatively more attractive. EUR/USD may trade in 1.3030-1.3150 range vs 1.3076 last. EUR/JPY biased up; may trade in 111.80-113.00 band vs 112.26. Markets focused on Japan authorities' next actions. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 2014ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100917e69h00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
