\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro, Sterling And The Aussie All Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 11:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The euro, the pound and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Australian dollar, are higher Friday as risk sentiment improves.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is broadly lower, falling to one-month lows against the euro and sterling.\par \par \uc2 A lack of disappointing economic data and an increase in global liquidity levels, following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the prospect of more quantitative easing from the U.S. Federal Reserve, has helped to lift immediate fears about global recovery.\par \par \uc2 The better mood is also evident in global equity markets with gains on Wall Street followed by rallies in Asian as well as European stock markets.\par \par \uc2 Investor concerns about the sovereign debt crisis in the euro zone have also subsided following a series of successful bill and bond auctions from the region this week. The latest auctions were held Thursday with both France and Spain raising funds with little difficulty. In Spain's case, this appeared to suggest that the country is no longer seen as the high sovereign risk it once was.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro has risen against the dollar:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4606"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4606}}}\par \par \uc2 As investors moved out of safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was pushed lower. However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did manage to tick up slightly against the yen and the Swiss franc. Gains against the yen were limited as the market waits to see if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further to drive the yen even lower.\par \par \uc2 So far, there has been little sign of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since its spent about Y2 trillion Wednesday to push the dollar 2-3 yen higher.\par \par \uc2 One of the main losers has been the Swiss franc, which was under pressure both because of the move out of safe havens as well as the threat by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  Thursday that it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed, because of slower Swiss growth prospects in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The other major issue is the continued pressure from the U.S. on China to let the yuan rise. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner stopped short of accusing the country of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation Thursday but insisted that Beijing simply isn't doing enough to accelerate the rise in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China has allowed the yuan to rise to its highest level since 2005 in recent months but the gains have been very gradual and ensure that Chinese exports remain highly competitive.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the euro had risen to $1.3154 from $1.3085 late Thursday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The euro was also able to make it up to Y112.96 from Y112.29 while the dollar rose a little to Y85.84 from Y85.81.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rallied to $0.9469 from $0.9368 while the dollar rose to CHF1.0157 from CHF1.0146.\par \par \uc2 The pound, meanwhile, rose to $1.5716 from $1.5635 despite a string of disappointing economic news from the U.K. that had hurt sterling earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 17-09-10 0915GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100917e69h000nq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
