\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: PBOC Official Comments On Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Says Stronger Dollar Good\b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 10:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds recent comments from PBOC report, sovereign wealth fund official on U.S. dollar.)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--A People's Bank of China official said Friday that "appreciation of the U.S. dollar would be good," in response to a question about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to suppress the value of the yen.\par \par \uc2 Zhang Jianhua, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's research bureau, was speaking to Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of a forum. He didn't elaborate.\par \par \uc2 His comment was the first by a Chinese government official on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and was in line with recent warnings from Beijing about the dangers of a weak dollar. A major portion of China's $2.45 trillion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's biggest, is invested in U.S. dollar assets, and China is the largest foreign holder of U.S. Treasurys ahead of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Data issued Thursday showed China's holdings of long-term U.S. assets rose $3.0 billion to $846.7 billion in July, following net sales of $24.0 billion in June. Sixty-five percent of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are invested in dollar assets, state-run China Securities Journal reported earlier this month, citing an unnamed government official.\par \par \uc2 Lou Jiwei, the chairman of China's $300 billion sovereign-wealth fund, wrote in a book published this month that China could diversify its assets away from the dollar if the U.S. maintains a loose monetary policy that threatens to weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In its annual financial stability report on Friday, the People's Bank of China warned that large swings in the dollar's value could harm the global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 A rapid appreciation in the dollar would put pressure on the prices of basic materials, affecting resource-dependent economies, while a rapid depreciation could lead to inflation, asset price bubbles and financial instability, the report said.\par \par \uc2 -Liu Li and Aaron Back contributed to this article, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7713; li.liu@dowjones.com [ 17-09-10 0827GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100917e69h000l1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
