\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Dollar/Yen Options Fall; Upside Hedges Sold\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 08:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Dollar/yen options extended their falls in Tokyo Friday, as some players exited upside hedges and demand for downside hedges declined as the underlying exchange rate stayed in a Y85.50-Y86.00 band in the morning.\par \par \uc2 An options dealer at a major Japanese bank said investors exited dollar-call/yen-put contracts, expecting the greenback's gains against the yen to be limited as Japanese exporters are selling the dollar, capping its rise.\par \par \uc2 The dealer said one player sold two-week dollar-call/yen-put options contracts at a strike price of Y86.50 with implied volatilities of 11.5% for about $150 million.\par \par \uc2 Since this contract benefits the holder if the exchange rate is above the set price at the contract's expiry, the deal suggests the person thinks the greenback will be below Y86.50 in two weeks.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said they expect massive dollar sales by Japanese exporters around the Y86.00-Y86.50 level toward the end of the quarter.\par \par \uc2 The options dealer said some investors also sold contracts to avoid their devaluation as Japanese markets go into a three-day weekend. Monday is a national holiday in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark volatilities have fallen sharply since surging Wednesday when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, causing significant fluctuations in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark one-month at-the-money dollar/yen volatilities dropped to 10.50%/11.20% in Tokyo Friday from 10.80%/11.50% in New York Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The options dealer said benchmark volatilities may fall to 10.00% in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Market participants are paying attention to U.S. consumer price index data for August, scheduled for release at 1230 GMT. They will look to these data to assess the likelihood of the U.S. Federal Reserve easing policy further at its Sept. 21 meeting.\par \par \uc2 The median forecast of economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires is for the U.S. consumer price index to rise by 0.3%, the same as in July.\par \par \uc2 If the data suggest inflation is slowing significantly, that could fuel expectations the U.S. monetary authorities will take more easing steps soon, weakening the dollar against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    1 Month Option Implied Volatilities At 0400 GMT \par  \par               Tokyo           New York        Tokyo \par               Friday          Thursday          Thursday \par Dollar/Yen    10.50%/11.20%   10.80%/11.50%   11.10%/11.70% \par Euro/Dollar   10.20%/11.20%   10.30%/11.30%   10.30%/11.30% \par Euro/Yen      12.20%/14.20%   12.40%/14.40%   12.95%/14.95% \par  \par  \par    3 Month Option Implied Volatilities \par  \par               Tokyo          Tokyo \par               Friday         Thursday \par Dollar/Yen    11.00%/11.60%  11.40%/12.00% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The volatilities used are over-the-counter, at-the-money rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1-month 25 delta risk-reversals favor dollar put/yen call options by 0.45%/0.95% compared with 0.50%/1.00% in Tokyo Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Kosaku Narioka, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2784; kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com [ 17-09-10 0621GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1077 4127   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100917e69h000de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
