\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Vows To Act Vs Yen Again If Needed\b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 05:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comment from finance minister; background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister threatened Friday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again if necessary to weaken the yen, signaling the government's resolve to defend the nation's export-led economy despite criticism from abroad.\par \par \uc2 "As we have been saying, our basic stance is that we will take decisive steps, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary, and I'd like to maintain this stance," Yoshihiko Noda said at a news conference after a Cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 The remarks suggest that while Tokyo still values international cooperation and will continue trying to get U.S. and European support for its campaign against the yen's strength, it will step into the market anyway if the yen rises too much or too quickly.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday by selling an estimated Y2 trillion yen. It was the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years and exceeded the daily record, leading to criticism in the U.S. and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Jean-Claude Juncker, Luxembourg's prime minister and the head of the Eurogroup finance ministers, said Thursday his euro-zone partners "don't like unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner has so far said little about the action, leading some to think the U.S. government will turn the blind eye to the action. But in a sign that not everyone in the U.S. Congress is happy, Rep. Sander Levin (D., Mich.), who chairs the powerful House Ways and Means Committee, Wednesday called the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "deeply disturbing."\par \par \uc2 Noda said he is "checking" the overseas response to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding, "I understand there are various opinions."\par \par \uc2 "At a time when our country's economy is mired in a severe situation with deflation continuing, it is not favorable if the yen strengthens further and its (uptrend) becomes protracted," Noda said. "I believe it is important for us to explain our situation to the international community."\par \par \uc2 Gains in the yen can slow Japan's economic recovery by making exports more expensive in overseas markets and reducing the value of overseas earnings when remitted to Japan.\par \par \uc2 Noda said the yen's "one-sided" climb in the run-up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was both excessively sharp and out of sync with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deflation-ridden economy.\par \par \uc2 Before the government stepped into the market, the dollar fell to Y82.87, the lowest in 15 years and about 13% below its level in early May.\par \par \uc2 Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku recently suggested the goal of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to keep the dollar from breaking below Y82.00. But Noda flatly denied this, saying, "There isn't any particular line of defense."\par \par \uc2 The government's drastic measures seem to be working for the moment. Immediately after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar rose by about Y3. It then stayed between Y85.23 and Y85.94 until rising to around Y86.70 at 0240 GMT Friday.\par \par \uc2 Noda also said he expects the Bank of Japan to take action when appropriate, as it has recently promised. He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shares the same sense of urgency about the yen's strength and deflation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, along with the rest of Japan's Cabinet, Noda resigned earlier Wednesday ahead of a reshuffle by Prime Minister Naoto Kan later in the day. Noda is expected to be reappointed to the post, and will in any case remain in place until a new Cabinet is sworn in by the emperor, according to a finance ministry official, meaning there won't be any major change in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the near term.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 17-09-10 0345GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100917e69h00072\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
