\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  action heralds new hot EM money battle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 14:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  represents a new headache for emerging markets already battling with huge capital inflows and could set off another round of steps to control the floodgates.\par \par \uc2 Japan spent an estimated $20 billion in a single day of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday and pointedly did not drain yen funds, leaving extra liquidity in the domestic banking system.\par \par \uc2 With banks in Japan and the rest of the developed world proving reluctant to lend to businesses and households domestically, this liquidity could eventually leave Japan -- where a stagnant economy has kept interest rates near zero for the past decade -- in a search for yield.\par \par \uc2 With borrowing costs in the developed world stuck near zero and speculation of further easing, emerging markets stand out as the top destination.\par \par \uc2 The resulting impact on inflation would be troublesome for emerging economies, which are generally recovering faster, and where the Institute of International Finance expects to see inflows of over $700 billion in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Policy tightening measures to curb inflation would attract even more flows, creating a vicious circle.\par \par \uc2 "A huge pool of liquidity is being created in developed economies and Japan is just another drop in that pool. Ideally you want this liquidity lent out to the domestic economy but some of it will end up in emerging economies," said Michala Marcussen, head of global economics at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She said this could trigger a new round of restriction to capital flows, following those in Latin America in the 1970-80s, in Asia in the early 1990s and emerging Europe in the 2000s.\par \par \uc2 "We've already seen a couple of policy measures taken by various countries. We could see more. It could potentially lead to a fourth wave of capital controls," Marcussen said.\par \par \uc2 Fund tracker EPFR data shows emerging equity and bond funds saw inflows of $73.49 billion so far this year, already approaching last year's total inflows of $75 billion.\par \par \uc2 SILENT GLOBAL WAR\par \par \uc2 Brazil, which already imposes tax on inflows, was the first one to threaten more action.\par \par \uc2 Hours after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  said he would not sit on the sidelines "watching the game" while others weakened their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the expense of Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to take appropriate measures to stop the real from appreciating," he said. "We're watching every market, every part, for inflows... If inflows come we'll buy it all. We're not going to leave any excess of dollars in the market."\par \par \uc2 Japanese retail investors already have huge stakes in Latin America, especially Brazil, with one in five polled by Reuters favouring Brazilian stocks.\par \par \uc2 Brazil has put words into action, calling auctions twice a day to buy dollars in the spot market and increasing the amount it buys. Last Thursday alone, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased more than it did during the whole of February.\par \par \uc2 Next possible steps include an auction of reverse swaps, a form of derivative which has the same effect as buying dollars in the futures market.\par \par \uc2 Columbia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its market on Wednesday, kicking off a four-month programme to buy at least $20 million a day to help ease the rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other countries who moved to curb local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength this week include South Korea and Thailand.\par \par \uc2 "It's the latest episode of ... the 'silent war' which is taking place in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," said Andre Perfeito, economist at Gradual Investimentos in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund has endorsed capital controls as a way to curb capital surges, which some economists say have prompted emerging economies to adopt them.\par \par \uc2 BENEFITING FROM FLOWS\par \par \uc2 Instead of stemming it, some countries are exploiting the benefit of capital inflows, using extra \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves accumulated by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to create a sovereign wealth fund. The $3 trillion industry is seen doubling in less than 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2007, China issued 1.55 trillion yuan in special bonds to buy about $200 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to create \uc2 China Investment Corporation\uc2 . That sum has grown to $300 billion and the CIC has been lobbying for additional funding.\par \par \uc2 South Korea plans to inject more than $5 billion into its SWF. Brazil said it could use the SWF to absorb extra dollars from a planned share offering of \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Authorities are under pressure to effectively manage reserves, which is essentially national savings, and also maintain financial stability by not taking too much risk.\par \par \uc2 "One way of squaring this circle is to set up a SWF with a very clear mandate to manage long-term national savings," said Andrew Rozanov, head of sovereign advisory at Permal Investment Management.\par \par \uc2 Rozanov, who coined the term sovereign wealth fund in 2005, suggests a one-off swap of funds between dollar-heavy reserves and national pension funds that tend to have a high home bias.\par \par \uc2 "(A swap) may be one way to redeploy excess \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to be managed more optimally in a more appropriate long-term investment vehicle," he said. "It would also be a sound way to achieve better diversification of national savings and to help monetary authorities shrink reserve assets." (Additional reporting by Samantha Pearson in Sao Paulo, editing by Mike Peacock)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING/JAPAN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100920e69h000si\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
