\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK-Dollar may fall; investors watch for Fed, yen\b0\par\par\uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 21:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * All eyes on Fed policy meeting on Tuesday\par \par \uc2  * Downside in dollar/yen seen limited\par \par \uc2  * Euro zone worries back on investors' radar  (Refiles to fix grammar in paragraphs 2 and 15)\par \par \uc2  By Wanfeng Zhou\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar may fall next week as investors look to the Federal Reserve for clues about the prospect of further easing to spur the U.S. economy, but its downside versus the yen is limited after Japan's recent massive yen sales.\par \par \uc2  Speculation the Fed might resume large-scale asset purchases later this year has pressured U.S. Treasury yields and pushed the dollar down 1.5 percent this week against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <.DXY>, the biggest drop in two months.\par \par \uc2  While most analysts expect the Fed to renew its pledge to keep monetary policy accommodative, but not to announce any new steps when it meets on Tuesday, investors will scrutinize the accompanying statement to try to determine if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take action in coming months.\par \par \uc2  "There's been a lot of speculation that the Fed will increase their quantitative easing activity over the next few months," said Jessica Hoversen, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  in Chicago. "The market is going to be looking for the Fed to say something in their statement about policy."\par \par \uc2  Such speculation could continue to pressure the dollar against the yen even after Japan sold around $1.8 trillion yen ($20.98 billion) on Wednesday in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in six years in a bid to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.\par \par \uc2  Daniel Katzive, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in New York, said while the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably reduces the risk the pair may overshoot below 80, he would not be surprised to see another test below 85 in the near term.\par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen is going to stay very heavy despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because yield spreads remain very compressed," he added.\par \par \uc2  The dollar last traded little changed at 85.74 yen <JPY=> in New York. Resistance was seen at just under 86 yen, followed by 86.30, the bottom of the resistance cloud on its Ichimoku chart, a technical indicator. Traders also cited stop-loss buy orders above 86.00 and 86.35.\par \par \uc2  LOW VOLATILITY\par \par \uc2  While the yen's six month uptrend did not look convincingly broken, market participants expect lower volatility and range-trading to prevail in the short run amid fears of more yen selling by Japan.\par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen implied volatility <JPY1MO=>, a gauge of expectations for price fluctuations over the next 30 days, last traded at 10.61 percent. Volatilities hit 15.75 percent last week as investors grew nervous about potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  But at least for now, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared successful.\par \par \uc2  Boris Schlossberg, director for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT in New York, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when U.S. data showed some moderate improvement and therefore were "trading with the news flow and not against it." Upbeat data tends to reduce safe-haven demand for the yen.\par \par \uc2  Key U.S. data releases next week include housing starts, building permits, existing home sales and durable goods.\par \par \uc2  "As long as U.S. data doesn't disappoint massively, this may be one of the cases in which \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may work in the near term," Schlossberg said.\par \par \uc2  Jonathan Xiong, director and global investment strategist at Mellon Capital Management, said the firm's models "see more risk in the yen" and the firm may cut even further its long yen positions. Xiong is part of a team that oversees $28.6 billion in assets in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  said dollar/yen has seen the bottom and may rise to 93.0 by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, renewed worries about the euro zone's debt and banking troubles will also be in focus after Irish/German spreads widened amid fears about Ireland's banking sector.\par \par \uc2  "There is more bad news coming down the pike with regards to peripheral euro zone sovereign risk, so what remains to be seen is how resilient the euro will prove," said Win Thin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.  (Additional reporting by Vivianne Rodrigues and Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss; Editing by Diane Craft)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DOLLAR (OUTLOOK) (REFILE)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|UKI|CANT|AUF|J|Z|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100917e69h001bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
