\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-How is  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  judging success in yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 ?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano and Charlotte Cooper   \par \uc2 1,001 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 10:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , click on [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in six years has lifted the dollar well above a 15-year low hit this week, raising questions about what authorities would consider success in the unilateral action.\par \par \uc2 Just minutes after the dollar hit a low of 82.87 yen on Wednesday, Japanese authorities stepped into the market and made their biggest one-day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on record, selling an estimated 2 trillion yen ($23 billion).\par \par \uc2 WHAT RESULT WOULD JAPAN CONSIDER A SUCCESS?\par \par \uc2 The chief aims at this stage appear twofold: dissuading speculators from aggressively selling the dollar against the yen by creating more two-way risk, and absorbing dollar selling by Japanese exporters repatriating earnings.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not appear to be trying to drive dollar/yen to specific levels, having stepped back from the market since Wednesday's spree of yen selling. Traders report that hedge funds have been getting out of long yen positions but may still have more to do.\par \par \uc2 They also do not rule out chances of the Bank of Japan, which acts for the MOF, trying to push the dollar above 86.00 yen to trigger stop-loss buy orders and shunt it a bit higher.\par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters are said to have held off some of their dollar sales in the hope of getting a better rate before closing their books on the fiscal half-year ending on Sept. 30. Japan's action is widely seen as creating an opportunity for them to sell before that date.\par \par \uc2 Because of this, some think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be scaled back or even put on hold after the end of September. Others are not so sure, believing that the MOF would not want to risk a sudden dollar slide by abruptly ending \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHAT KIND OF TACTICS ARE THEY LIKELY TO EMPLOY?\par \par \uc2 After the shock and awe of the first day, further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be more tactical and opportunistic.\par \par \uc2 If the dollar stabilises above 85 yen there may be no need to act. But if it starts to fall again on selling by hedge funds and other speculators, the authorities may show themselves somewhere between 83.00-85.00 yen.\par \par \uc2 Traders say the uncertainty that has been created could last for a week or two, but if there is no follow-up action and the dollar remains under broad pressure -- for instance, from expectations of more Federal Reserve quantitative easing -- selling pressure will build again.\par \par \uc2 In that case, market players do not rule out chances of the authorities waiting for the market to become quite short dollars before springing a trap.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  notes, however, that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can bring its own problems: it attracts those keen to unload large dollar positions, especially at a time when many people think the dollar is likely to fall further.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also tends to suppress volatility, allowing big positions to build up that may result in bigger price swings when authorities back away.\par \par \uc2 HAVE THE MOF/BOJ DRAWN A LINE IN THE SAND?\par \par \uc2 Probably not. Japanese authorities would likely try to avoid giving the impression they are defending a particular level.\par \par \uc2 This week the government's spokesman, who has no say on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, was asked if the 82 level was the level being defended after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred there. The response: he believed the finance minister thought so.\par \par \uc2 But Tokyo market players took this comment in stride because if specific levels were cited they would become targets. Finance Minister Noda says they are not targeting a specific level.\par \par \uc2 HOW MUCH CAN THEY DO?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its borrowing limit for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the budget for fiscal 2010/11 by 5 trillion yen to 145 trillion. Before the first round of yen selling on Sept. 15 the funds available were roughly 35 trillion yen ($408 billion).\par \par \uc2 Kyodo news agency has reported that Japan is considering increasing the amount of funds available for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but did not cite a specific figure.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the authorities cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for long as heavily as they did the first time, but there is plenty of money for a few more rounds of large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WILL BOJ BOOST BALANCE SHEET?\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is expected to hold off draining yen that swashed into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in Tokyo's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The move will temporarily boost commercial banks' current account deposits parked at the BOJ, which used to be the target when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quantitative easing policy was in place for five years until 2006.\par \par \uc2 Unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may filter into the bond market and push down yields. But the effect on the economy and deflation will be limited as long as the abundant cash does not foster fund demand and bank lending. The money will simply accumulate at current account reserves parked at the BOJ.\par \par \uc2 DOES THIS SIGNAL A MORE PROACTIVE STANCE TO FIGHT DEFLATION?\par \par \uc2 With the government having played its part in curbing yen gains with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it may pile pressure on the BOJ to take further action to beat deflation.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is well aware and may ease monetary policy at its next rate review in early October. An increase in outright government bond purchases has been floated as among the more likely options, sources say. [ID:nTOE68G02D]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hesitant of increasing bond buying from the current 21.6 trillion yen per year, fearing that it would be seen as printing money to finance public debt. But it may not have much choice if the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins to fade, heightening pressure on the BOJ for more action. ($1=85.81 Yen) (Additional reporting by Kaori Kaneko and Leika Kihara; Editing by Eric Burroughs and Michael Watson)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Q+A)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD|PCO   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100917e69h000k6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
