\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Gyohten: govt couldn't let strong yen continue\b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 09:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept. 17 (Reuters) - Toyoo Gyohten, an adviser to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, said on Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week was because the government couldn't allow a surging yen to continue to weigh on exports and the economic outlook.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15 by selling yen <JPY=> for the first time in more than six years as the yen's surge to a 15-year high versus the dollar threatened to derail Japan's economy and worsen deflation.\par \par \uc2  Gyohten was director general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international bureau at the time of the Plaza Accord in 1985, when rich countries agreed to let the dollar depreciate to reduce the United States' trade deficit.\par \par \uc2  He then served as Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official until he retired from the finance ministry in 1989.  (Reporting by Stanley White; Editing by Chris Gallagher)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/MOF (URGENT)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100917e69h000fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
