\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2 , Fed QE positive for cross/yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,003 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 08:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosts global liquidity, to aid cross/yen\par \par \uc2 * Potential for more Fed QE also to benefit higher yielders\par \par \uc2 * Japan QE experience shows ample liquidity hunts for yield\par \par \uc2 * Aussie, Brazilian real and Indonesia rupiah may benefit\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against yen strength for the first time in six years means even more liquidity for already flush global markets, paving the way for further gains in higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar are poised to break out of this year's sideways struggle as the Bank of Japan's move to inject funds without sterilising their impact coincides with expectations for the Federal Reserve to boost its bond buying.\par \par \uc2 Resilient growth in emerging markets and higher interest rates in countries like South Africa and Brazil are proving alluring to yield-seeking Japanese investors and market players around the world seeking to boost returns via carry trades.\par \par \uc2 Even with the bouts of market volatility seen this year, carry trade strategies are proving a success because sharp market swings tend to be fleeting.\par \par \uc2 Cross/yen pairs -- such as Aussie/yen South African rand/yen and Brazilian real/yen -- may be able to hold gains and extend them even if dollar/yen stays stuck near 15-year lows despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the liquidity that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pumping into the markets from what is expected to be unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may provide more fuel to those funds seeking higher yields.\par \par \uc2 When markets turned rocky in August after the Federal Reserve's surprise decision to maintain the size of its balance sheet, the dollar jumped as stocks and commodities fell, suggesting the dollar is being used to fund carry trades.\par \par \uc2 Both of these fit with the emerging pattern that the dollar could remain weak against the yen even as higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  resume their upward climb, as long as the economic slowdown worries do not spark a long slide in global equities.\par \par \uc2 "As soon as risk appetite comes back, excess liquidity will have to go into some markets," said Hiroshi Yokotani, portfolio manager at Tokio Marine Rogge Asset Management in London.\par \par \uc2 "When that happens, I'm sure investors will focus on interest rate differentials," Yokotani said.\par \par \uc2 The yen has a history of weakening against higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while firming versus the greenback in such situations.\par \par \uc2 After the BOJ started quantitative easing in 2001 and the Fed slashed rates to then record lows, the yen weakened against most other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and continued to lose value up until the financial crisis struck in 2007.\par \par \uc2 In the two years after the BOJ started QE, both the euro and the Australian dollar rose about 16 percent against the yen. The New Zealand dollar rose 29 percent. But the U.S. dollar lost 6 percent. For a graphic, click on: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/gef34p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/gef34p }}}\par \par \uc2 Thus, where capital moves easily across borders, ample liquidity in one country can flow to high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and risky assets rather than stimulating lending in a moribund domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 QEII AND CARRY TRADE II?\par \par \uc2 With the Fed appearing to be on course to embark on its second quantitative easing to shore up growth and spur employment, the dollar's outlook could be even bleaker.\par \par \uc2 As dollar interest rates are expected to stay near zero "for an extended period" at minimum and the Fed could significantly increase the size of its $2.3 trillion balance sheet, the dollar may play a bigger role as the funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for carry trades.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie dollar is one of the best candidates to attract hungry investors. Australian interest rates are at 4.5 percent, by far the highest in the developed world.\par \par \uc2 Using the Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  carry trade calculator, since the start of the year a mix of three-month funding using the dollar and yen to buy the Aussie and Indonesia rupiah would have returned 4.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 In general, a gap of three percentage points is needed for carry trades to be effective, traders say. The gap between U.S. and Japanese two-year swap rates and Aussie swap rates is about 4.5 percentage points, and with Indonesia it is 7 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Australia is looking particularly strong thanks to robust demand for its abundant commodities, strong employment growth and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confident in raising overnight rates further.\par \par \uc2 "The Australian economic cycle is different from the rest of the West. It is more linked to China or India. If you want to do dollar-carry trade, the easiest thing is to go long in the Aussie," said Masato Chin, president of Chin Associates, an investment advisory firm.\par \par \uc2 Chin expects the Aussie to reach parity against the dollar from current levels near $0.94 in the next year.\par \par \uc2 Funds from the developed world are also likely spilling over to emerging economies.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real with interest rates of 10.75 percent, has been particularly popular among investors in Japan.\par \par \uc2 This year individual Japanese investors have bought nothing but the Aussie and the real through toushin, or investment trusts, according to State Street.\par \par \uc2 They bought 2.4 trillion yen ($28 billion) of funds investing in the real and 1.2 trillion yen in the Aussie, roughly matching the overall investment in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the rupiah and Indian rupee are also gaining popularity in Japan.\par \par \uc2 One condition for carry trades to thrive is low market volatility because big market swings can wipe out the returns from rate differentials. But despite Wall Street's "flash crash" in May and other turbulence this year, volatility is low.\par \par \uc2 One-month historical volatility in the Aussie/yen has fallen to about 23 percent after a spike near 50 percent in June. Before the global financial crisis, Aussie/yen volatility was just under 10 percent to about 20 percent. (Editing by Eric Burroughs)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100917e69h000cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
