\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan to seek global understanding on yen-finmin\b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 02:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Finmin Noda says Tokyo must gain global understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Noda says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if needed\par \par \uc2 * PM Kan to meet Obama on Sept. 23 in New York - Asahi\par \par \uc2 * Kan to seek U.S. understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - Asahi\par \par \uc2 * Noda: \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not targeting specific levels (Adds trader's quote, details)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Japan will try to dispel international criticism of its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by highlighting the damaging impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb on an economy mired in deflation, the finance minister said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Japan's solo foray into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Wednesday, its first in six years, sparked fears other nations might follow suit to prop up sagging exports and that such competitive devaluations could prove lethal to the slackening global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, who has retained his post in a cabinet reshuffle, told a news conference that even though he was aware of such concerns Tokyo was ready to act again if confronted with rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 The first opportunity to explain Tokyo's actions may come next week when Prime Minister Naoto Kan will meet U.S. President Barack Obama in New York on Sept. 23 on the sidelines of a U.N. General Assembly meeting, Japan's Asahi newspaper reported on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In its biggest one-day \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tokyo offloaded up to 1.86 trillion yen ($21.67 billion), money market data suggests, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped to another 15-year high against the dollar, threatening to derail Japan's tentative economic upturn.\par \par \uc2 Noda said the yen's rapid rise -- which he said was deviating from the economy's fundamentals -- was the reason Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and that he had no specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels in mind when deciding whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I'm aware that there are various opinions. But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance is that a prolonged yen rise is undesirable as the economy remains in a severe situation with ongoing deflation. It is important to consistently explain our stance to the international community," Noda told reporters after a cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 Jean-Claude Juncker, chairman of euro zone finance ministers and Luxembourg's prime minister, has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not welcomed and that the euro zone had told Japan of its disapproval.\par \par \uc2 With the market wary of pushing the dollar down too far against the yen after Tokyo's action, the dollar was steady around 85.80 yen on Friday, 3 yen above Wednesday's 15-year low of 82.87 yen.\par \par \uc2 "It seems that Japan has shifted its argument to focus more on deflation and less on exporters," said Kimihiko Tomita, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at State Street Bank & Trust Co in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen weighs on Japanese exporters' profits and makes it harder for Tokyo to end the long spell of falling prices that dampens consumer demand and discourages corporate investment.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has refrained from draining the extra funds released into the banking system via foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases, effectively further easing monetary conditions by boosting the the amount of money circulating in the market.\par \par \uc2 Still, economists expect the impact to be limited given that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark interest rate is already near zero and corporate demand for loans is weak.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not conduct a money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday morning, boosting cash that commercial banks park at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by around 2 trillion yen to about 17.1 trillion yen and pushing the overnight call rate slightly below the 0.1 percent policy target as a result. ($1=85.81 Yen) (Additional reporting by Rie Ishiguro, Leika Kihara; Editing by Edmund Klamann and Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100917e69h0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
