\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER VOWS TO ACT VS YEN AGAIN IF NEEDED \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister threatened Friday to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again if necessary to weaken the yen, signaling the government's resolve to defend the nation's export-led economy despite criticism from abroad. (published 0345 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ EXPECTS CURRENT ACCOUNT BALANCE AT Y17.10 TLN FRI VS Y15.09 TLN THU \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's latest forecast showed it expects its current account balance to increase by Y2 trillion Friday, indicating that it doesn't plan, at least for now, to soak up the yen that the government sold Wednesday in its massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC: RISK OF INFLATION, ASSET BUBBLES, BAD LOANS MAY RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday that loose monetary conditions may create risks of inflation, asset price bubbles, or bad loans in the banking system. (published 0321 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC: CHINA FACES MANY DIFFICULTIES IN MAINTAINING HIGH GROWTH RATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though economic confidence is increasing, China still faces many challenges in maintaining a fast and stable growth rate, including risks in the financial sector that are "hard to ignore," the People's Bank of China said Friday. (published 0258 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM KAN SET TO ORDER DRAFTING EXTRA BUDGET FOR FISCAL STIMULUS -KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan will order government officials to draft an extra budget for fiscal 2010 to finance an additional stimulus package worth several trillion yen, Kyodo News reported Friday, citing sources close to the matter. (published 0324 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVERNMENT DEBT SURPASSES THAT OF PRIVATE FIRMS IN APRIL-JUNE -NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO --The Japanese government's outstanding liabilities exceeded those of private companies in the April-June quarter, according to preliminary data the Bank of Japan released Friday. (published 0346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA MAY LET LOCAL FIRMS INVEST OFFSHORE YUAN DOMESTICALLY - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China could soon launch a trial to allow domestic financial institutions with yuan-denominated funds in Hong Kong to invest in China's A-share and bond markets, the state-run Securities Times reported Friday, citing unnamed sources. (published 0346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA YUAN ENDS 6-DAY RALLY VERSUS DOLLAR AFTER GEITHNER TESTIMONY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan ended its six-day rally against the U.S. dollar Friday, following a less aggressive effort by China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to guide its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  higher after the completion of the closely watched U.S. Congressional hearings on Beijing's exchange rate policy. (published 0213 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA MINISTER HOPES TO HAVE MINING TAX CERTAINTY SOON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Energy Minister Martin Ferguson said Friday he hopes to provide industry certainty on a proposed new levy on mining company profits "sooner rather than later", noting that both investment and government revenue streams are riding on the controversial tax proposal. (published 0321 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IMF: BANK INDONESIA'S MONETARY POLICY STANCE APPROPRIATE FOR NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia's current monetary policy stance is appropriate given that inflation is within the target range and the risk that hiking rates now could attract more volatile portfolio capital, the International Monetary Fund said Friday. (published 0200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO LAUNCH \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MEASURES BY END-SEPT - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  plans to introduce measures by the end of this month to prevent excessive strength in the baht, Thai-language daily Post Today reported Friday, citing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Satit Rungkasiri. (published 0137 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NZ 3Q CONSUMER CONFIDENCE SLIPS BUT REMAINS RESILIENT - WESTPAC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2 -McDermott Miller Consumer Confidence Index fell to 114.1 points in the third quarter from 119.3 points in the second quarter as the economic recovery remains patchy, a survey published Friday showed. (published 2316 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM TAPS BANRI KAIEDA AS ECON MINISTER - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan is considering choosing Committee on Financial Affairs Chairman Banri Kaieda to serve as economy minister in revamped Cabinet, the Asahi Shimbun reported Friday. (published 0152 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BBMG: TO ISSUE CNY2 BLN WORTH OF 5-YEAR NOTES IN CHINA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cement company \f28 \uc2 \f2 BBMG Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday it will issue CNY2 billion worth of five-year notes in China, in the first tranche of a CNY2.8 billion medium-term note issuance. (published 0011 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: NO NEED FOR LONG \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PROGRAM - EX-MOF UTSUMI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week was highly successful and will not need to be prolonged or expensive, according to a former top Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official who guided \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaigns in the past. (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =POINT OF VIEW: NO GOOD ANSWERS IN THE YUAN DEBATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the U.S. Treasury Secretary, there are probably no fun days and less fun days. Maybe history will look more kindly, but it's tough sledding right now. (published 0003 GMT) [ 17-09-10 0400GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100917e69h0007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
