\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(9/16) Tokyo Acts To Rein In Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,084 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 02:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A14\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki in Tokyo and Tom Lauricella in New York \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A wave of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  estimated at $20 billion sent the dollar sharply higher against the yen Wednesday, a successful beginning to what appears to be a campaign by Tokyo to stop a soaring yen that threatens to cripple its fragile economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Although Japanese officials have for weeks signaled their unhappiness with the yen's more-than-10% rise since early May, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were surprised by the exact timing of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and impressed by its size. Traders say that if their estimate for the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is accurate, it would likely mark the biggest one-day effort on record by the Bank of Japan to cap the yen.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, who confer regularly with counterparts at the U.S. Federal Reserve and Treasury Department, alerted them to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan. The U.S. has been involved in past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sometimes acting as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's middle man in U.S. markets and others, scooping up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for its own account.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the Fed and Treasury conferred with Bank of Japan officials but stayed out. U.S. officials would welcome an orderly decline in the dollar, because it could support exports, a needed driver of growth.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have been more concerned about the strength of China's yuan -- which they see as very undervalued -- than about the yen. Still, Japan's decision to stop the yen from appreciating raises concerns. One is that it could take pressure off the Chinese to allow their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 The initial barrage of yen-selling orders hit the market early in the Asian day as the dollar fell to a 15-year low below 83 yen, apparently a trigger point for authorities. The dollar jumped to 84.50 yen and then pushed higher as the Japanese authorities continued selling yen and buying dollars through European and New York trading hours. By late Wednesday, the yen had retreated nearly 3% and the dollar stood at 85.59 yen.\par \par \uc2 Japanese stocks were buoyed by relieved investors who had grown concerned in recent weeks about the impact of the yen's strength on Japan's export-dependent economy. The Nikkei Stock Average posted its biggest gain in over five weeks, rising 2.3% to 9516.56 led by exporters such as \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said in a statement he "strongly expects" the action "will contribute to a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate formation."\par \par \uc2 Many in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market say that while the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a big short-term impact, on its own the long-term effectiveness is questionable. But for now, "it seems there are very few people who are willing to take the other side and fight it," says Douglas Borthwick, a managing director at Faros Trading in Stamford, Conn.\par \par \uc2 "It's positive as a first move," \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro said. "The timing came as a surprise," he said, for traders who had believed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was less likely after Prime Minister Naoto Kan was re-elected as leader of the Democratic Party of Japan on Tuesday. Mr. Kan was seen as less inclined to push back against the yen's rise than his opponent.\par \par \uc2 Japan Chamber of Commerce Chairman Tadashi Okamura said that had the government not waited so long, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have been more effective." He also said that it would be "appropriate" for the dollar to rise to 95 yen.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a one-time step or the beginning of a longer campaign. If history is any guide, Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was just the opening salvo.\par \par \uc2 "More likely than not, more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coming," said Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Bank in New York. Tokyo "wouldn't begin an effort like this without thinking it was going to have to be something more than a 'one and done' \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Adding extra weight to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are signs that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is leaving the yen it sold in the financial markets. In the past it has mopped up the extra liquidity -- a process known as "sterilization."\par \par \uc2 A person familiar with the thinking of the Bank of Japan said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees "no need to absorb" the yen sold, since it would blunt the effectiveness of the government's efforts to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts saw this as an important measure to increase Japan's monetary base and a further weapon to fight a long deflationary spiral that has dragged down the economy. Financial analysts have criticized the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for refusing to take strong steps to end Japan's deflationary cycle.\par \par \uc2 But the Japanese government has a battle on its hands. Analysts attribute most of the yen buying to Japanese exporters repatriating profits, and a desire by global investors to park their money in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that appeared to have few risks amid a turbulent global economy.\par \par \uc2 "The yen is rising on the back of global moves to keep home \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak and amid continuing expectations for further monetary easing in the U.S.," said Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. "Unless there are fundamental changes in these areas, it will not be possible to stem the yen's rise," he added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has other global implications. Just a few months ago, policy makers in many countries were considering ways to tighten their monetary policies with the financial crisis having passed and a recovery seeming to take hold.\par \par \uc2 Increasingly, with global growth disappointing, especially in developed countries, policy makers in many places are looking for ways to ease financial conditions.\par \par \uc2 Allowing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate or preventing its appreciation is one step in that direction and could be a signal of a shift toward easier money policies globally, particularly in the slow-growing developed world.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan pared its gains against the dollar on news. Beijing, which closely manages the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , didn't comment on Japan's move. China has long rebuffed foreign criticism of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices by saying that countries should have the right to determine their own exchange-rate mechanisms.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Jon Hilsenrath in New York and Megumi Fujikawa in Tokyo contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2010 20:05 ET (00:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100916e69g0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
