\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(9/17) Heard on the Street:Tokyo's Yen Shift Poses Trader Puzzle\b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 19:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 28\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Richard Barley and Simon Nixon \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 How should investors respond to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surprise \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the strength of the yen?\f28 \par \par \uc2 That depends largely on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  turns out to be a political gesture, limited to the roughly two trillion yen ($23 billion) so far spent, or something more like the 2003-2004 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the tune of 35 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo certainly has room in its budget plans to fund a bigger program.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the initial impact on exchange rates other than the dollar-yen has been limited. While the yen has fallen 3.3% against the dollar, the dollar hasn't gained against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if the yen is no longer a one-way trade, investors could switch to commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's move also is likely to affect fixed-income markets, particularly as the Bank of Japan seems intent on leaving the funds in the market, making this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a form of quantitative easing as well. But it will be crucial to watch Japanese fiscal policy, because further quantitative easing could increase sovereign credit risk.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it is possible a lower yen will succeed in boosting Japanese exporters. But exports have never been Japan's problem: The real issue is the lack of domestic demand. And on that score, Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by reducing the value of domestic earnings, may be self-defeating.\par \par \uc2 That would point to Japan's actions being a political gesture, albeit a futile one. Tokyo's rhetoric suggests otherwise.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 16, 2010 13:08 ET (17:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100916e69g0048f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
