\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Dollar/Yen Options Down; Geithner Comments Awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 07:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Dollar/yen options fell sharply in Tokyo Thursday as the underlying exchange rate hovered well above the psychologically important Y85.00 during Asian morning trade, reducing demand for protection against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuation.\par \par \uc2 However declines may be short lived if U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner says anything negative at U.S. Congressional hearings about the Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Benchmark volatilities surged yesterday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold the yen to help curb its recent rise, triggering a sharp ascent in the dollar against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speculation of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  then helped push benchmark volatilities even lower Thursday, said an options dealer at a major Japanese bank.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark one-month at-the-money dollar/yen volatilities dropped to 11.10%/11.70% in Tokyo from 11.80%/12.50% in New York Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 If the exchange rate stays around its current level for the remainder of the global day, volatilities may decline 20 basis points; and if the dollar gradually rises against the yen, volatilities may decline toward 10.5% in the near term, the dealer said.\par \par \uc2 However the Treasury's Geithner is scheduled to speak at Congressional hearings at 1400 GMT and 1800 GMT. If he says anything negative about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar may resume falling against the yen--pushing up volatilities--as that would discourage Japanese authorities from continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    1 Month Option Implied Volatilities At 0200 GMT \par  \par               Tokyo           New York        Tokyo \par               Thursday        Wednesday       Wednesday \par Dollar/Yen    11.10%/11.70%   11.80%/12.50%   12.45%/13.15% \par Euro/Dollar   10.30%/11.30%   10.55%/11.55%   10.65%/11.65% \par Euro/Yen      12.95%/14.95%   13.40%/15.40%   13.55%/15.55% \par  \par  \par    3 Month Option Implied Volatilities \par  \par               Tokyo          Tokyo \par               Thursday       Wednesday \par Dollar/Yen    11.40%/12.00%  12.30%/12.90% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The volatilities used are over-the-counter, at-the-money rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1-month 25 delta risk-reversals favor dollar put/yen call options by 0.50%/1.00% compared with 1.20%/1.70% in Tokyo Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Kosaku Narioka, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2784; kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com [ 16-09-10 0513GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Naoto Kan's continuance in the premiership is a good thing for debt-ridden Japan because policy continuity will enable the efficient implementation of fiscal improvement programs, a senior Moody's Investors Service credit analyst said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Prime Minister Kan's reelection was credit positive," said Tom Byrne, senior vice president and regional credit officer for Asia and the Middle East during a meeting with the press.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He noted Japan should work hard to decrease its fiscal spending because with an expected low economic growth rate, it's unlikely that Japan will see its income increase in the years to come.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Japan is able to restrain its spending, it could be positive for the economy in the longer-term by improving sentiment among firms less concerned about potential tax increases, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Byrne added he doesn't think tax hikes will come about in Japan, including in the consumption tax, over the next few years as the nation's economic recovery isn't full-fledged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  has a Aa2 rating on Japan with a stable outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 17-09-10 0959GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1077 4127  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g0009o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
