\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Ruble May Rally After Finding Support at 31.09\b0\par\par\uc2 1,323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 11:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble may rally after finding support at RUB31.09 to the dollar and RUB35.27 to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar-euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket, a Moscow trader at an international investment bank said. The ruble slump in the last two days was caused by dollar-euro fluctuations that had the effect of moving traders out of the ruble basket after the Japanese dollar-yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , trader says. Current weakness today is just "echoes of yesterday's selloff," trader says. Ruble on Thursday slips to 35.16 against the dollar-euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket at 0914 GMT, from 35.12 late Tuesday. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  falls to 30.9720 against the dollar, the weakest since early July, and slumps to 40.3135 vs euro. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0951 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed in thin trade; offshore players were spotted selling medium- to long-term tenors, says local dealers. Expects rangebound trade Friday; there may be some follow through selling but current levels still look attractive. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.77%/2.72% vs 2.78%/2.72% late yesterday, November 2016 quoted at 2.92%/2.87% vs 2.90%/2.80%, June 2019 quoted at 3.00%/2.95% vs 2.98%/2.92%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0946 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end down 0.5% at 3,341.632 in large volume on profit-taking; but foreign fund inflows lend support, lifting index from intraday low of 3,318.523, traders say. "Most locals are cashing in their gains, and this will likely continue tomorrow. On the other hand, foreigners are buying a decent amount of shares," trader says; foreigners net buyers today, picking up around IDR828 billion worth of stocks. Index tipped in 3,260-3,370 range Friday. Among decliners: \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) down 4.3% at IDR55,300; Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 1.1% at IDR9,200; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 1.5% at IDR6,650; and Bumi Resources (BUMI.JK) down 0.6% at IDR1,820. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] The unexpected 0.5% drop in UK August retail sales deals a significant blow to growth hopes and will likely fuel fears of a double dip recession says Global Insight's Howard Archer. He notes that the fall in retail sales were not only broad based but also occurred despite evidence of competitive pricing on the high street with the year on year deflator at a 9 month low of 1.5%. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  economist Christian Keller says Hungary's 2011 financing is risky on uncertainties about the government's final fiscal policy decisions and Prime Minister Viktor Orban's yet unrevealed plans. Expects investors to be hesitant to buy into the presented plan due to high volatility. Says the financing plan requires potential investors to be very confident about Hungary's outlook to be willing to buy into the increased issuance, and adds the government's communication on its strong liquidity position and the feasibility of the financing plan is overly optimistic. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB unchanged vs late yesterday at 30.85. Expectations BOT will keep policy rate steady next month supporting pair but THB widely expected to continue rising after BOT's chief said yesterday bank has no immediate plan to take additional steps to stem THB strength. No explicit sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD/THB today. "As fears of (possible) \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures subside, I think the dollar/baht would quickly resume its downward trend," says Bangkok-based dealer. Next support at 30.80, resistance pegged at 30.90.(phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday "could be a loud warning shot to Beijing, given the (recent) record buying of Japanese government bonds by China," says Ashraf Laidi, chief market strategist at CMC Markets. China in July increased net buying of Japan financial assets, all or nearly of which thought to be in JGBs. Some analysts speculate China's JGB buying could be meant in part to keep JPY strong, weighing on Japan economy, which China looks to surpass as world's second biggest this year. Japan Finance Minister Noda recently questioned "true intention" of China's JGB purchases. Given Noda raising such suspicions, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  statements on resolve to pursue further action "sound like a warning shot to Beijing's yen-purchases as opposed to the average \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculator," Laidi says. Adds, Beijing could counter by tightening monetary policy, which could upset risk trade, possibly push safe-haven JPY higher.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares lower as institutions sell, with cement, fertilizers shares leading losses. KSE-100 index last down 0.3% at 10,014.25. "Funds are booking profits at every high levels due to uncertainty and inflation concerns. Markets rose over 1% in early session on approval of margin trading system by SECP and IMF's $450 million aid for the country for emergency needs," says dealer. Mohammad Sohail, analyst at the Topline Securities says investors wary that higher inflation could lead to higher interest rates. DGK Cement (DGKC.KA) down 2.1% at PKR25.38, MCB Bank (MCBB.KA) down 0.2% at PKR193.00. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] The successful Spanish bond auction Thursday is the most likely trigger for the healthy bounce we have seen in EUR, but it's not a wholly satisfactory explanation. After all, EUR tends to fall on bad auctions but do nothing on good results. That's getting the rumor mill going again. "You can never prove it, but maybe this is some Chinese buying," says Lutz Karpowitz at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . He points out the USD/CNY fixing has fallen a lot by its standards of late, and China has an interest in a weak dollar to keep trade-weighted values favorable. EUR/USD stands at 1.3059. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR tad lower at 85.78 vs 85.79 Wednesday as export settlements, remittances offset demand from imports, oil payments. Still, pair unlikely to fall sharply as demand from importers strong in afternoon session. Almost $27 million-$28 million being bought by oil importers, $10 million-$12 million for payments of non-oil imports, says trader. Tips pair to trade in 85.75-85.85 band. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0903 GMT [Dow Jones] A "modest" $1.6 billion drop in Russia's international reserves in the past week may indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  slightly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling dollars on behalf of the sinking ruble, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  says. But most of the decline in reserves in dollar terms probably came from dollar-euro fluctuations, RBS says. Ruble on Thursday slips to 35.15 against the dollar-euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket at 0857 GMT, from 35.12 late Tuesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls to 30.9816 against the dollar, the weakest since early July, and slumps to 40.3052 vs euro. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher late, in line with broad USD strength vs regional units; players continue to cover short positions after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to weaken JPY vs USD) yesterday. Pair last at 44.40 in heavy volume vs 44.26 late Wednesday. "There are also concerns about the U.S. economy so some investors are shifting funds," says local bank trader. Tips pair may head towards 44.70 resistance Friday; psychological support at 44.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0555ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00074\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
