\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Looks Vulnerable; Eyes On China\b0\par\par\uc2 1,837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 01:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 looks vulnerable to further profit taking before the weekend, with a fall toward former "inverse head & shoulders neckline" resistance at 4590 (weekly charts) possible after an early bounce toward the 200-day moving average at 4628, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. More likely, the index will stay within those levels today but it's likely to fade in the afternoon, particularly if China's equity market remains on the backfoot. IG Markets tips 0.4% opening rise to 4625, with an afterhours surge in \uc2 Oracle\uc2  and RIM lifting U.S. index futures 0.2% in early trading. However, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is more of a leading indicator for the economy and its disappointing earnings guidance last night should encourage further profit taking on upticks today, as should the disappointing Philadelphia Fed manufacturing survey. Although traders have been turning bullish after the recent break above range resistance at 4622, the break is not set in concrete. Also, while the inverse head & shoulders bottom targets 5025 while the index remains above the neckline, it's interesting to note that Shanghai has been unable to stay above equivalent neckline support. Index last 4605.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely rangebound amid dearth of fresh leads; market participants awaiting auction of 3-year Islamic bonds, likely for amount of MYR3 billion, for clearer direction, dealers say; auction announcement from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected next week