 (2823.HK) leads with HK$402.74 million, then \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (3968.HK) with HK$217.39 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$197.09 million, China National Building Material (3323.HK) with HK$160.65 million and China Citic Bank (0998.HK) with HK$144.10 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] Autumn may represent "prime buying opportunity" for Japanese stocks, says Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Says if yen appreciates against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on back of concerns over prospects for the US economy, additional monetary easing by the Fed, and fears regarding euro/European financial system, expects selling pressure on Japanese equities to increase. "At the same time though, Japanese companies have strengthened their earnings mixes by becoming more international and tightening cost controls, likely making them more resistant to headwinds." Notes if Japanese stocks pull back further, average P/B (currently around 1X) could drop to historically low 0.9X. Assuming a further sell-off, adds investor interest in Japanese equities will likely shift to high-yield plays as safe havens. "On a long-term horizon though, we see a buying opportunity for companies with high overseas sales weightings active in Asia," says house. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2340 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 3Q consumer confidence slips, but consumers surprisingly resilient despite massive earthquake, collapse of South Canterbury Finance, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  chief economist Brendan O'Donovan. Index falls to 114.1 points from 119.3 points in 2Q. Says fall in confidence, sharp drop in short-term economic outlook "implies that growth in consumer spending will remain modest in the near term." Spending hampered by weak income growth but says once situation improves "it is hard to believe consumers will not act." Adds survey challenges RBNZ's view of subdued consumer spending as index still above long run average of 111.6, broadly consistent with annual average growth in real consumer spending of around 3.0%. Notes RBNZ revised down forecasts for real consumer spending at yesterday's monetary policy review, with growth tipped to slow from 2.3% in the year to March 2011 to just over 0.5% in March 2012. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may get a mild lift from Wall Street's rise but index likely choppy in tight range before long Korean thanksgiving holiday next week. "Since no event is expected and Korea is entering into a long holiday (from Tuesday), the market is likely to remain sluggish for a while," says \uc2 Hana Daetoo Securities\uc2 ' Suh Dong-pill; "expectations for third-quarter corporate earning results will start arising some time at the end of this month at the earliest." Kospi tipped in 1810-1825 range after ending down 0.7% at 1811.85; techs may get a small boost from gains in U.S. peers, analysts say. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude set to consolidate with downside bias after settling $1.45 lower at $74.57/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; positive MACD histogram bars contracting, pointing to downside risk. Support at $74.11 (yesterday's low); breach would target $73.88 (Sept. 9 low), then $73.37 (Sept. 8 low), $72.63 (Sept. 7 low) and $71.53 (Aug. 31 reaction low). Resistance at $75.54 (hourly chart); break would temper near-term negative outlook, targeting $76.21 (previous base set Tuesday), then $78.04 (1-month high when hit Monday), a break of which would open way to $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 Aug. 4-Aug. 25 decline), then $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). October crude now off 2 cents at $74.55 on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may raise to 86.50 as players still unwilling to pile into short positions so soon after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says senior dealer at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  trust bank. "Fear (of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) remains in the markets," dealer says. Says upside limited, however, due to Japan exporters' USD sales orders lined up in 86.00-86.50 range. Traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows big exporters to sell USD at reasonably attractive levels. Pair may trade in 85.20-86.50 range vs 85.86 last. EUR/USD may rise to 1.3150 as Europe sovereign risk fades while U.S. Treasury yields still low, making EUR relatively more attractive. EUR/USD may trade in 1.3030-1.3150 range vs 1.3076 last. EUR/JPY biased up; may trade in 111.80-113.00 band vs 112.26. Markets focused on Japan authorities' next actions. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2324 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may rise to 1.070% vs Thursday's 1.040% as JPY remains on downward trend with lingering caution that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again, which likely help lift Tokyo shares, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Tetsuro Sawano. But upturn in long-term JGB yields may be limited on growing view that BOJ leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, which would effectively be monetary easing. Lead December JGB future likely to hover in 141.95-142.35 band vs Thursday's 142.26. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads mostly positive, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.5% at 4653.0 after Wall Street consolidated in quiet trading amid mixed economic reports and disappointing earnings guidance from \uc2 FedEx\uc2 . DJIA up 0.2% at 10594.83, S&P 500 near flat at 1124.66, Nasdaq up 0.1% at 2303.25. After hours, \uc2 Oracle\uc2  rose 4.3% and \uc2 Research in Motion\uc2  rose 3.5% on improved earnings guidance. U.S. weekly initial jobless claims fell 3,000 to 450,000 vs 460,000 expected. Philadelphia Fed's September manufacturing activity index fell to -0.7 vs flat reading expected. August PPI rose 0.4% as expected. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  fell 3.0% on disappointing 2Q earnings forecast. In technology sector, \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  rose 1.8%, \uc2 Cisco\uc2  rose 1.6% and Intel rose 1.3%. S&P 500 Financials down 0.4%, with KBW Bank index down 0.7%. S&P 500 Materials up 0.4%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  down 1.5%, Freeport up 0.4%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  up 1.6%, Barrick up 2.0%. BHP ADRs at A$39.11 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9366) vs local close at A$38.75. London listed BHP flat, Rio (RIO.AU) flat. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.3%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.4%. Spot gold at US$1,275.23 after setting record high of US$1,277.85 vs US$1,267.78 yesterday. Nymex crude oil down 1.9% at US$74.57. LME copper up 1.1%, zinc up 0.1%, aluminum up 0.6%, nickel near flat. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3077 vs 1.3012. AUD/USD at 0.9362 vs 0.9379. S&P/ASX 200 last 4605.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore exports likely continued to grow at double-digit rate in August, though at slower pace than previous months as base effect diminishes. Non-oil domestic exports expected +14.9% on-year vs +18.2% in July, Dow Jones Newswires poll of 6 economists shows. Compared with previous month, August exports expected to decline 1.5% in seasonally adjusted terms. Exports shrank 3.9% on-month in July. Possible weakness in pharmaceuticals sector could contribute to export softness. "While we will still continue to expect healthy year-on-year growth of headline NODX due to the favourable low base effect...this effect will be much weaker in 2H compared to 1H," Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew says. Data due 0500 GMT. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 800,000 Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 6th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 19.1 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 18.3 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 1951ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g000ha\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
