latively more attractive. EUR/USD may trade in 1.3030-1.3150 range vs 1.3076 last. EUR/JPY biased up; may trade in 111.80-113.00 band vs 112.26. Markets focused on Japan authorities' next actions. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2324 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may rise to 1.070% vs Thursday's 1.040% as JPY remains on downward trend with lingering caution that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again, which likely help lift Tokyo shares, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Tetsuro Sawano. But upturn in long-term JGB yields may be limited on growing view that BOJ leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, which would effectively be monetary easing. Lead December JGB future likely to hover in 141.95-142.35 band vs Thursday's 142.26. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads mostly positive, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.5% at 4653.0 after Wall Street consolidated in quiet trading amid mixed economic reports and disappointing earnings guidance from \uc2 FedEx\uc2 . DJIA up 0.2% at 10594.83, S&P 500 near flat at 1124.66, Nasdaq up 0.1% at 2303.25. After hours, \uc2 Oracle\uc2  rose 4.3% and \uc2 Research in Motion\uc2  rose 3.5% on improved earnings guidance. U.S. weekly initial jobless claims fell 3,000 to 450,000 vs 460,000 expected. Philadelphia Fed's September manufacturing activity index fell to -0.7 vs flat reading expected. August PPI rose 0.4% as expected. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  fell 3.0% on disappointing 2Q earnings forecast. In technology sector, \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  rose 1.8%, \uc2 Cisco\uc2  rose 1.6% and Intel rose 1.3%. S&P 500 Financials down 0.4%, with KBW Bank index down 0.7%. S&P 500 Materials up 0.4%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  down 1.5%, Freeport up 0.4%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  up 1.6%, Barrick up 2.0%. BHP ADRs at A$39.11 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9366) vs local close at A$38.75. London listed BHP flat, Rio (RIO.AU) flat. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.3%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.4%. Spot gold at US$1,275.23 after setting record high of US$1,277.85 vs US$1,267.78 yesterday. Nymex crude oil down 1.9% at US$74.57. LME copper up 1.1%, zinc up 0.1%, aluminum up 0.6%, nickel near flat. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3077 vs 1.3012. AUD/USD at 0.9362 vs 0.9379. S&P/ASX 200 last 4605.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore exports likely continued to grow at double-digit rate in August, though at slower pace than previous months as base effect diminishes. Non-oil domestic exports expected +14.9% on-year vs +18.2% in July, Dow Jones Newswires poll of 6 economists shows. Compared with previous month, August exports expected to decline 1.5% in seasonally adjusted terms. Exports shrank 3.9% on-month in July. Possible weakness in pharmaceuticals sector could contribute to export softness. "While we will still continue to expect healthy year-on-year growth of headline NODX due to the favourable low base effect...this effect will be much weaker in 2H compared to 1H," Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew says. Data due 0500 GMT. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 800,000 Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 6th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 19.1 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 18.3 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold adds 20 cents early in Asia at $1,273.90/oz, after trading up to a fresh record high of $1,277.85 overnight, a gain fueled by safe haven demand, according to \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 