 signals that it is the strength of Asia that is more important for policy makers than previous close economic ties with economies like the U.S. Says Stevens likely to reinforce that hawkish mood and bring some clarity to the rates outlook. Notes some in the market are talking about a hike in October, so the speech will be watched even more intently than usual. Support for December 3-year bonds likely at 95.14. Now 95.18, down 7 basis points. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices to fall again today after U.S. wheat prices sank for a third session overnight as disappointing weekly export sales fueled fears a rush to buy grain after a severe Russian drought is ending. Weak sales caused traders to reconsider because the U.S., the world's top wheat exporter, had recorded weekly export business of more than 1 million tons in previous five weekly reports. Importers have enough wheat to cover their needs roughly through November after getting spooked by Russia's drought, traders said. Soft red winter wheat for December delivery on CBOT fell 7.5 cents, or 1%, at US$7.1925/bushel. AUD remains strong, holding most recent gains, AUD/USD last 0.9362, near midpoint of yesterday's daytime trading range. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales fell A$4.50 to settle at A$276.00/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia fell the same amount to A$304.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold adds 30 cents early in Asia at $1,274.00/oz, after yesterday trading a range $1,265.05 up to a fresh record high of $1,277.85, extending a rally from intraday low of $1,158.30 on July 27. Gold futures on Comex in NY also posted a new record settlement overnight as USD weakened and on safe haven buying as equities eased. LME base metals mostly rose yesterday after drifting in their recent ranges for much of the session, with mixed U.S. data failing to provide any clear direction, although finding support in a stronger euro, which peaked at 1-month highs against USD. Market participants reported lower volumes ahead of next week's national holidays in China, which increased market volatility. LME 3-month copper rose $81 vs Wednesday PM kerb to $7,696/metric ton, aluminum edged $12 higher at $2,165, zinc firmed $2 at $2,147.00, nickel climbed $45 to $23,245 and tin extended its rally, jumping $345 to $23,495, a 52% gain from June 7. Lead bucked yesterday's trend, losing $33 to $2,202. Of other precious metals, silver early in Asia loses 3 cents at $20.77/oz, platinum down $2 at $1,605, palladium loses $1 at $548. Nymex October crude futures lose 78 cents overnight at $75.82/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2228 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Technology companies boosted stocks as confidence mounted economy would avoid double-dip recession; \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  rose 1.8%, Cisco Systems gained 1.6%, Intel +1.3%; "It seems like we priced in too much of a double dip, and it's not coming to pass as bad as people anticipated," says Patrick Becker Jr., principal at Becker Capital Management. Industrials weakened, weighed by 3.8% drop in \uc2 FedEx\uc2  after 1Q profit more than doubled on rise in revenue, but company forecast 2Q earnings below analysts' average estimate. Company also said will combine FedEx Freight, \uc2 FedEx National LTL\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at end of January, as it cuts 1,700 full-time employees, closes 100 facilities. Shipping peer \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  also fell 1.4%. \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  added 4.8% after car maker upgraded to "overweight" from "equal weight" by \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , which said North American market will sustain strong margins. Dow +0.2%, Nasdaq up 0.1%, Philly semicons +0.8%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2225 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says it's time to buy USD/JPY, EUR/JPY on