\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Foreign Net Buy Orders On 800,000 Japan Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 01:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 800,000 Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 6th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 19.1 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 18.3 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold adds 20 cents early in Asia at $1,273.90/oz, after trading up to a fresh record high of $1,277.85 overnight, a gain fueled by safe haven demand, according to \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , while others pinned the gain to USD weakness, extending a rally from intraday low of $1,158.30 on July 27. Noting this 8-week uptrend, ScotiaMocatta now puts trend support at $1,247, while area around $1,263 it tagged secondary support. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says market isn't yet fading from either the new all-time high or the aggressive upside move earlier this week. Technically, despite intraday overbought conditions that are accumulating, short-term trend is up while seasonal trends remain constructive, it says, adding that it expects gold to make further headway towards $1,350/$1,377 target in coming weeks. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional share markets have mild upward lead from slightly higher closes for DJIA, Nasdaq; Japanese exporters likely to stay in demand with JPY weakening a little more vs USD, EUR after Tokyo authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets earlier this week though investors likely cautious before long weekend in Japan. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, traders still watching JPY closely for signs of continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . EUR/USD at 1.3078 vs 1.3085 late NY, EUR/JPY at 112.25 vs 112.29, USD/JPY at 85.86 vs 85.81. Not much on data slate: Singapore non-oil exports at 0500 GMT, Japan department store sales at 0530 GMT. Later there's German PPI, euro area BOP, EU construction output, U.S. real earnings, CPI, preliminary Thomson Reuters/University of Michigan survey of consumers, Fed governor Tarullo in panel discussion. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD upward momentum has stalled as RBA rate hike expectations have stepped back a bit, says John Kyriakopoulos, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at NAB. Says there's now a 19% chance of a 25 bps rate hike in October priced, down from 25% two days ago. Adds U.S. bond yields have stopped plunging. As such, the 0.9430/60 area will provide stiff resistance for AUD/USD near-term in the absence of further gains in global stock markets and commodity prices or a re-widening in the Australia-U.S. 2-year swap yield spread. Expects initial support is at 0.9330, then 0.9270. AUD/USD now 0.9358. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2250 GMT [Dow Jones] Downward drift in prices for Australian bonds likely to continue with focus on speech by RBA Governor Glenn Stevens on Monday. Andrew Bryan, debt strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , says RBA has been sending signals that it is the strength of Asia that is more important for policy makers than previous close economic ties with economies like the U.S. Says Stevens likely to reinforce that hawkish mood and bring some clarity to the rates outlook. Notes some in the market are talking about a hike in October, so the speech will be watched even more intently than usual. Support for December 3-year bonds likely at 95.14. Now 95.18, down 7 basis points. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices to fall again today after U.S. wheat prices sank for a third session overnight as disappointing weekly export sales fueled fears a rush to buy grain after a severe Russian drought is ending. Weak sales caused traders to reconsider because the U.S., the world's top wheat exporter, had recorded weekly export business of more than 1 million tons in previous five weekly reports. Importers have enough wheat to cover their needs roughly through November after getting spooked by Russia's drought, traders said. Soft red winter wheat for December delivery on CBOT fell 7.5 cents, or 1%, at US$7.1925/bushel. AUD remains strong, holding most recent gains, AUD/USD last 0.9362, near midpoint of yesterday's daytime trading range. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales fell A$4.50 to settle at A$276.00/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia fell the same amount to A$304.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold adds 30 cents early in Asia at $1,274.00/oz, after yesterday trading a range $1,265.05 up to a fresh record high of $1,277.85, extending a rally from intraday low of $1,158.30 on July 27. Gold futures on Comex in NY also posted a new record settlement overnight as USD weakened and on safe haven buying as equities eased. LME base metals mostly rose yesterday after drifting in their recent ranges for much of the session, with mixed U.S. data failing to provide any clear direction, although finding support in a stronger euro, which peaked at 1-month highs against USD. Market participants reported lower volumes ahead of next week's national holidays in China, which increased market volatility. LME 3-month copper rose $81 vs Wednesday PM kerb to $7,696/metric ton, aluminum edged $12 higher a