\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Gold $1,273.90; Short-Term Trend Still Up -\uc2 Barclays\b0\par\par\uc2 1,796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 01:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold adds 20 cents early in Asia at $1,273.90/oz, after trading up to a fresh record high of $1,277.85 overnight, a gain fueled by safe haven demand, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 , while others pinned the gain to USD weakness, extending a rally from intraday low of $1,158.30 on July 27. Noting this 8-week uptrend, ScotiaMocatta now puts trend support at $1,247, while area around $1,263 it tagged secondary support. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  says market isn't yet fading from either the new all-time high or the aggressive upside move earlier this week. Technically, despite intraday overbought conditions that are accumulating, short-term trend is up while seasonal trends remain constructive, it says, adding that it expects gold to make further headway towards $1,350/$1,377 target in coming weeks. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional share markets have mild upward lead from slightly higher closes for DJIA, Nasdaq; Japanese exporters likely to stay in demand with JPY weakening a little more vs USD, EUR after Tokyo authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets earlier this week though investors likely cautious before long weekend in Japan. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, traders still watching JPY closely for signs of continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . EUR/USD at 1.3078 vs 1.3085 late NY, EUR/JPY at 112.25 vs 112.29, USD/JPY at 85.86 vs 85.81. Not much on data slate: Singapore non-oil exports at 0500 GMT, Japan department store sales at 0530 GMT. Later there's German PPI, euro area BOP, EU construction output, U.S. real earnings, CPI, preliminary Thomson Reuters/University of Michigan survey of consumers, Fed governor Tarullo in panel discussion. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD upward momentum has stalled as RBA rate hike expectations have stepped back a bit, says John Kyriakopoulos, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at NAB. Says there's now a 19% chance of a 25 bps rate hike in October priced, down from 25% two days ago. Adds U.S. bond yields have stopped plunging. As such, the 0.9430/60 area will provide stiff resistance for AUD/USD near-term in the absence of further gains in global stock markets and commodity prices or a re-widening in the Australia-U.S. 2-year swap yield spread. Expects initial support is at 0.9330, then 0.9270. AUD/USD now 0.9358. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2250 GMT [Dow Jones] Downward drift in prices for Australian bonds likely to continue with focus on speech by RBA Governor Glenn Stevens on Monday. Andrew Bryan, debt strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , says RBA has been sending signals that it is the strength of Asia that is more important for policy makers than previous close economic ties with economies like the U.S. Says Stevens likely to reinforce that hawkish mood and bring some clarity to the rates outlook. Notes some in the market are talking about a hike in October, so the speech will be watched even more intently than usual. Support for December 3-year bonds likely at 95.14. Now 95.18, down 7 basis points. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices to fall again today after U.S. wheat prices sank for a third session overnight as disappointing weekly export sales fueled fears a rush to buy grain after a severe Russian drought is ending. Weak sales caused traders to reconsider because the U.S., the world's top wheat exporter, had recorded weekly export business of more than 1 million tons in previous five weekly reports. Importers have enough wheat to cover their needs roughly through November after getting spooked by Russia's drought, traders said. Soft red winter wheat for December delivery on CBOT fell 7.5 cents, or 1%, at US$7.1925/bushel. AUD remains strong, holding most recent gains, AUD/USD last 0.9362, near midpoint of yesterday's daytime trading range. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales fell A$4.50 to settle at A$276.00/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia fell the same amount to A$304.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold adds 30 cents early in Asia at $1,274.00/oz, after yesterday trading a range $1,265.05 up to a fresh record high of $1,277.85, extending a rally from intraday low of $1,158.30 on July 27. Gold futures on Comex in NY also posted a new record settlement overnight as USD weakened and on safe haven buying as equities eased. LME base metals mostly rose yesterday after drifting in their recent ranges for much of the session, with mixed U.S. data failing to provide any clear direction, although finding support in a stronger euro, which peaked at 1-month highs against USD. Market participants reported lower volumes ahead of next week's national holidays in China, which increased market volatility. LME 3-month copper rose $81 vs Wednesday PM kerb to $7,696/metric ton, aluminum edged $12 higher at $2,165, zinc firmed $2 at $2,147.00, nickel climbed $45 to $23,245 and tin extended its rally, jumping $345 to $23,495, a 52% gain from June 7. Lead bucked yesterday's trend, losing $33 to $2,202. Of other precious metals, silver early in Asia loses 3 cents at $20.77/oz, platinum down $2 at $1,605, palladium loses $1 at $548. Nymex October crude futures lose 78 cents overnight at $75.82/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2228 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Technology companies boosted stocks as confidence mounted economy would avoid double-dip recession; \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  rose 1.8%, Cisco Systems gained 1.6%, Intel +1.3%; "It seems like we priced in too much of a double dip, and it's not coming to pass as bad as people anticipated," says Patrick Becker Jr., principal at Becker Capital Management. Industrials weakened, weighed by 3.8% drop in \uc2 FedEx\uc2  after 1Q profit more than doubled on rise in revenue, but company forecast 2Q earnings below analysts' average estimate. Company also said will combine FedEx Freight, \uc2 FedEx National LTL\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at end of January, as it cuts 1,700 full-time employees, closes 100 facilities. Shipping peer \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  also fell 1.4%. \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  added 4.8% after car maker upgraded to "overweight" from "equal weight" by \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , which said North American market will sustain strong margins. Dow +0.2%, Nasdaq up 0.1%, Philly semicons +0.8%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2225 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says it's time to buy USD/JPY, EUR/JPY on Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "While we await balance sheet data from the (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to confirm the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that it has not been sterilized, we take the move at face value and shift our bias to buy in both." USD/JPY last 85.815, EUR/JPY last 112.215. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2225 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says it's time to buy USD/JPY, EUR/JPY on Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "While we await balance sheet data from the (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to confirm the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that it has not been sterilized, we take the move at face value and shift our bias to buy in both." USD/JPY last 85.815, EUR/JPY last 112.215. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2217 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD may come under pressure in Asia with profit-taking likely in a slightly over extended market, says Jonathan Cavenagh, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . Says focus has switched to next week's FOMC meeting against a somewhat improved picture for the U.S. economy. Says equity markets seem to be treading water until then, so leads for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like AUD will be limited. Says heightened speculation surrounding a rate hike in October is overdone, and some support will be withdrawn from AUD/USD as markets realise the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has more time on its hands. Expects solid support on dips down to 0.9300. Now 0.9360. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2213 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/AUD could tumble in coming sessions on back of RBNZ's bearish outlook, fair value in 0.7500-0.7700 range, support around 0.7155, says Bank of New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones. Pair last 0.7736. "We wouldn't be surprised to see NZD/AUD continue to slide in coming sessions." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2209 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD held sharp gains against JPY after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strangle recent strength in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's estimated Y2 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  left \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors cautious, with Japanese officials saying more action could be on table to stem JPY's strength, a stance that might have also given a boost to EUR, says Steven Englander, head of G10 strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in New York; "If the market becomes convinced that [Japan] will be resisting U.S. dollar weakness against the yen, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will become more attractive as U.S. dollar alternatives," he said. EUR/USD at 1.3085 late Thursday vs 1.3012 late Wednesday, USD/JPY at 85.81 vs 85.63, EUR/JPY at 112.29 vs 111.36, GBP/USD at 1.5635 vs 1.5623, USD/CHF at 1.0146 vs 1.0035, USD Index at 81.224 vs 81.453. Treasurys maturing in five or more years fell, hurt by selling related to new corporate and government agency debt supply; 2-year yields flat at 0.480%, 10-year yields +4.3 bps at 2.766%. Tech concerns boosted stocks as confidence mounted economy would avoid double-dip recession; \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  rose 1.8%, while Cisco Systems gained 1.6%, Intel added 1.3%; Dow +0.2%, Nasdaq up 0.1%, Philly semicons +0.8%. Gold futures posted new record settlement, as USD weakened, investors wanted more of precious metal as haven; December Comex gold rose $5.10 to $1,273.80/oz. Crude oil futures prices dropped as scheduled restart of a major pipeline rekindled oversupply concerns in world's biggest oil consumer; October Nymex crude settled down $1.45, at $74.57/bbl. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 1903ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g000gu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
